    {
      "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "RigorPilot",
        "description": "Reproduces a deep learning research repo from its README in small audited steps, with a separate lane for trying new ideas.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want an agent to run a deep learning research repo from its README and record honestly what worked"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "finds the smallest documented run, such as one evaluation command, sets it up, runs it and audits the result, instead of a full training run"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a repro_outputs/ folder: a verdict (verified, partial or blocked), the exact commands, logs, patches, any change to data, evaluation or metrics, and the README annotated section by section"
            },
            {
              "label": "Main skill",
              "text": "ai-research-reproduction runs a reproduction end to end and works alone; six of the others are steps it uses as needed, and each can be called by name"
            },
            {
              "label": "Exploration lane",
              "text": "ai-research-explore, explore-code and explore-run try new ideas on a saved baseline, only when you ask, and never report them as reproduced"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "safe-debug, which diagnoses a failed run and patches only with your approval"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python 3.11+ and Git for the main skill's scripts; five of the other skills load a runtime, writer or reference from it, so install it with them"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ai-research-reproduction is the entry point, and the README offers it as a self-contained install. It picks the smallest documented target (inference, then evaluation, then a training startup check; full training only after you confirm) and runs it through its script in three calls: plan, run, verify. Six skills are steps it uses as needed, and each can also be called by name. repo-intake-and-plan sorts the README's commands and recommends the first run. env-and-assets-bootstrap plans a conda environment and where checkpoints and datasets go. paper-context-resolver answers one gap the README leaves, such as a dataset split, from the paper, and notes any conflict. analyze-project maps a repo's entry points and configs, read-only, when its structure is unclear. minimal-run-and-audit records an inference or evaluation run in repro_outputs/, and run-train starts, checks or resumes training, recording it in train_outputs/. When a run fails, safe-debug diagnoses it and patches only with your approval. Exploration is a separate lane, only when you ask and from a saved baseline: ai-research-explore ranks ideas and hands code changes to explore-code, which works on an isolated branch, and short trials to explore-run. Their results go to explore_outputs/ as candidates, never as reproduced results.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/tree/fb3ccdf5aa64b1fa5b1d0463e666a8bc8bc26e01"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/ai-research-reproduction",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/repo-intake-and-plan",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/paper-context-resolver",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/env-and-assets-bootstrap",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/analyze-project",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/minimal-run-and-audit",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Its run and report scripts load a shared runtime that ships inside ai-research-reproduction, the repo's main skill, and stop with an error without it."
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/run-train",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Its run and report scripts load a shared runtime that ships inside ai-research-reproduction, the repo's main skill, and stop with an error without it."
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/safe-debug",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/ai-research-explore",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Loads its required research loop and shared runtime from ai-research-reproduction, the repo's main skill, and hands code changes and trial runs to the repo's explore-code and explore-run skills."
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/explore-code",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Its report script loads a shared writer that ships inside ai-research-reproduction, the repo's main skill, and stops with an error without it."
          },
          {
            "id": "lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/skills/explore-run",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Its report script loads a shared writer that ships inside ai-research-reproduction, the repo's main skill, and stops with an error without it."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills --skill '*'"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "browser-act/skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "BrowserAct",
        "description": "Real-browser automation for agents, plus Skill Forge, which turns a site you scrape often into a reusable skill.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want the agent to use real browsers on logged-in or bot-protected sites, or to turn a repeated scrape into a reusable skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "browser-act drives your Chrome or BrowserAct's stealth browsers from its CLI; browser-act-skill-forge explores a site once and writes a skill that repeats the extraction"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "pages read, clicked through and screenshotted on demand, and tested skill packages (SKILL.md plus Python scripts) for sites you scrape often"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the browser-act CLI (Python 3.12+, uv). Your own Chrome needs no account; stealth browsers, CAPTCHA solving and browser-act-skill-forge need a free BrowserAct account"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "before creating or deleting browsers, logging in, submitting forms or uploading files"
            }
          ],
          "text": "BrowserAct is a browser-automation service for AI agents, and these skills use its local CLI rather than its cloud product. browser-act lets the agent open pages in your own Chrome, reusing your logins, or in BrowserAct's stealth browsers, which each present their own device fingerprint so sites see separate, ordinary visitors rather than a bot. The agent reads a numbered list of page elements and clicks or types by number, takes screenshots, captures network responses, and can send you a live link to take over when it gets stuck. browser-act-skill-forge builds on browser-act, which it loads first. You describe the data you want from a site and confirm a plan; it works out how the site loads that data, preferring the site's own data APIs over scraping its HTML, then generates a skill of SKILL.md plus Python scripts, has a subagent test it, fixes what fails and installs it. Later runs call that skill without exploring again, which suits bulk jobs such as hundreds of listings. Beyond the free account, only managed proxies and more than five stealth browsers cost money.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/browser-act/skills/tree/11c057b03f92101642cadc9f840564574120d184"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "browser-act/skills/browser-act",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "browser-act/skills/browser-act-skill-forge",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Loads the browser-act skill in its first step and explores every site through it, so it needs that skill and its CLI installed."
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add https://github.com/browser-act/skills --skill browser-act --skill browser-act-skill-forge"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills@spec-workflow",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Warp spec workflow",
        "description": "Spec-first feature work: a product spec, a tech spec when warranted, then code built from them and checked against them, with the specs kept current in the same PR.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a feature is big, risky or ambiguous enough that an agent will build it better from written specs"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "decides whether specs are worth it, writes a product spec and, when warranted, a tech spec, builds the feature from them, and finally checks code and specs agree"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<ticket>/PRODUCT.md on what users see and, when warranted, TECH.md on how it's built, in the same PR as the code when practical, updated to match what ships"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Linear MCP server, for spec-driven-implementation, which files a Linear issue when there's none"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "Matt Pocock's to-spec, which publishes the spec to an issue tracker, the specs here are files checked into the repo and reviewed with the code"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Five skills from Warp's shared repo that take a substantial feature from intent to checked code. spec-driven-implementation is the entry point: it decides whether the feature needs specs at all (small bug fixes, simple refactors and narrow UI tweaks usually don't), makes sure a Linear issue exists to name the specs folder after, and hands its three main steps to the next three skills. write-product-spec writes PRODUCT.md: the feature's behavior from its user's side, as numbered, testable rules with no implementation details, after asking for a Figma mock when there's UI. write-tech-spec reads that spec and the code, then writes TECH.md: the current system, the proposed changes and trade-offs, and a test or check for each important rule. implement-specs builds from the approved specs, updating them as soon as the implementation changes, in the same PR when practical, so they describe what ships. validate-changes-match-specs, which you run yourself near the end or in review, lists each material mismatch between the branch's specs and its code, checks what security specs allow and prevent, and asks whether to fix the code or the spec. They're written for Warp's own features; all but the entry point and implement-specs also work alone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Loads write-product-spec, write-tech-spec and implement-specs for its three main steps; on its own it only decides whether specs are needed and files the Linear issue."
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-product-spec",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/implement-specs",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Starts only once PRODUCT.md, and TECH.md when warranted, exist and are approved: the specs that write-product-spec and write-tech-spec write."
          },
          {
            "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/validate-changes-match-specs",
            "standalone": true
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Warp, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills@latest add warpdotdev/common-skills --skill spec-driven-implementation --skill write-product-spec --skill write-tech-spec --skill implement-specs --skill validate-changes-match-specs --agent warp --global"
          },
          {
            "label": "Warp, with the repo's 24 other skills",
            "command": "npx skills@latest add warpdotdev/common-skills --skill '*' --agent warp --global"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Firecrawl Workflows",
        "description": "Firecrawl's outcome-focused skills that turn web search, scraping and browsing into research reports, audits, lead lists, knowledge bases and design systems.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want finished, sourced work from the live web, such as a research report, SEO audit, QA report, lead list or local copy of a docs site"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sends each request to one of its workflow skills, which gathers evidence with Firecrawl's search, scraping and browser and writes the deliverable"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one deliverable per request, such as a cited report, a ranked lead CSV, a DESIGN.md or a folder of Markdown docs, most ending with inputs to rerun it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Firecrawl CLI and a Firecrawl API key; lead data and SEO ranking figures come from Alexandria providers, which need a signed-in Firecrawl account"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike the firecrawl skill",
              "text": "that one gives the agent Firecrawl's raw commands; these skills package them into whole jobs with a set output"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also installs",
              "text": "three workflows not listed here: published business contacts, current job openings and fantasy-sports decisions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Firecrawl Workflows turns Firecrawl, a web search, scraping and browser service, into complete jobs. firecrawl-workflows is the entry point. It matches a request to one workflow, or runs a generic process that gathers evidence and writes it up when none fits. Most workflows follow one pattern: infer the inputs, ask at most three questions and only when blocked, collect evidence with Firecrawl, split independent parts across subagents where the agent has them, and end with sources and the inputs to rerun the job. For research there are web reports (deep-research), literature reviews over Firecrawl's paper index (research-papers), market figures and competitor tracking. Lead work runs from exporting directories (company-directories) to finding prospects through Alexandria, Firecrawl's catalog of data providers (lead-gen), to enriching people and companies you already have (lead-research). Other workflows audit a site's SEO, test a live site, walk through a product's flows, extract a site's design system and read dashboard metrics. knowledge-base saves web docs as local files, and knowledge-ingest does the same for portals that need a browser. shop compares products and adds one to a cart only when asked. Some skills hand off to others: deep-research passes literature to research-papers.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/tree/d2a986240f9d786ae54fc33fcfd9ad37308e9cc3"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-workflows",
            "standalone": false,
            "note": "Picks which of the other workflow skills to load for a request; alone, it only has its generic fallback process."
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-deep-research",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-research-papers",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-market-research",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-competitive-intel",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-company-directories",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-lead-gen",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-lead-research",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-seo-audit",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-qa",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-demo-walkthrough",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-website-design-clone",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-dashboard-reporting",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-knowledge-base",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-knowledge-ingest",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-shop",
            "standalone": true
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with the skills CLI",
            "command": "npx skills add firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows"
          },
          {
            "label": "With the Firecrawl CLI installed",
            "command": "firecrawl setup workflows"
          },
          {
            "label": "Any agent, with Firecrawl's full catalog, CLI skills included",
            "command": "npx skills add firecrawl/skills"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "firecrawl/cli@core",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Firecrawl CLI",
        "description": "Web work through the Firecrawl CLI: an overview skill that picks the command, and a detailed guide to each, from search and scrape to crawl, monitor and interact.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want the agent to do its web work through Firecrawl: searching, reading pages, crawling sites, acting on pages, or watching them for changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "firecrawl, the overview skill, picks the right Firecrawl command for the task; nine companion skills each give one command a detailed guide"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Markdown or JSON files in a .firecrawl/ folder, read in small pieces, and answers that cite them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the firecrawl-cli npm package and a Firecrawl account, whose plan sets your credits; search, scrape and interact also work without one, rate-limited"
            },
            {
              "label": "Installs with",
              "text": "firecrawl setup core, which also adds the repo's other skills, among them the ones for its developer-docs and research-paper indexes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "Firecrawl's build skills, for adding Firecrawl to an app's code, and its workflow skills, for deliverables such as research briefs or SEO audits; each has its own setup command"
            }
          ],
          "text": "firecrawl is the entry point. Its description covers any live-web task, ordinary research included, and it sets an order to escalate in: search first, scrape a URL you already have, map a large site to find the right page, crawl a whole section such as /docs, set up a monitor for repeated checks, and interact with pages that need clicks, forms or a login. Beside that path, agent sends Firecrawl's own AI agent through a complex site to extract structured data, download saves a site as folders of Markdown files, and parse converts local files such as PDFs. For each command, firecrawl sends the agent to a companion skill with its options, costs and rules, such as search's feedback, which refunds a credit, or how to word a monitor's goal. Each companion also works alone and points to its neighbours, such as scrape to interact when a page needs clicks. Search also finds, and scrape runs, tools from Alexandria, Firecrawl's catalog of data providers and site workflows. The same files are copied unchanged into Firecrawl's firecrawl/skills catalog, which skills.sh lists separately.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/firecrawl/cli/tree/da25d5cd06db21cb33b100661cea0d9b89d457a3"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-search",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-scrape",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-map",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-crawl",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-monitor",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-interact",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-agent",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-download",
            "standalone": true
          },
          {
            "id": "firecrawl/cli/skills/firecrawl-parse",
            "standalone": true
          }
        ],
        "install": [
          {
            "label": "Detected coding agents, once the Firecrawl CLI is installed",
            "command": "firecrawl setup core"
          },
          {
            "label": "One step: installs the CLI, logs in and adds Firecrawl's skills to detected agents",
            "command": "npx -y firecrawl-cli@latest init -y --browser"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills",
      "skew": 0,
      "agentReview": {
        "name": "Addy's Agent Skills",
        "description": "Twenty-five engineering skills for every phase of building software: define, plan, build, verify, review and ship.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want your agent to follow a senior engineer's process at every phase, from a vague idea to a production launch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives each phase its own skills: interview, spec, plan, build in tested slices, debug, review, simplify, secure, commit, document and ship"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a spec you approve, a plan in tasks/plan.md and tasks/todo.md, small tested commits, a five-axis code review and a launch checklist with a rollback plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself: each skill loads when the work matches it, and using-agent-skills maps a task to its skill when the choice isn't obvious"
            },
            {
              "label": "Commands",
              "text": "in Claude Code, slash commands such as /spec, /plan, /build, /test, /review and /ship start the matching skills; /build auto works through a whole plan after one approval"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, Antigravity, Command Code and any agent the skills CLI supports"
            },
            {
              "label": "Install whole",
              "text": "a single-skill install copies only that skill's folder, so its links to the repo's shared checklists break, though the skill still works"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Addy Osmani's pack maps software work onto six phases. using-agent-skills, the entry point, picks the skill for a task and sets shared rules: state assumptions, push back, stay in scope, verify before claiming done. Define: interview-me finds out what you actually want, idea-refine turns a rough idea into a one-pager, spec-driven-development writes the spec and waits for your approval, and constraint-driven-development records the quality bar in CONSTRAINTS.md. Plan: planning-and-task-breakdown turns the spec into ordered tasks. Build: context-engineering loads the right context and source-driven-development checks framework code against official docs; incremental-implementation and test-driven-development then deliver each task as small tested commits, helped by frontend-ui-engineering and api-and-interface-design, with observability-and-instrumentation adding telemetry alongside and doubt-driven-development sending non-trivial decisions to an adversarial reviewer. Verify: browser-testing-with-devtools and debugging-and-error-recovery. Review: code-review-and-quality, code-simplification, security-and-hardening and performance-optimization. Ship: git-workflow-and-versioning, ci-cd-and-automation, documentation-and-adrs, deprecation-and-migration and shipping-and-launch. Every skill but the router has a table of excuses to reject and a verification checklist, and works alone. The repo also ships reviewer personas and shared checklists that only a whole-repo install brings.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde"
        },
        "skills": [
          {
            "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/using-agent-skills",
