    {
      "id": "mattpocock/skills/skills/in-progress/setup-ts-deep-modules",
      "name": "setup-ts-deep-modules",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/mattpocock/skills/setup-ts-deep-modules",
        "installs": 407928,
        "weeklyInstalls": [27915, 25969, 23066, 22668, 28434, 25170, 22748, 20889],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Enforces deep-module boundaries with dependency-cruiser.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want each package in a TypeScript repo used only through its entry files, enforced by a check"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "adds dependency-cruiser rules: outside code may import only a package's top-level files, never its subfolders, and imports may not form cycles"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a lint:boundaries check run with your type check, an example package, a README, and a pointer from AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proves",
              "text": "the rules work, by adding a forbidden import and watching the check fail"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you invoke it; it sits in its author's in-progress folder, so expect changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A deep module is a lot of behavior behind a small interface, and this skill makes that a rule a tool can enforce. Each folder under src/packages (or packages) is a package: its top-level files, such as index.ts or client.ts, are its public entry points, and everything in a subfolder, by convention lib/ for code and tests/ for tests, is private. dependency-cruiser, a tool that checks imports, then fails when outside code reaches into a subfolder or when imports form a cycle. Tests, even a package's own, must also go through its entry files, like any outside caller. Files inside a package still import each other freely, and several small entry files are preferred to one index that re-exports everything. The agent installs it with your package manager, merges into an existing config rather than overwriting it, adds the check to the command that already runs your type check, and leaves tsconfig alone. It's done only once the check passes, fails on a deliberate bad import, then passes again. It doesn't decide which packages may depend on which; that's left for you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/5beb35b040e3de97e54fa9e386d7e42d15c295c2/skills/in-progress/setup-ts-deep-modules"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:setup",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:typescript",
          "stack:dependency-cruiser",
          "architecture"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "obra/superpowers/skills/test-driven-development",
      "name": "test-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/obra/superpowers/test-driven-development",
        "installs": 271829,
        "weeklyInstalls": [6275, 6629, 6383, 6060, 5605, 7253, 6777, 6482],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3",
        "updated": "2026-10-20",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes the failing test first, then the code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent implements any feature, bug fix, refactor or behavior change"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes it write one failing test, watch it fail, write the least code that passes, then clean up"
            },
            {
              "label": "Forbids",
              "text": "code without a failing test first: code written before its test is deleted, not kept as a reference"
            },
            {
              "label": "Exceptions",
              "text": "throwaway prototypes, generated code and config files, each only with your permission"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its one rule is \"no production code without a failing test first\". Each cycle starts with one small test of real behavior, with a clear name and no mocks unless they can't be avoided. The agent runs it and checks that it fails for the right reason: the feature is missing, not a typo. A test that passes straight away is testing existing behavior and gets fixed. Then it writes the simplest code that passes, with no extra options, and runs the project's whole test suite, not just the new test, reporting any failure it sees, even one it didn't cause. Only when everything passes does it refactor. A bug gets a test that reproduces it before any fix. The skill answers the usual excuses (too simple to test, I'll test after, I already tried it by hand, deleting hours of work is wasteful) and lists the signs that mean: delete the code and start over. A companion guide covers writing tests that fail when the code breaks, and when mocking is safe.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/obra/superpowers/tree/bb92a77741419a4ab5f06e711a283343f1ada0c3/skills/test-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "tdd",
          "testing",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
