        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a codebase's animations and writes prioritized, self-contained fix plans that another agent or a cheaper model can carry out.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a roadmap of animation fixes across a whole app, not a review of one change"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "maps where motion lives, audits it in eight areas such as easing, interruptibility, performance and accessibility, then ranks confirmed findings by impact for the effort"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a findings table, then one Markdown plan per finding you pick, in a plans/ folder, with exact values, steps and a feel check"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike review-animations",
              "text": "that one judges a single change; this one surveys the codebase and never edits source code itself"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents for anything beyond a small repo: the audit is split across read-only subagents, one per area"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The audit-then-plan skill of Emil Kowalski's animation set: a capable model does the judging, and any agent, even a cheaper one, makes the edits. It first maps the motion libraries, existing easing and duration tokens, the product's personality and how often each animated element is seen, since something opened a hundred times a day is judged harder than an onboarding screen. It audits eight areas: purpose and frequency, easing and duration, physicality and origin, interruptibility, performance, accessibility, consistency of tokens, and missed opportunities. It re-checks every finding at its file and line, drops anything deliberate, and shows one table ranked by impact for the effort. Then it waits for you to choose. Each chosen finding becomes a numbered plan in plans/, stamped with the current commit and written for an executor with no context: current code quoted, exact curves and durations, ordered steps, boundaries and a slow-motion feel check, plus a plans/README.md giving the order to run them. Other modes: quick or deep, one area only, a single plan from your description, execute (a subagent applies a plan in a separate worktree, then its diff is reviewed), and reconcile (marks finished plans done).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/improve-animations"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
