        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds an animation from scratch, deciding in order whether it should animate, why, and with which tool, curve and duration.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to add motion to a web interface, such as a dropdown, modal, toast or drag, and want it right the first time"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs a fixed sequence: whether to animate, its purpose, the cheapest tool, properties, easing or spring, interruption and exit, then reduced-motion handling"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the animation code, plus a few lines on the decisions and what to check by eye"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike review-animations",
              "text": "that one critiques motion you already wrote; this one writes it so it passes that review"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "the web: CSS, the Web Animations API or the Motion library; animate-expo covers React Native"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The building skill of Emil Kowalski's animation set. Its first two steps can end the job with no code: anything seen a hundred times a day, like a keyboard shortcut or command palette, gets no animation, and motion without a named purpose isn't built. Then it picks the cheapest tool that works, from a CSS transition up to the Motion library, and sends components such as toasts or dropdowns to pick-ui-library instead of hand-rolling them. It animates only transform and opacity, never scales up from zero, grows popovers out of their trigger, and takes curves and durations from fixed tables: a strong ease-out for entrances, never ease-in, under 300ms for UI. Springs are for drags and gestures; transitions, not keyframes, for anything triggered rapidly. Reduced-motion handling, and hover effects limited to mouse users, ship with every animation. A recipes file covers common cases, such as button press, tooltip, drawer, toast, accordion, stagger, hold-to-confirm and drag-to-dismiss. It finishes with a self-check against the mistakes review-animations blocks.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/animate"
        },
        "tags": [
