      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Diagnoses deep-learning training and inference failures and proposes the smallest fix, patching only with your approval.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a deep-learning run fails with a traceback, CUDA out-of-memory error, checkpoint load failure, shape mismatch or NaN loss"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads the error, narrows down the likely cause with evidence, and proposes the smallest safe fix without editing code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a debug_outputs/ folder with a diagnosis, a patch plan and a machine-readable status"
            },
            {
              "label": "Asks first",
              "text": "before any patch, and recommends a savepoint or branch before medium- or high-risk changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike general debugging",
              "text": "it says when a fix would change what the experiment means or how its results compare"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The debugging step of RigorPilot, a set of skills that reproduces deep-learning research code from its README. It diagnoses first and, by default, leaves the repository's code untouched, writing only its report files. It names a likely cause category, and a script that ships with the skill can sort an error into one, such as out of GPU memory, checkpoint mismatch, distributed-training failure, tensors on different devices, shape mismatch, NaN loss or a missing file, with cautious actions for each, such as lowering batch size or resolution through configuration before touching the model. The diagnosis states the likely cause, the evidence and the smallest fix, and a patch happens only after you approve it. A debug fix isn't treated as a research contribution: if it changes what the experiment measures or how comparable its results are, the skill says so. It stays out of broad refactors and doesn't slip into trying new ideas.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/lllllllama/rigorpilot-skills/tree/fb3ccdf5aa64b1fa5b1d0463e666a8bc8bc26e01/skills/safe-debug"
        },
        "tags": [
