      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes pull request bodies with a visual summary, before-and-after evidence and a merge-risk call.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent writes a pull request description and you want it short and visual, with no preamble"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "fills a three-part template: a summary drawn as the smallest visual that shows the change, before-and-after evidence, and the merge danger"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a PR body with a diagram, diff sketch or tree, proof such as screenshots or test runs, and how hard the change is to undo"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "by itself whenever the agent writes a PR body"
            },
            {
              "label": "Adapted from",
              "text": "Dex Horthy's show-me skill from Humanlayer, which its frontmatter credits"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Matt Pocock's shape for pull request bodies. The summary picks the smallest view that makes the change clear: pseudocode for logic, a call tree for control flow, a component tree for UI, a shallow file tree for a refactor, a Mermaid diagram for how parts interact, a diff of one of these when the surrounding shape already exists, or the whole block when most of it is new. Each visual sits next to the short text it supports, keeping only what the point needs. Evidence shows a before and an after: screenshots rank best when the change is visual and the environment can take them, then test results or console output, such as the exact test failing before and passing after, in pseudocode. Merge danger says whether the change is a two-way door, cheap to roll back, or a one-way door, such as a destructive or hard-to-reverse change, and names its blast radius, what it could affect, from layout shift to breakage for consumers. Prose stays brief, with no preamble, and uses the project's terms from GLOSSARY.md, the glossary the repo's domain-modeling skill keeps. It covers the body only.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/4ae2317ddb22b046e26455e4f9c9b3aaaf62212d/skills/engineering/pr"
        },
        "tags": [
