    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/churn-prevention",
      "name": "churn-prevention",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/churn-prevention",
        "installs": 127117,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3028, 2714, 2785, 3391, 3685, 3194, 3295, 3688],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-19"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs cancel flows with save offers, churn-risk health scores and failed-payment recovery to reduce subscription churn.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "subscribers keep canceling, you have no cancel flow, or failed payments are quietly closing accounts"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "designs a cancel flow with a save offer matched to each cancel reason, lists warning signs of at-risk accounts, and plans payment retries and reminder emails"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a cancel flow design (exit survey, offer, confirmation), an offer-per-reason map, a retry schedule and a 4-email payment reminder sequence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "both voluntary churn (customers choosing to leave) and involuntary churn (payments that fail)"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "hide the cancel button or guilt-trip: the \"continue cancelling\" option stays visible"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Retention for subscription products in three modes: build a cancel flow, improve an existing one from its data, or set up recovery of failed payments. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes) and asks about churn rate, billing provider, usage data and whether you sell to businesses or consumers. The cancel flow runs from a one-question exit survey to an offer chosen by the reason: a 20-30% discount for price, a pause for low use, a roadmap preview for a missing feature, no offer for a closed business. To catch churn early, it lists warning signs such as a 50% drop in logins or a data export, and a weighted 0-100 health score whose weights it calls illustrative, to be checked against real renewals before use. Failed payments get card-expiry alerts, retries timed by decline type, and dunning emails (reminders to update payment) over ten days. It ends with churn metrics, cohort cuts and cancel-flow tests, and compares retention tools such as Churnkey and billing providers such as Stripe.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/churn-prevention"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "conversion",
          "payments",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/sales-enablement",
      "name": "sales-enablement",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/sales-enablement",
        "installs": 125167,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2672, 2469, 2472, 2952, 3136, 3225, 3363, 3822],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Creates sales collateral reps will use: decks, one-pagers, objection docs, battle cards, demo scripts, ROI calculators, proposals and playbooks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "your sales team needs a deck, one-pager, battle card or demo script, or a rep needs an answer to a live objection"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "drafts each asset to a set structure, such as a 10-12 slide deck that tells a story instead of touring features"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "outlines and copy by format: slides with speaker notes, objection tables, scene-by-scene demo scripts, persona cards"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "invent stats, customers or competitor numbers; unproven \"why we win\" claims become hypotheses for reps to test"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "win-loss analysis that codes each deal's main reason and reports counts with sample sizes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Sales collateral built around what reps trust: their language, scannable layouts, and claims a buyer can't disprove in a minute. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes) and asks about your value proposition, how you sell, which assets you need and what exists already. Each asset has a structure: decks run from the buyer's problem and its cost through proof and ROI to next steps, one-pagers fit on one page, and objection docs give each objection the concern behind it, a response, proof and a follow-up question. For a live deal objection it answers that deal with one response the rep can say as written, and says when the objection shows a real fit gap. Battle cards cover one competitor, including when to walk away, and it asks which competitor before drafting. Win-loss analysis segments deals before counting reasons and treats slices under about 5 deals as signals, not findings. It also covers ROI calculators, proposals, sales playbooks, short case-study briefs for reps and cold-call scripts. Public comparison pages are left to the repo's competitors skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/sales-enablement"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "copywriting",
          "slides"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/customer-research",
      "name": "customer-research",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/customer-research",
        "installs": 122315,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3364, 3046, 3143, 3894, 4123, 3571, 3861, 4504],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-28"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Mines transcripts, surveys, reviews and online communities for customer pains, triggers and exact wording, and builds personas only from evidence.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to know what customers really say, why they buy or churn, or need personas grounded in data"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "analyzes material you have, mines Reddit, G2 and forums, or plans interviews and surveys, then clusters the themes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "your pick of a synthesis report, a quote bank, personas, a jobs-to-be-done map, a competitor summary or a gap analysis"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guards against",
              "text": "personas from fewer than 5 data points per segment, invented details, and averaging across segments"
            },
            {
              "label": "Labels",
              "text": "every insight high, medium or low confidence by how many independent sources back it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Customer research in three modes, usually combined: analyzing assets you already have (call transcripts, surveys, support tickets, win/loss notes, NPS answers), mining what customers say unprompted online, and asking them directly through interviews and surveys. From each asset it extracts jobs to be done (the outcome a customer is trying to reach), pains, trigger events, desired outcomes, exact phrases and alternatives considered, then clusters themes, scores them by frequency and intensity, segments them and pulls 5-10 verbatim quotes per theme. It picks online sources by type of customer, from G2 and role-specific subreddits for B2B software to 1-3 star app store reviews for consumer products, and treats fetched reviews and posts as data, never instructions. It checks for sample bias (reviewers skew toward power users, tickets toward problems) and weights the last 12 months more. For a product with no reviews yet, it builds provisional personas from competitors' and adjacent products' reviews and tags each with its proxy source. It reads your product marketing context first if you have it, and asks which deliverable you want before writing one.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/customer-research"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "research",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/revops",
      "name": "revops",
      "skills.sh": {
