        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs the systems linking marketing to sales: lifecycle stages, lead scoring, routing, pipeline stages, CRM automations and deal desk rules.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "leads aren't reaching sales, nobody agrees what an MQL is, or you're setting up a CRM pipeline"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "defines stages with entry and exit criteria, builds fit and engagement scores, and writes routing rules with fallbacks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "standalone documents: lifecycle stages with SLAs, a scoring spec, routing rules, pipeline configuration and a metrics dashboard spec"
            },
            {
              "label": "Terms",
              "text": "an MQL is a lead marketing judges ready for sales, needing both fit and engagement; an SQL is one sales has accepted"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule",
              "text": "define stages, scores and routing on paper before automating anything"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Revenue operations: the stages, rules and handoffs between marketing, sales and customer success. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes) and asks about your sales motion, contract size, sales cycle, CRM stack and what's breaking. Leads move through stages from subscriber to evangelist, each with an owner, and an MQL handoff has response times: contact within 4 business hours, qualify or reject within 48. Scoring combines who the lead is, what they do and disqualifying signals, and a new model is tested on a later holdout of won and lost deals before it routes real leads. Routing goes to the most specific match first, with a fallback owner, and prioritizes speed to lead. It also covers pipeline stage hygiene, CRM and scheduling automations, discount approval tiers for the deal desk, deduplication and quarterly data audits, and benchmark metrics such as MQL-to-SQL rate and LTV:CAC. Guidance gets platform-specific when it knows your CRM, such as HubSpot or Salesforce.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/revops"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "conversion"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/lead-magnets",
      "name": "lead-magnets",
      "skills.sh": {
