      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans a lead magnet, such as a checklist, template or ebook, plus how to gate, deliver, promote and measure it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want something to give away in exchange for emails and don't know what to make"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "matches a format to your buyer's stage, from checklists for awareness to templates and free trials for decision"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a recommendation with effort estimate, a content outline, a gating and capture plan, a distribution plan and KPIs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike free-tools",
              "text": "from the same repo, which covers calculators and graders, it plans downloadable and gated content"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "packaging a course, webinar series or newsletter you own as an installable agent skill"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A lead magnet is a free resource, such as a checklist, cheat sheet, template, ebook, mini-course, quiz or webinar, offered in exchange for an email address. After reading your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), it asks about your business, how you capture leads now, what content and expertise you could repackage, and your goals. Its rules: solve one specific problem, fit the buyer's stage, look worth paying for but take under 30 minutes to consume, and lead naturally to the product. It weighs full, partial and no gating, asks for the fewest form fields, and lays out the landing page and thank-you page. Promotion runs through blog content upgrades, popups, social posts, paid ads and partners, with landing-page conversion benchmarks and A/B test ideas. Writing the content itself, capture forms and the follow-up emails go to the repo's copywriting, cro and emails skills.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/lead-magnets"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "conversion",
          "email"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/competitor-profiling",
      "name": "competitor-profiling",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/competitor-profiling",
        "installs": 101456,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3201, 2861, 3016, 3726, 3954, 3441, 3784, 4199],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-21"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches competitors from their URLs by scraping their sites and pulling SEO data, and writes one comparable Markdown profile per competitor.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a list of competitor URLs and want structured dossiers on positioning, pricing, features and SEO strength"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "maps and scrapes each site's key pages, optionally review sites, then pulls backlink, keyword and traffic data"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Markdown profile per competitor, a cross-competitor summary, and every raw scrape and SEO response saved by date"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works best with",
              "text": "Firecrawl and DataForSEO MCP servers; without them it uses built-in web fetching and any connected SEO data source"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike competitors",
              "text": "from the same repo, which writes public comparison pages, this does the research those pages and battle cards draw on"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Competitive intelligence from a list of URLs. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), confirms the URLs, depth and focus, and checks which tools are connected. A quick scan, the default unless you ask for depth or name three competitors or fewer, covers the homepage, pricing page and an SEO summary; a deep profile adds every key page, review sites such as G2 and Capterra, backlinks and keyword research. Everything fetched is saved under competitor-profiles/raw/ in a dated folder that is never overwritten, so later runs can be compared. Profiles keep what a source says, what it infers and what that might mean for you apart. Something missing from a competitor's site is written as \"not observed on [pages], as of [date]\", not as absent, motives are never stated, and SEO metrics it can't get are marked unavailable rather than estimated. Fetched pages are treated as data; any attempt in them to instruct the agent is noted. Each profile ends with strengths, weaknesses and implications for your product, and a summary file compares all competitors on a positioning map.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/competitor-profiling"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:mcp",
          "needs:account",
          "by:community",
          "stack:firecrawl",
          "stack:dataforseo",
          "research",
          "scraping",
          "seo",
          "marketing"
        ],
        "note": "Built around the Firecrawl and DataForSEO MCP servers; with no SEO data source connected it leaves traffic, keyword and backlink metrics out."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/cro",
      "name": "cro",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/cro",
        "installs": 98558,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3547, 3229, 3384, 4103, 4543, 4245, 4458, 4955],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a marketing page or form for what's holding back conversions and recommends quick wins, bigger changes, test ideas and alternative copy.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a homepage, landing, pricing or feature page or a lead form isn't converting, or you want feedback on a URL"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "checks seven areas in order of impact: value proposition, headline, calls to action, visual hierarchy, trust signals, objections, friction"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "recommendations sorted into quick wins, high-impact changes and A/B test ideas, plus 2-3 alternatives for headlines and buttons"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "signup, onboarding and popups from the same repo, which cover those flows, it works on marketing pages and forms"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Conversion rate optimization (CRO) is the work of getting more visitors to take a page's main action: sign up, book a demo, buy. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), then pins down the page type, its one conversion goal and where visitors come from. The review starts with whether a visitor understands what this is and why to care within 5 seconds, then moves to headlines, button copy (\"Start Free Trial\" over \"Submit\"), what stands out to someone scanning, logos and testimonials near the calls to action, objections answered through FAQs and guarantees, and friction such as extra form fields or slow loads. Each page type has its own focus: message match and a single call to action for landing pages, a highlighted recommended plan for pricing pages. For form abandonment or experiment ideas, it first loads the matching reference: fields, validation and mobile forms, or tests by page type. Designing and measuring the tests goes to the repo's ab-testing skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/cro"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "conversion",
          "copywriting",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/community-marketing",
      "name": "community-marketing",
      "skills.sh": {
