        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans and checks analytics tracking: event plans and naming, GA4 and Tag Manager setup, UTM conventions and validation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're setting up tracking, your events aren't firing, or you're asking how to tell whether something works"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "starts from the decisions the data should inform, then names events object_action, picks properties and gives a checklist and tools to confirm they fire"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a tracking plan document with events, properties, triggers, custom dimensions and conversions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Insists on",
              "text": "computing every figure from row-level data, never by eye, and dividing rates over matching closed time windows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "conversion-tracking and attribution from the same repo, which cover ad-platform conversions and attribution models"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Analytics implementation for marketing and product decisions, built on tracking only what informs a decision. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes) and asks what decisions the data will drive, what's tracked now, and what your stack and privacy requirements are. Events follow an object_action format such as signup_completed, keeping GA4's own names where its recommended events apply. It lists essential events for marketing sites and apps, standard properties, and setup steps for GA4 and Google Tag Manager. UTM links stay lowercase and hyphenated with one spelling per source and no personal data, with fixed patterns per partner type (newsletters, creators, podcasts, affiliates, communities) and a GA4 channel group so partner traffic doesn't land in Unassigned. Visits from AI assistants get their own channel, treated as a floor since many arrive as direct traffic. For analysis it computes figures with a script or spreadsheet, cross-checks headline numbers, counts cohorts still inside their window as pending rather than failed, and ties each recommendation to the brief's stated goal.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/analytics"
        },
