        "installs": 89879,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3235, 2920, 3010, 3752, 4140, 3693, 3914, 4409],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans and optimizes paid campaigns on Google, Meta, LinkedIn, X and TikTok: structure, targeting, budgets, retargeting and weekly account checks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're starting paid ads, a campaign's cost per acquisition is climbing, or you want to know if it's working"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "loads the playbook for the platform or decision at hand, such as Meta kill-or-scale calls, Google Search structure or B2B budgets"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "recommendations ending in facts, hypotheses and missing data, with unavailable inputs labelled MISSING rather than estimated"
            },
            {
              "label": "Weekly check",
              "text": "spend, traffic, conversions, creative, landing page, tracking, ending in one call: hold, fix, test or investigate"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ad-creative",
              "text": "from the same repo, which writes the ads themselves, this handles campaign strategy and account decisions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Paid acquisition across ad platforms, with the detail in references it loads by intent: payback math, B2B playbooks, Meta and LinkedIn operations, Google Search, account-based campaigns, account audits, and Google responsive search ads, which must follow a strict output spec. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), including its paid-ads targets and learnings, so tested angles don't come back as new ideas. Its central claim for 2026 is that audience knowledge goes into the creative first and targeting filters second, by a ratio that varies by platform: about 80/20 on Meta, but 40/60 on Google Search and LinkedIn, where keywords and job-title filters still lead. Retargeting shows people who didn't buy a different offer, through four ad types run together. Brand and non-brand search are measured separately, since cheap brand conversions flatter blended numbers. Guardrails for live accounts: unknown isn't failing, search terms are only a sample, no invented negative keywords, no adding conversions across attribution windows, and changes are proposed with a rollback and applied only with your approval.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/ads"
        },
