        "installs": 85396,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2897, 2716, 2787, 3156, 3363, 3636, 3894, 4224],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes JSON-LD structured data for your pages, first checking which Google rich results still exist, with a validation checklist.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want star ratings or other rich results in Google, or need to add or fix structured data"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "matches the page to schema.org types such as Organization, Article, Product or BreadcrumbList, written as JSON-LD"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a complete JSON-LD code block and a testing checklist for the Rich Results Test and Schema.org Validator"
            },
            {
              "label": "Knows that",
              "text": "Google dropped FAQ rich results in May 2026, HowTo in 2023 and the sitelinks search box in 2024"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike seo-audit",
              "text": "from the same repo, which covers SEO broadly, this only writes and checks structured data"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Schema markup is structured data, written in the schema.org vocabulary, that describes what a page contains, such as a product's price or an article's author, so search engines can show enhanced results. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), then asks about the page type, existing markup and the rich results you want. Its rules: mark up only what's visible on the page, use JSON-LD, follow Google's guidelines, and test before deploying. A table lists the common types with their required properties, and several types can share one page through @graph. Before writing markup for a search appearance, it checks Google's current list of supported features: a type can be valid schema.org and still earn no rich result, and it won't recommend markup to chase a retired one. Implementation notes cover static sites, React and Next.js, and WordPress plugins. Optimizing for AI search goes to the repo's ai-seo skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/schema"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "seo",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/competitors",
      "name": "competitors",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/competitors",
        "installs": 84898,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3087, 2744, 2873, 3499, 3630, 3083, 3301, 3738],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes competitor comparison and alternative pages in four formats, keeps claims about rivals dated and sourced, and audits existing pages for stale claims.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want pages that rank for \"[competitor] alternative\" or \"X vs Y\", or need to check old comparison content"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks one of four formats (alternative, alternatives list, you vs them, two competitors) and writes the page section by section"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a YAML data file per competitor, full page copy with meta tags and tables, and a prioritized page plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Evidence rules",
              "text": "\"not listed on their pricing page (as of date)\" instead of \"doesn't have\", dated prices, and no guessed motives"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike competitor-profiling",
              "text": "from the same repo, which researches competitors from scratch, this turns what you know into public pages"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Pages that position your product against competitors for people comparing options. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), and if no competitor is named it asks and stops rather than guessing. Each format has its own search intent, URL pattern and section order: a single alternative page for people ready to switch, a list of 4-7 real alternatives for earlier research, a you-versus-them page, and a page comparing two competitors that introduces you as the third option. Pages admit competitor strengths, say who each product is best for, and explain why differences matter beyond a feature checklist. Competitor facts live in one central file per competitor, so a fix reaches every page. The asset audit lists every claim about a competitor across pages, battle cards and decks, re-checks it, and marks it current, changed, unverifiable or overclaimed, then reports a fix list with replacement wording, public pages first. It notes that alternatives lists can earn citations in AI answers without AI recommending you.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/competitors"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "seo",
          "copywriting",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/launch",
      "name": "launch",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/launch",
        "installs": 84778,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2879, 2744, 2844, 3410, 3814, 3321, 3544, 3983],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans product and feature launches, from a readiness check through five rollout phases, Product Hunt and post-launch follow-up.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're about to release a product, feature or update and want a launch plan, or need to size how much noise an update deserves"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "checks the product is ready to launch, then plans five phases from internal testing to general availability across owned, rented and borrowed channels"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a phased launch plan with checklists for before, on and after launch day, plus Product Hunt preparation"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike marketing-ideas",
              "text": "from the same repo, which covers ongoing marketing after launch, it plans the release itself"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Launches are treated as a phased process. A readiness gate comes first: is what you're launching simple (it does one thing), lovable (people would choose it) and complete (a whole experience for that one thing)? It warns against both polishing in private forever and pushing the date for one more feature. Channels are sorted into owned (email list, blog, community), rented (social platforms, app stores) and borrowed (guest posts, podcasts, influencers), with everything meant to lead back to owned ones. The five phases run internal, alpha, beta, early access and full launch, each with actions and a goal. Product Hunt gets its own before, during and after plan with two case studies. After launch it covers onboarding emails, comparison pages and interactive demos, and for later releases a matrix sizes the marketing to the update: a full campaign for major features, a targeted note for integrations, a changelog entry for fixes. References cover QA for a new site and drafting changelogs from merged pull requests. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/launch"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/emails",
      "name": "emails",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/emails",
        "installs": 84774,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2851, 2761, 2862, 3470, 3848, 3351, 3521, 3903],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs automated email sequences such as welcome, nurture, onboarding and re-engagement flows, with timing, subject lines and full copy for each email.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a welcome, lead nurture, onboarding, re-engagement or other automated email flow, or want to find gaps in your lifecycle emails"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks what triggers the sequence, who's in it and its goal, then sets the length, spacing and one job for each email"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a sequence overview with trigger, goal and exit conditions, then each email's send time, subject, preview text, body and button, plus a metrics plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bans",
              "text": "AI tells such as \"it's not X, it's Y\", \"no X, no Y\" lists, fake \"Re:\" subject lines and \"I hope this email finds you well\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike cold-email",
              "text": "from the same repo, which handles cold outreach, it designs flows triggered by signups, purchases, events or inactivity"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Email sequence design for lifecycle marketing. It first reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes), including its Learnings section, so angles and offers already tested aren't proposed again as new. Four principles run through every sequence: one email, one job; value before the ask; fewer, more relevant emails; and an obvious next step. It gives default lengths and spacing, such as 5 to 7 welcome emails over about two weeks, or 3 to 4 re-engagement emails after 30 to 60 days of inactivity, with an email-by-email outline for welcome, lead nurture, re-engagement and onboarding sequences. Copy rules cover structure (hook, context, value, call to action, sign-off), short mobile-friendly paragraphs, word counts by email type, and subject lines of 40 to 60 characters. It lists lifecycle emails by category, from failed-payment recovery to win-back, and loads references for detailed templates, the lifecycle audit, and personalization and testing. For sending, it points to guides for tools such as Customer.io, Mailchimp, Resend and Kit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/emails"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "email",
          "copywriting",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/ab-testing",
      "name": "ab-testing",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/ab-testing",
        "installs": 83774,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3097, 2789, 2817, 3373, 3588, 3305, 3405, 3997],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs A/B tests with a hypothesis, metrics and sample size, interprets results without common statistical mistakes, and helps run an ongoing experiment program.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to test a change, need to know how long a test should run, or want experiments to become a regular practice"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes a hypothesis, picks primary, secondary and guardrail metrics, sizes the sample, and checks results against the plan made before the test"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a documented test plan, an analysis checklist, and a playbook entry recording each finished experiment"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guards against",
              "text": "stopping early when results look good, testing several changes at once, and misreading p-values and confidence intervals"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike cro",
              "text": "from the same repo, which finds what to improve on a page, this designs the test and judges its result"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Experiment design with statistical care. Each test starts from a hypothesis in a fixed form: because of this observation, we believe this change will cause this outcome for this audience, and we'll know when these metrics move. A quick table gives the sample size per variant for common baselines and lifts at 95% confidence and 80% power, with links to calculators. It covers test types (A/B, several variants, multivariate, split URL), traffic splits, client-side versus server-side setup, and a pre-launch checklist. For changes only some visitors interact with, such as a hero video, it explains comparing only the visitors who reached it. Its analysis follows the American Statistical Association's statement on p-values: what a p-value and a confidence interval do and don't mean, and why a finished test isn't extended until it turns significant. For a program, it adds a loop: gather hypotheses from analytics, research and support tickets, score them on impact, confidence and ease, run them, and record each result in a playbook of reusable patterns, reviewed weekly, monthly and quarterly. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/ab-testing"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "conversion",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/aso",
      "name": "aso",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/aso",
        "installs": 81725,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3042, 2709, 2782, 3315, 3544, 3082, 3249, 3858],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits an App Store or Google Play listing, scores it out of 100, and returns a prioritized plan of specific fixes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you share an App Store or Google Play link and want to know why the app isn't found or downloaded"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "fetches the live listing, screenshots the page, and scores six areas from title to ratings, adjusted for how well-known the app is"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a report with a score card, three quick wins, keyword suggestions, visual advice and an action plan, with character counts for text changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "compares your listing with 2 or 3 competitors and finds keywords they target that you don't"
            },
            {
              "label": "Limits",
              "text": "says what it can't judge without paid tools, such as search volume and exact rankings"
            }
          ],
          "text": "ASO, app store optimization, is the store version of SEO: getting a listing found in store search and turning visits into downloads. The agent fetches the listing page and extracts every field it can, from title and subtitle to ratings, release notes and languages, then takes a screenshot to judge the icon, screenshots and video, asking you for one if it has no browser tools. Before scoring it classes the app as dominant, established or challenger: a household name with millions of ratings gets credit for brand-only titles and lifestyle imagery that would be mistakes for an unknown app. Six areas are scored 0 to 10 and weighted into a grade from A to F: title and subtitle, description, visual assets, ratings and reviews, metadata and freshness, and conversion signals. Store rules are built in, such as Apple's hidden 100-byte keyword field and unindexed long description, against Google Play's indexed full description and limit of eight screenshots. Every recommendation must be specific (\"change subtitle from X to Y\"). It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/aso"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:app-store",
          "stack:google-play",
          "mobile",
          "seo",
          "conversion",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/free-tools",
      "name": "free-tools",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/free-tools",
        "installs": 79864,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2770, 2673, 2725, 3252, 3333, 2985, 3321, 3619],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans and evaluates free tools, such as calculators, generators or graders, that bring in leads, search traffic and links.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're considering building a free calculator, generator, grader or similar tool to attract leads or links"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "finds tool ideas in your audience's pain points, checks search demand and fit with your product, and weighs gating, build options and scope"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a verdict from an eight-factor scorecard out of 40: strong candidate, promising, or reconsider"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike lead-magnets",
              "text": "from the same repo, which covers downloadable content such as ebooks and checklists, this is about interactive tools"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Engineering as marketing: building something useful and giving it away so it brings in leads, search traffic and links. The idea is to take a capability others charge for and offer a free version, as Unsplash did with stock photos. A good tool solves a real problem for your audience, leads naturally to your product, and is worth more in leads than it costs to build and maintain. It sorts tools into calculators, generators, analyzers, testers, libraries and interactive tools, then finds ideas in what your audience searches for, the tedious tasks they do by hand, and what they need before buying. It weighs gating (fully, partly or not at all), keyword targets, custom, no-code or embedded builds, and a first version that skips accounts and saved results. Each idea is scored 1 to 5 on eight factors, including search demand, buyer match, uniqueness and maintenance burden. It warns against overbuilding before demand is proven and against counting visitors instead of leads. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/free-tools"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "seo"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/onboarding",
      "name": "onboarding",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/onboarding",
        "installs": 79743,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2666, 2468, 2379, 2663, 2895, 3098, 3294, 3713],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits or designs post-signup onboarding so new users reach value fast, from finding the aha moment to checklists, empty states and emails.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "people sign up but don't start using the product, or you're designing a first-run experience"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "defines your activation event, finds where users drop off, and designs the shortest path from signup to the product's core value"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an audit (finding, impact, recommendation and priority per issue) or a flow design with checklist items, empty-state copy, email triggers and a metrics plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike signup",
              "text": "from the same repo, which optimizes registration itself, this covers what happens once the account exists"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Onboarding aimed at the aha moment: the point where a new user first gets real value. Its core rule is to remove every step between signup and that value: design the minimum path to it, aim for one goal per first session, and have users do the thing rather than watch a tutorial. It explains why the mechanics work, such as checklists that open partly done, ending sessions on a high, and a behavior needing motivation, ability and a prompt at the same moment. To find your aha moment, it compares early actions with later retention on fully observed signup cohorts, treats strong correlations as hypotheses, and tests them before adopting them. It judges activation by conversion to customer and 90-day retention, not signup volume. It then covers the first 30 seconds after signup, checklists of 3 to 7 items, empty states, short tours, emails that reinforce in-app steps, and recovering stalled users. References cover the minimum path to value, five activation models such as freemium and free trial, and experiment ideas. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/onboarding"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "conversion",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/referrals",
      "name": "referrals",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/referrals",
        "installs": 78109,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2630, 2417, 2277, 2590, 2951, 3108, 3247, 3664],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs and improves referral, affiliate and review programs that turn customers and partners into a source of new customers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want customers or partners to bring in new customers, or need more reviews on sites such as G2"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "checks whether sharing can be built into the product first, then designs the referral loop: when to ask, how to share, and the reward"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a program design with incentives, fixes for weak spots, metrics, a launch checklist, a launch email and a schedule of reminder emails"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "affiliate and partner programs, review requests and replying to bad reviews, mostly through its references"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Word-of-mouth programs, from customer referrals to affiliates. Before any reward scheme it asks whether the product could spread on its own, through \"Powered by\" badges, shared outputs or embeds, and warns against forcing that on backend or internal tools. Customer referral programs suit products people already talk about; affiliate programs reach audiences you don't have, through partners paid ongoing commission. Program design follows a loop: pick a trigger moment such as right after the aha moment or a milestone, choose a share mechanism (in-product sharing converts best, a referral code works offline), and choose single-sided, double-sided or tiered rewards, presented as whichever number feels bigger and given at a milestone rather than at signup. It covers fixing low share or conversion rates, tests to run, fraud limits, metrics such as referred customers' lifetime value, and a 30-day launch checklist. Affiliate operations, viral mechanics, review programs and each review platform's rules live in references, and it lists tools such as Rewardful, Tolt and PartnerStack. It asks about lifetime value, acquisition cost and reward budget. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/referrals"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
