      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/signup",
        "installs": 77708,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2600, 2396, 2434, 2683, 2805, 2955, 3156, 3580],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits signup and registration flows field by field to cut drop-off, with prioritized fixes, test ideas and an optional form redesign.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "people start signing up but don't finish, or you're designing a trial, freemium or waitlist signup"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "questions every field, then reviews email and password fields, social sign-in, one step versus several, errors, mobile and what happens after submit"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "findings with impact, fix and priority, grouped into same-day fixes, bigger changes and A/B test ideas, plus a redesigned form if asked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike onboarding",
              "text": "from the same repo, which covers what happens after signup, it stops once the account exists"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Conversion work on the account-creation step. The rule is that every field costs signups: for each one it asks whether you need it before the person can use the product, could collect it later, or could infer it, which often leaves only email and password as essential. It suggests showing value before asking for commitment, setting expectations (\"takes 30 seconds\"), and trust lines such as \"no credit card required\" when true. Field by field it covers typo checks on emails, showing passwords and allowing paste, deferring phone and company fields, and placing Google, Apple or Microsoft sign-in prominently. It says when one step beats several (three fields or fewer), how to order multi-step forms from easy to hard, inline error handling, mobile layout, and email verification that doesn't stop people exploring. It lists metrics down to field-level drop-off, common patterns for B2B trials, consumer apps, waitlists and e-commerce accounts, and a long list of experiments. Lead-capture forms are left to the repo's cro skill. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/signup"
        },
