        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans compliant SMS, MMS and WhatsApp marketing programs, from consent and carrier registration to flows like abandoned cart and win-back.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're starting or fixing text-message marketing, deciding between SMS and email, or need to get compliant in the US, EU or Canada"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "checks compliance first, then ranks which flows to build by return, such as abandoned cart, welcome, post-purchase and win-back"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an SMS plan: compliance blockers, flow designs with timing and copy with character counts, a platform pick, a measurement plan and STOP/HELP templates"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "WhatsApp for audiences outside the US, with its 24-hour reply window and approved message templates"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Text-message marketing built on compliance. It explains US rules (written consent under the TCPA, honoring STOP within seconds, quiet hours, and registering business numbers with carriers through A2P 10DLC, without which messages are silently filtered) plus EU, UK and Canadian consent rules. It says when SMS beats email, such as cart recovery, shipping updates and flash sales, and when it doesn't, such as newsletters. It compares short codes, toll-free and regular numbers by list size, and sets principles: every send costs money, one link per message, the brand name in every text, and staying within 160 characters, since emojis cut a segment to 70. Flow templates give timing, such as a cart reminder 30 minutes after abandonment, with any discount saved for the second or third message. It compares platforms such as Klaviyo, Postscript, Attentive and Twilio, lists healthy benchmark ranges, and covers WhatsApp's reply window, templates, per-message pricing and its lack of US marketing delivery. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/sms"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "messaging",
          "marketing",
          "ecommerce"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/offers",
      "name": "offers",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/offers",
        "installs": 64200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2810, 2637, 2519, 2910, 3203, 3421, 3686, 4287],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-17"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs or improves the offer behind a sale (deliverable, bonuses, guarantee, urgency, name and payment terms) using Hormozi's value equation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "your offer isn't converting, or you're packaging a service, course, coaching or high-ticket product"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "scores your offer on the four levers of the value equation, finds the weakest of six parts, and redrafts one part per round"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a reworked bonus, guarantee, deadline, name or payment plan, with an honest estimate of the lift to confirm by test"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike pricing",
              "text": "from the same repo, which sets tiers and price levels, it improves what's included and how it's framed; self-serve SaaS should start with pricing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "use fake scarcity, inflated \"worth $X\" bonuses or over-promising guarantees"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Offer design for services, courses, coaching, info products, high-ticket B2B and direct response. Its premise is that a stronger offer with average copy beats better copy on a weak offer. The value equation, from Alex Hormozi's $100M Offers, rates value as the dream outcome times the buyer's belief they'll get it, divided by the wait and the effort it takes; most requests to lower a price are really requests to raise the top or shrink the bottom. A complete offer has six parts: the core deliverable, a bonus stack, a guarantee, scarcity or urgency, a name, and price with payment terms. Its diagnostic loop states the current offer plainly, scores each lever from 1 to 10, audits the six parts, changes only the weakest, and projects a 10 to 40% lift per change, multiplied rather than added. It bans words such as \"game-changing\" and unconditional \"100% guaranteed\", and advises against discounting to win new customers. References cover each part, formats by business type, SaaS offers and worked examples. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/offers"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "conversion"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/public-relations",
      "name": "public-relations",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/public-relations",
        "installs": 61100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2581, 2349, 2208, 2513, 2778, 2866, 3197, 3560],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Helps earn press coverage through pitches, newsjacking and journalist requests, and preps podcast appearances, press pages and crisis statements.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want journalists, podcasts or newsletters to cover you, or something has gone wrong and you need a public statement"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "routes your job to a playbook: reacting to trending news, pitching journalists, answering press requests, a press page and media kit, podcast prep, or a crisis"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "depending on the job, scored angles and a pitch under 150 words, a list of journalists, a podcast prep brief or a holding statement"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike directory-submissions",
              "text": "from the same repo, which lists you on startup directories, this earns coverage from journalists and shows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Measures",
              "text": "placements, backlinks, referral traffic, brand searches, AI citations and sales conversations, not advertising value equivalents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Public relations (not pull requests) for software products. Its premise: press multiplies distribution rather than replacing it, and a hit brings backlinks, credibility and AI citations, not direct sales. It works four modes, reacting to news, pitching stories, answering journalist requests and a press page with a media kit, and advises running at least three. The story is never the product but a trend, data, a conflict or a person, with the product as evidence; pitches use a founding story, an underdog against a giant, or an enemy that is a broken system, never a competitor. Jobs such as newsjacking, building a journalist list, answering HARO or Qwoted queries in under 200 words and prepping a podcast appearance each have a reference. A pitch goes out only if the journalist covers the beat, there's a news hook, the email alone holds a story, it's under 150 words and the ask is clear. For a crisis it assigns a severity tier and runs a first-hour checklist, bringing in legal counsel for breaches, injuries or lawsuits. It reads your product marketing context if you have it (a shared file on your product, audience and positioning that the repo's product-marketing skill writes).",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/public-relations"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/marketing-loops",
      "name": "marketing-loops",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/marketing-loops",
        "installs": 53800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2667, 2517, 2494, 2930, 3173, 3030, 3240, 3743],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up recurring marketing workflows an agent runs on a schedule, such as weekly SEO scans, ad-fatigue checks or churn watches, with safeguards.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a marketing task done on a cadence, such as a weekly SEO scan, an ad-fatigue check or a daily churn watch"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks or adapts one of 60 loops from its catalog, tunes how often it runs, decides what needs your approval, and schedules it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a scheduled loop with a trigger, steps, a check before acting, memory between runs and a stop condition"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "through Claude Code's /loop or scheduling tools, cron with any agent, or by hand each week"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike marketing-ideas",
              "text": "from the same repo, which suggests what to try once, it sets up what you keep doing, using the repo's other skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A marketing loop is a repeatable job an agent runs on a cadence: check a signal, act only when a condition is met, and otherwise skip. Each loop has nine parts: how often it checks, when it acts, its purpose, which of the repo's marketing skills it uses, its steps, a self-check so it doesn't act on noise or a tracking bug, what it remembers between runs so it doesn't act twice or nag the same person, when it stops or escalates, and where its output goes. Cadence follows how fast the signal moves: rankings weekly, ad fatigue every two or three days, churn signals daily. Drafting is fine to automate; publishing or shifting budget needs a human checkpoint unless you've authorized it with caps, and a guardrails reference covers spend and send limits, consent rules and a kill switch. The catalog holds 60 loops across SEO, paid, social, outbound, activation, retention, revenue and referrals. Two references bundle loops into an SEO operator and an outbound operator, with shared memory in .agents/ and a weekly report, and a template helps you write new loops. It advises starting with one loop, not ten.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/marketing-loops"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "agent-workflows",
          "marketing"
        ],
        "note": "Loop steps call the repo's other marketing skills, so it's most useful with them installed."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/marketing-council",
      "name": "marketing-council",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/marketing-council",
        "installs": 51600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2717, 2596, 2591, 3003, 3135, 2841, 3075, 3550],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-06"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Puts a marketing question to a simulated board of famous marketers like Ogilvy, Godin and Hormozi, then maps their disagreements into a recommendation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want several expert perspectives on a strategy, page, price change or launch before deciding"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "seats 3 to 5 of 12 marketers whose views clash, always including a dissenter, and has each apply their published frameworks to your case"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "each advisor's take, a map of where they disagree and what would settle it, and a chair's recommendation with next steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Grounding",
              "text": "labelled as simulation, with no invented quotes and no claims about what the real people think of your company"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "a quick take from one advisor, a council of 3 to 5, or all 12 for major decisions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A board of advisors built from twelve marketers' documented thinking: Seth Godin, David Ogilvy, Eugene Schwartz, Claude Hopkins, Gary Halbert, Russell Brunson, Alex Hormozi, April Dunford, Rory Sutherland, Byron Sharp, Ann Handley and Gary Vaynerchuk. The bench is chosen so their views conflict, because the disagreement is the useful part. For a council session it seats two or three advisors who fit the question type, such as Dunford and Godin for positioning, plus at least one whose position cuts against where the question is leaning. Each take runs two to four paragraphs, applying that advisor's frameworks from a dossier file, in their voice. An optional research pass searches the web, or uses a research skill if you have one, for what they've actually said on the topic, with citations. The synthesis names the trade-off behind each conflict, recommends what fits your stage, picks one advisor's warning to watch, and hands execution to skills such as offers or copywriting. Living advisors get extra care, and you can add your own advisor, such as a former boss whose positions you supply, saved in your project's .agents/advisors/ folder.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/marketing-council"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/attribution",
      "name": "attribution",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/attribution",
        "installs": 44500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2967, 2696, 2731, 3269, 3489, 3007, 3243, 3614],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Works out which marketing channels really drive conversions, reconciling conflicting numbers across tools, and helps you build first-party attribution yourself.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "your ad platforms, analytics and CRM disagree, or you need to know which channels actually drive revenue"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks one source of truth for the conversion count, explains each tool's bias, and triangulates with surveys and holdout tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an attribution readout: each source's numbers side by side, first-touch against last-touch, blind spots, and a budget call with confidence levels"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also builds",
              "text": "first-party tracking on a site you control, tying anonymous visits to signups and to bookings on tools like SavvyCal or Calendly"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike analytics",
              "text": "from the same repo, which sets up event tracking and UTMs, it assumes tracking exists and decides who gets credit"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Attribution, meaning which marketing touch gets credit for a sale, in two parts. Interpretation, for everyone: it treats attribution as directional, not truth, and every model as an opinion. It compares seven models, from first-touch to data-driven, with how each one misleads, noting that Google Ads and GA4 now offer only data-driven and last-click. It separates multi-touch tracking, media mix modeling (a regression of spend against outcomes) and incrementality tests that withhold ads from a group, and says which to use by budget. It recommends asking \"How did you hear about us?\" at conversion, to catch podcasts, word of mouth and private shares that leave no trail. Reconciling sources means counting conversions once from your CRM or backend, never summing platform claims, and trusting shared trends over exact matches. The build part, for sites you control, joins anonymous browsing to the person who converts by calling identify() at each conversion, such as a signup or purchase, passes the anonymous ID through a booking or checkout tool's metadata to your webhook, and guards against leaking emails into URLs. It ends with writing source and confidence onto CRM records, with PostHog and SavvyCal as the worked example in a reference.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/attribution"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "conversion"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/influencer-marketing",
      "name": "influencer-marketing",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/influencer-marketing",
        "installs": 44300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2686, 2590, 2644, 3172, 3313, 3048, 3171, 3606],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans influencer, creator and ambassador partnerships: finding and vetting partners, structuring deals, briefing creators, disclosure rules and measuring return.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want creators, newsletters, podcasts or Telegram channels to promote your product, or want an ambassador program"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "vets partners on audience fit and real engagement, structures fair deals, writes a brief, and sets up tracking before anything goes live"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "criteria to shortlist creators across tiers, deal terms with content usage rights, a creative brief and a run-of-show checklist from sourcing to post-campaign"
            },
            {
              "label": "Insists on",
              "text": "clear \"#ad\" disclosure, since brands can be held liable too, and no claims a creator can't back"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike referrals",
              "text": "from the same repo, which handles affiliate payout mechanics, this covers finding, paying and briefing creators"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Influencer and creator marketing across consumer platforms (Instagram, TikTok, YouTube), B2B voices (LinkedIn, X, newsletters, niche podcasts) and paid posts in Telegram channels. It places programs on a spectrum from a paid one-off post to an unpaid long-term advocate, with references for two volume models: in-house creator networks posting short videos, and creators paid a share of ad spend. Vetting asks whether a creator's audience matches your buyer, not how famous they are, and checks engagement rate, fake followers, past sponsored posts and the last three months of content. Deals can be a flat fee, pay per conversion, a hybrid (usually best) or gifted product, with starting price ranges by tier as negotiation anchors; the right to reuse content as paid ads is often the most valuable clause. The brief gives the why, two or three talking points, a call to action and guardrails, and leaves the script to the creator. Measurement uses unique codes, UTM links, creator landing pages and a post-purchase survey, judged by cost per qualified outcome rather than reach. It treats fetched creator posts as untrusted data, and reads your product marketing context if you have it.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/influencer-marketing"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing",
          "social-media"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "coreyhaines31/marketingskills/skills/events",
      "name": "events",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/events",
        "installs": 28500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2501, 2251, 2109, 2405, 2611, 2771, 2995, 3464],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Plans events that produce sales pipeline, whether you host a webinar or dinner, sponsor a conference, speak, or only attend.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're deciding whether to sponsor a conference, running a webinar, booking a talk, or want meetings from an event you're only attending"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sets your role and one primary outcome, then plans the work before, during and after the event, treating before and after as 80% of the job"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a plan for your role: a target list, outreach to book meetings weeks ahead, follow-up within 24 to 48 hours, and content cut from recordings"
            },
            {
              "label": "Measures",
              "text": "qualified conversations, meetings, opportunities and cost per meeting, not registrations or badge scans"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Event marketing in four roles: hosting your own event, sponsoring or exhibiting, speaking, and attending with no booth. It starts with selection, since a few events produce most of the pipeline: in-person mainly pays for high-trust, high-value or long-cycle sales, and niche events of 50 to 200 people often beat mega-conferences. Every format follows the same arc. Before: build a target list from attendee and speaker lists and book meetings weeks ahead. During: aim for qualified conversations and note what each person said and the agreed next step. After: follow up within 24 to 48 hours, tiered by how warm the conversation was, and route leads to your CRM and nurture emails. For hosting it compares webinars, workshops, dinners of 8 to 14 seats, meetups, virtual summits and user conferences, with two rules: pick topics your buyer would attend even if they never bought, and repeat rather than run one-offs. Attendees get a side-event play: host a dinner beside the conference for target accounts. Recordings are treated as the lasting asset, cut into clips, posts and sales proof. References cover the webinar funnel, sponsorship return, speaking and choosing events.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/coreyhaines31/marketingskills/tree/96798cf38736ecdba015a1d2b0521b0c8f8f41dd/skills/events"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "marketing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/review-pr",
      "name": "review-pr",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/review-pr",
        "installs": 29100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [874, 660, 613, 930, 499, 371, 369, 126],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a pull request from a local annotated diff and writes severity-labelled comments to review.json for a CI workflow to post.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a CI job has checked out a pull request and you want the agent's review as a file to publish, not comments posted directly"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reviews the lines the PR changes for correctness, security, error handling, performance, comment quality and tests, and labels each finding by severity"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "review.json: an APPROVE or REJECT verdict, a review body with an overview, issue counts and a recommendation, and inline comments, with suggestion blocks for code changes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "a line-annotated pr_diff.txt and pr_description.txt from your workflow, and Python 3 to run its bundled validator"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Built for an automated review step. The workflow checks out the PR and supplies pr_diff.txt, with every line annotated by its old or new line number, plus pr_description.txt. The agent puts correctness, security, error handling and real performance issues first, and treats comment and test quality as equal priorities, judged by the repo's own conventions. Every comment starts with a label: CRITICAL, IMPORTANT, SUGGESTION, or NIT, which is allowed only with a suggestion block. Line numbers are copied from the annotations; feedback on untouched code goes in the review body. Suggestion blocks must replace exactly the lines they target and keep brackets balanced. Before choosing a verdict, it lists every comment and test the diff adds and checks each against the repo's guidelines. It applies a repo's review-pr-local skill when one exists and, when spec_context.md is present, checks the code against the spec with a check-impl-against-spec skill if available. review.json must pass the bundled validator before it's done.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/review-pr"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:github",
          "code-review",
          "ci"
        ],
        "note": "Expects a workflow to supply pr_diff.txt with line annotations; it writes review.json and never posts to GitHub itself."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation",
      "name": "spec-driven-implementation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/spec-driven-implementation",
        "installs": 28900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [848, 648, 595, 930, 492, 362, 358, 112],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a spec-first workflow for large features: product spec, tech spec when needed, then code, with specs kept current in the same PR.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "starting a large, risky or ambiguous feature that an agent will build, such as one around 1k+ lines"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "decides whether specs are worth it, files a Linear issue if there's none, then has the agent write the product spec, the tech spec when warranted, and the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "PRODUCT.md and TECH.md under specs/<ticket>/, usually in the same PR as the code, kept current so they describe what ships"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Linear MCP server, and the write-product-spec, write-tech-spec and implement-specs skills"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Warp's process for features where written specs will improve an agent's work and the review. Specs are for substantial work: product or architectural ambiguity, around 1k+ lines, deep or cross-cutting changes, risky behavior changes. Small bug fixes, straightforward refactors and narrow UI tweaks usually skip them. Specs live in specs/<Linear ticket>/, and if no Linear issue exists the agent creates one first with the Linear MCP tools, asking you when the team or labels aren't obvious. write-product-spec writes PRODUCT.md, the user-facing behavior, and the agent asks for a Figma mock when there's UI. write-tech-spec writes TECH.md when the work spans subsystems or has trade-offs worth reviewing, possibly after an end-to-end prototype. implement-specs builds from them once they're approved. For large features it may offer PROJECT_LOG.md and DECISIONS.md to record explored paths and decisions. When the implementation drifts, the specs are updated with the code, in the same PR whenever practical, and the work is verified against them with unit and integration tests, and walkthrough recordings or screenshots for UI-heavy work.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/spec-driven-implementation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:mcp",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "stack:linear",
          "planning",
          "requirements",
          "implementation",
          "issue-tracking"
        ],
        "note": "Needs the Linear MCP server: it creates a Linear issue before writing specs when none exists."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-product-spec",
      "name": "write-product-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/write-product-spec",
        "installs": 28900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [858, 648, 593, 944, 496, 375, 365, 121],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a PRODUCT.md spec describing a feature's behavior from its user's side as numbered, testable rules, before implementation starts.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a feature's behavior pinned down in writing before an agent builds it, as a product spec or PRD"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks you for missing context and for a Figma mock when there's UI, then writes the behavior as numbered rules that can each be tested"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<id>/PRODUCT.md: a short summary and an exhaustive Behavior section, with no implementation details and no test plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "Next step",
              "text": "write-tech-spec, which plans the implementation and the tests from these rules"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A product spec here describes behavior only from the side of whoever uses the feature: the person in the app, code reading a data model, callers of an API, or a developer running a CLI. It names no internal types, modules or algorithms. The Behavior section carries the spec: the happy path, every visible state and transition, all inputs, empty, error, loading and cancelled states, edge cases such as offline, timeouts, races and focus loss, keyboard and accessibility, and what must never regress. Everything else stays thin: a summary of one to three sentences, and Problem, Goals, Figma or Open questions only when they add something. Testing is left out on purpose; it belongs in TECH.md, which write-tech-spec writes from these rules. The folder is named after a Linear ticket, a GitHub issue or a short feature name, and a new ticket is filed only if you ask. Length follows the feature: no spec for trivial fixes, about 30 to 60 lines for a small feature. It's written for Warp, and its worked example specifies Markdown tables in Warp's block list.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/write-product-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "planning"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec",
      "name": "write-tech-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/write-tech-spec",
        "installs": 28800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [856, 646, 594, 950, 493, 371, 367, 109],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches the codebase and writes a TECH.md implementation plan for a feature, with commit-pinned code links and tests mapped to the product spec.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a feature spans several modules, has real architectural trade-offs, or reviewers should see the plan before or with the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads the product spec and the relevant code, then plans the changes and maps each important rule of the spec to a test or check"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "specs/<id>/TECH.md next to PRODUCT.md, with file references linked to GitHub at the commit it inspected"
            },
            {
              "label": "Comes after",
              "text": "write-product-spec, whose PRODUCT.md it prefers to have first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The step after a product spec. Before writing, the agent reads PRODUCT.md if there is one and the code involved, and records the commit SHA of each repository it inspected, so file references link to GitHub at that commit and line range. The spec has four parts. Context: what's being built, how that area works today and the key files. Proposed changes: modules, new types, APIs and state, data flow, ownership, and the trade-offs when there's more than one reasonable path. Testing and validation: each important rule from PRODUCT.md mapped to a test or manual check. Parallelization, only when the agent has a run_agents tool: which sub-agents to run, local or remote, in which worktrees and branches, and how their work lands, or why it isn't worth it. Diagrams, an end-to-end flow, risks and follow-ups appear only when they add something. When the implementation is still too uncertain, an end-to-end prototype comes first and the spec is written from what it showed. A single-file change gets no tech spec or under 40 lines; multi-module work runs about 80 to 150. TECH.md is updated in the same PR as the implementation changes. It's written for Warp's codebase.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/write-tech-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "architecture",
          "multi-agent"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/resolve-merge-conflicts",
      "name": "resolve-merge-conflicts",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/resolve-merge-conflicts",
        "installs": 28700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [840, 643, 580, 931, 503, 385, 373, 119],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Resolves Git merge conflicts one file at a time from a script's compact view of each conflict, instead of reading whole files.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a merge, rebase, cherry-pick or stash pop stops on conflicts"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs a bundled Python script that lists the unresolved files, then shows one file's conflict hunks with ours, base and theirs and a compact diff"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the conflicts resolved and staged, with no markers left and targeted tests, builds or linters run on the touched area"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "context small: whole files are opened only when the compact view isn't enough"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Python 3; its commands call the script at .agents/skills/resolve-merge-conflicts/scripts/ in your repo"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The point is to keep the agent's context small. A bundled Python script first summarizes the conflict: which files are unmerged, which index stages exist and how many hunks each file has. The agent then takes one file at a time, asking the script for just that file: nearby lines, the ours, base and theirs versions of each hunk, and a compact diff between ours and theirs. It takes one side whole with git checkout --ours or --theirs, or edits the file and removes the markers, reading more of the file only when the compact view isn't enough to decide. Conflicts that leave no markers in the file, such as add/add or modify/delete, are shown from the index stages. Options print every conflicted file at once, output JSON, or change how many context lines and how much output it shows. It's done when no unmerged paths and no conflict markers remain, targeted tests, builds or linters have run for the touched area, and the resolved files are staged.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/resolve-merge-conflicts"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "git",
          "token-saving"
        ]
      }
    },
