        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds where the relevant code lives in a repository, read-only, and replies only with path:line citations for the agent that asked.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent must find where something lives in an unfamiliar codebase, search across many files, or retry after a direct search failed"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "only a list of path:start-end citations with one short reason each, or the single line \"no relevant locations found\""
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "fans out several Glob, Grep and Read calls at once, follows the evidence for a turn or two, and never edits, runs commands or proposes fixes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "as an explorer another agent delegates to; its frontmatter lists only the Read, Glob and Grep tools and the Haiku model"
            },
            {
              "label": "Skip it when",
              "text": "the exact file or symbol is already named"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A prompt for a fast, cheap, read-only code finder, which the SKILL.md calls FastContext. Another agent, the solver, hands it a question about where some code lives. In its first turn it casts a broad net with several searches at once: likely file paths, symbol and string matches, and reads of the most promising files. It follows the evidence for one or two more turns only if needed, and stops as soon as it can name the locations. It never edits files, runs commands or proposes a solution. Its whole reply is one citation per line, path:start-end plus a short reason, citing only line ranges it actually read, or \"no relevant locations found\" when nothing fits, since an honest empty answer beats a guess. The point is to keep the search's reads out of the solver's context, so the solver gets a short, exact list of locations for a few tokens.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2/skills/caveman-explore"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "category:output-style",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "token-saving"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/verify-and-stop",
      "name": "verify-and-stop",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/verify-and-stop",
        "installs": 117119,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12480, 13099, 10879, 10087, 8241, 7107, 7560, 7182],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Proves finished work meets its acceptance conditions with the smallest set of checks, then stops without adding fixes or polish.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a task only asks to check that existing work is done, such as a completion check, a focused gate run or last-mile proof"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "turns the acceptance conditions into the smallest set of checks that proves them, reusing results still current for the same code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the commands run, their results and any unresolved risk, with each check marked pass, fail, unavailable or blocked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "edit product code unless you asked for fixes, or add polish, cleanup or unrelated tests once the criteria pass"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike surgical-patch",
              "text": "which fixes a bug and then proves the fix, this only proves"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "on its own, whenever the agent sees a task that fits; it's a short list of rules, not a step-by-step guide"
            }
          ],
          "text": "One of Caveman's short work patterns, for tasks that ask for proof rather than changes. The agent translates the acceptance conditions into the smallest set of checks that would prove them. It reuses results that are still current for the same repository state, runs focused checks before wider gates, and keeps pass, fail, unavailable and blocked apart exactly. It doesn't edit product code unless the request includes fixes, and once the criteria pass it adds no polish, cleanup or unrelated tests. It stops as soon as the proof is complete, and reports only the commands, their results and any risk left unresolved.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2/skills/verify-and-stop"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "verification",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/ultracave",
      "name": "ultracave",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/ultracave",
        "installs": 38700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [0, 0, 0, 2037, 8495, 7368, 7838, 7418],
        "firstSeen": "2026-10-03"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Switches the agent to fragment-only replies, one word where one word does and each fact said once, until you say \"stop caveman\".",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "caveman's short sentences still feel too long and you want the tersest replies possible"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "drops articles, linking verbs and connectives, answers in one word when one is enough, and states each fact once"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "code, commands, paths, error strings, numbers and every \"not\", \"never\" or \"only\" exact, in your language"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike caveman",
              "text": "which keeps short plain sentences, this strips grammar down to fragments wherever the order stays clear"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you type /ultracave or /caveman ultra, then for the whole session until \"stop caveman\" or \"normal mode\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "Ultracave is caveman with the grammar stripped: the tersest of Caveman's reply modes. Replies become fragments, such as \"Inline object prop, new ref, re-render. `useMemo`.\", with articles, linking verbs and connectives dropped as long as the order stays clear; when a fragment isn't shorter than the sentence, the sentence wins. Nothing is restated and lists get no summary, a code change shows only the changed lines with context, and a tool run gets one line in and one line out. It never cuts code, commands, paths, API names, error strings, negations, numbers or units, and never invents abbreviations or adds words for flavor. It switches to plain prose for security warnings, confirmations of irreversible actions, ambiguous fragments, a confused user, questions to you, and anything saved outside the chat, such as code, commits, docs or PRs, then resumes. It doesn't load on its own: you start it with /ultracave or /caveman ultra, and it stays on until you say \"stop caveman\" or \"normal mode\".",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2/skills/ultracave"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "token-saving",
          "output-style"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "juliusbrussee/caveman/skills/megacave",
      "name": "megacave",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/juliusbrussee/caveman/megacave",
        "installs": 37600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [0, 0, 0, 1979, 8235, 7173, 7612, 7203],
        "firstSeen": "2026-10-03"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Switches the agent to terse replies in Classical Chinese, with code and technical terms kept verbatim, until you say \"stop caveman\".",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you read Classical Chinese and want replies that take far fewer characters on screen"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "answers in 文言文, the classical literary register: subjects omitted where clear, verb before object, particles instead of connecting phrases"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "code, commands, paths, API names and error strings in their original script, never translated; every negation; numbers and units in Arabic numerals"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike ultracave",
              "text": "which compresses your own language into fragments, this changes the language; it says its token savings are small and noisy"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you type /megacave or /caveman wenyan, then for the whole session until \"stop caveman\" or \"normal mode\""
            }
          ],
          "text": "Megacave is Caveman's reply mode in Classical Chinese (文言文) rather than modern written Chinese (白話). Replies omit subjects where they can be recovered, put the verb before the object, and use one particle per relation, such as 之, 乃 or 故, instead of connecting phrases, so answers take fewer characters on screen. The skill itself says the token savings are small and noisy, and tells the agent never to claim them. Code, commands, paths, API names and error strings stay in their original script, and negations and Arabic numerals are kept. A clause that would be ambiguous in Classical Chinese, or a sentence that wouldn't get shorter, is written in modern Chinese instead. For security warnings, confirmations of irreversible actions, a confused user, questions to you and anything saved outside the chat, such as code, commits or docs, it switches to modern Chinese or your language, in full sentences, then resumes. You start it with /megacave or /caveman wenyan, and it stays on until you say \"stop caveman\" or \"normal mode\".",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/juliusbrussee/caveman/tree/4bf8898b31c93326a5e5d90999241a1c15b52ed2/skills/megacave"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:output-style",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "output-style"
        ],
        "note": "Replies in Classical Chinese, so it's only useful if you read it."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-deep-research",
      "name": "firecrawl-deep-research",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/firecrawl-deep-research",
        "installs": 35900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [340, 389, 337, 326, 270, 259, 214, 255],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2a986240f9d786ae54fc33fcfd9ad37308e9cc3",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Researches a complex topic across the web with Firecrawl and writes a formal, cited report with contrarian views and open questions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a rigorous, cited report on a complex technical, policy, market or scientific topic, not a quick answer or a product pick"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks how long it should run, then searches and scrapes from 5 to 25 or more web sources through Firecrawl to match"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Markdown report: executive summary, numbered findings with links, analysis, contrarian views and risks, open questions, every source, and rerun inputs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Firecrawl CLI, or another way to call Firecrawl, and a Firecrawl API key"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike firecrawl-research-papers",
              "text": "it gathers evidence from web pages; literature reviews over published papers go to that skill"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Firecrawl's workflow for report-scale research, meant only for questions a short search can't answer; Firecrawl is a web search and scraping service. Before starting it asks how long the task should run and maps the answer to a depth: a few minutes means 3 to 5 searches and 5 to 10 scraped sources, 10 to 15 minutes means 5 to 10 searches from different angles and 15 to 25 sources, and longer means 10 or more searches and 25 or more sources, including primary sources, papers, expert and contrarian views. Where the agent can run subagents, it can split the work by angle: overview, technical detail, market context, contrarian views and primary sources, each returning claims, URLs, notes on source quality and uncertainty. The report cites sources for factual claims, prefers primary sources, flags conflicting evidence and synthesizes rather than listing page summaries. When the evidence is published literature, it hands that part to firecrawl-research-papers, which queries Firecrawl's paper index, and merges the results. The closing rerun inputs record the topic, depth and format so the same research can be repeated.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/tree/d2a986240f9d786ae54fc33fcfd9ad37308e9cc3/skills/firecrawl-deep-research"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "category:browser",
          "type:workflow",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:account",
          "by:official",
          "stack:firecrawl",
          "effect:none",
          "research",
          "web-search",
          "flag:mirror"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/skills/firecrawl-research-papers",
      "name": "firecrawl-research-papers",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/firecrawl-research-papers",
        "installs": 34000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [158, 173, 168, 188, 195, 314, 247, 161],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "d2a986240f9d786ae54fc33fcfd9ad37308e9cc3",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a sourced literature review from Firecrawl's index of research papers, finding related work and checking claims inside papers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a literature review, survey of studies or paper summary, especially on biomedical, clinical or computer-science topics"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "searches Firecrawl's paper index of PubMed, bioRxiv, medRxiv and arXiv abstracts, expands to similar, citing and cited papers, and reads papers to verify claims"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Markdown literature review: abstract, key papers with methods and findings, consensus, debates, emerging trends, sources and rerun inputs"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "a Firecrawl API key, and the Firecrawl CLI or MCP server"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike firecrawl-deep-research",
              "text": "that skill searches web pages; this one queries paper records, using web search only for whitepapers, leaderboards and other material outside the index"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Through the Firecrawl CLI or MCP server it has four paper tools: semantic search over abstracts, expansion from strong seed papers to similar papers, papers that cite them or papers they cite, canonical metadata for a paper, and a read that answers one question inside a paper's full text. It matches the approach to the request: one named paper takes a single search, a topic family takes searching then expanding, and constraints such as author, venue, date or method are checked in the metadata or the paper body before a candidate is kept. For rankings and leaderboards it uses ordinary web search, then maps the top entries back to papers. It errs toward including the relevant family of papers rather than one best hit, and drops only clearly off-topic ones. The index is mostly PubMed, bioRxiv and medRxiv, plus arXiv in computer science, physics and maths; coverage elsewhere is thinner. It warns that Firecrawl's research search category only filters web results to research sites and doesn't query the index. Work can be split across subagents by source type or sub-topic. The review separates peer-reviewed work from blogs and vendor reports and notes PDFs it couldn't read.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/firecrawl/firecrawl-workflows/tree/d2a986240f9d786ae54fc33fcfd9ad37308e9cc3/skills/firecrawl-research-papers"
        },
        "tags": [
