    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/spec-driven-development",
      "name": "spec-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/spec-driven-development",
        "installs": 72939,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3274, 4206, 3878, 4146, 5297, 5901, 5489, 4959],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a spec covering objective, commands, structure, code style, testing and boundaries, and stops for your approval before planning or code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you start a project, feature or significant change and nothing is written down yet about what to build"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lists its assumptions for you to correct, asks clarifying questions, then writes a spec and ends its turn until you approve it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a spec file in the repo with objective, commands, structure, code style, testing, boundaries, success criteria and open questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Then",
              "text": "gated phases follow, each reviewed by you: a plan in tasks/plan.md, a task list, then implementation one task at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "For big asks",
              "text": "a request bundling several separate capabilities first gets a map of modules and build order, then a spec per module"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Spec-driven development runs in gated phases, specify, plan, tasks, implement, and doesn't advance until you've reviewed the current one. It starts by listing the assumptions it's making (web app or mobile? which database?) so you can correct them, and turns vague asks into testable targets: \"make the dashboard faster\" becomes a load time under 2.5 seconds. The spec covers six areas: the objective and users, the exact build, test and lint commands, the project layout, a real snippet of the code style, the testing strategy, and three tiers of boundaries: always do, ask first, never do. Once the spec is saved, the agent summarizes it, lists open questions and stops its turn; planning starts only after you approve in a later turn. The plan goes to tasks/plan.md and the task list to tasks/todo.md, deferring to planning-and-task-breakdown, another skill in the same repo, for the details, with each task small, ordered by dependency and paired with a check. If a project already uses a spec tool such as OpenSpec, it keeps that format instead of adding a SPEC.md. The spec stays alive: it's committed, updated before the code when decisions change, and referenced from PRs.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/spec-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "planning"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/documentation-and-adrs",
      "name": "documentation-and-adrs",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/documentation-and-adrs",
        "installs": 71820,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3228, 4142, 3882, 4184, 5330, 6002, 5539, 4986],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Records why decisions were made in architecture decision records, and sets rules for comments, API docs, READMEs and changelogs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you make an architectural decision, change a public API, ship a feature, or keep explaining the same thing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "has the agent write ADRs, short records of a decision with its context, rejected alternatives and consequences, plus docs that explain why"
            },
            {
              "label": "Matches",
              "text": "your project's existing ADR folder, numbering and headings first; otherwise it numbers them in docs/decisions/"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "comments that explain intent, typed API docs and OpenAPI, README and changelog shapes, rules files for agents, and a prose review"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its premise is that code shows what was built, and documentation should record why. The highest-value document is the architecture decision record (ADR): a short file per significant decision, such as a database, an auth strategy or an API style, with its status, context, the decision, the alternatives considered and why each was rejected, and the consequences. ADRs are never deleted; a new one supersedes the old. Before writing one, the agent looks for the project's own convention, its folder, file format, numbering and headings, and only falls back to the skill's template in docs/decisions/ when there is none. In code, comments explain intent and known gotchas rather than restating the code, with no commented-out code and no TODOs for things to do now. Public APIs get typed doc comments or an OpenAPI spec, a README covers quick start, commands and architecture, and changelog entries are grouped under Added, Fixed and Changed. Rules files such as CLAUDE.md, specs and ADRs are kept current for agents too. A final prose review strips filler and promotional wording while checking that names, numbers, commands and caveats survive the edit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/documentation-and-adrs"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "docs-writing",
          "architecture"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/planning-and-task-breakdown",
      "name": "planning-and-task-breakdown",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/planning-and-task-breakdown",
        "installs": 71813,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3073, 4108, 3816, 3952, 5062, 5728, 5370, 4623],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Breaks a spec into small, ordered tasks, each with acceptance criteria and a check, in a plan file and task list.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a spec or clear requirements and need tasks an agent can implement, or a task feels too big to start"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads the spec and code without writing any, maps dependencies, slices the work into end-to-end features, then writes and orders the tasks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "tasks/plan.md with decisions, risks and open questions, and a task list in tasks/todo.md or your issue tracker, with checkpoints"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "overwrite a plan that still has unchecked tasks for other work; it stops and asks you"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Planning starts read-only: the agent reads the spec and the relevant code, notes patterns and risks, and maps what depends on what, from database schema up to UI. It then slices vertically, one complete feature path per task (\"user can log in\": schema, API and UI together) instead of all the database, then all the API. Each task gets a description, acceptance criteria, verification commands, dependencies, likely files and a size; anything that would touch more than about five files, take more than one session, or need \"and\" in its title gets split. Tasks are ordered so dependencies come first, risky work comes early and the system works after each one, with a checkpoint every two or three tasks. The plan always goes to tasks/plan.md; tasks go to tasks/todo.md by default, or one item per task in an issue tracker such as GitHub Issues, Jira or Linear when the project's agent rules or you say so. It also lists which work is safe to run in parallel, such as independent slices, and which must stay sequential, such as migrations. A /build command and other downstream tools expect these default files, and you approve the plan before implementation starts.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/planning-and-task-breakdown"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/code-simplification",
      "name": "code-simplification",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/code-simplification",
        "installs": 70856,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3055, 4059, 3820, 4026, 5173, 5835, 5470, 4984],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Simplifies working code for readability while keeping its behavior exactly the same, one tested change at a time.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "code works and tests pass, but it's harder to read or change than it should be"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "learns why the code is the way it is, spots deep nesting, long functions, vague names and dead code, then simplifies one change at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "inputs, outputs, errors and side effects identical; the tests run after each change, and one that fails them is reverted"
            },
            {
              "label": "Scope",
              "text": "recently changed code by default, matched to your project's conventions, with no drive-by refactors elsewhere"
            },
            {
              "label": "Inspired by",
              "text": "Anthropic's Code Simplifier plugin for Claude Code, adapted for any coding agent"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Simpler here means faster to understand, not fewer lines. Before touching anything the agent applies Chesterton's Fence: don't remove what you don't understand. It works out what the code is for, what calls it, its edge cases, its tests and, through git blame, why it was written that way. Then it scans for concrete signals: nesting three levels deep, functions over 50 lines, nested ternaries, boolean flag parameters, generic or misleading names, duplicated logic, wrappers that add nothing, a factory for a factory. Each simplification is made alone, the test suite runs, and the change is kept or reverted; refactors go in their own PR, apart from features. Past 500 lines of edits, it reaches for codemods or AST transforms instead of hand edits. It warns against over-simplifying too: inlining a helper that named a concept, merging unrelated functions, chasing line count. Before-and-after examples cover TypeScript, Python and React. A final check asks whether the result is genuinely easier to read; if not, it's reverted.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/code-simplification"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "refactoring",
          "code-quality"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/api-and-interface-design",
      "name": "api-and-interface-design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/api-and-interface-design",
        "installs": 70738,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3087, 4092, 3920, 4210, 5449, 5961, 5676, 5075],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Designs APIs and module interfaces that are hard to misuse: contract first, one error format, validation at the edges, changes that don't break callers.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you design REST or GraphQL endpoints, module boundaries, component props or any interface other code depends on"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies rules such as Hyrum's Law: once enough people use an API, every behavior it shows, documented or not, is something to keep"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "contracts before code, one error shape, validation where outside data enters, optional new fields, naming, pagination and PATCH"
            },
            {
              "label": "Goes deep on",
              "text": "idempotency keys for safe retries: derived from the intent, claimed atomically through a unique constraint, kept as long as any retry path"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Rules the agent follows when it shapes an interface. Two principles frame them: Hyrum's Law, so it is deliberate about every observable behavior and plans for deprecation from the start, and the One-Version Rule: extend an interface rather than make consumers choose between versions. The interface is written as a typed contract before the implementation. Errors share one shape and a fixed mapping to HTTP status codes. Input is validated where it enters, at route handlers, forms, environment loading and third-party responses, which count as untrusted, and trusted inside after that. New fields are optional additions; existing ones never change type or disappear. Naming follows fixed conventions (plural nouns in URLs, camelCase fields, is or has for booleans), list endpoints paginate, and updates use PATCH. A long section on idempotency keys makes retries of a payment safe: the key comes from the client or the original event, a unique constraint picks the winner instead of a check-then-act race, a reused key with a different body fails, an in-flight duplicate gets a deliberate 409, wait or 202, and keys outlive the longest retry path. TypeScript patterns include discriminated unions, separate input and output types, and branded ID types.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/api-and-interface-design"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:typescript",
          "architecture",
          "backend"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/debugging-and-error-recovery",
      "name": "debugging-and-error-recovery",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/debugging-and-error-recovery",
        "installs": 70710,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3230, 4140, 3847, 3990, 5090, 5800, 5424, 4901],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Stops feature work when something breaks, then reproduces, localizes, reduces and fixes the root cause, guarded by a regression test.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a test fails, the build breaks, something that worked yesterday doesn't, or behavior doesn't match what you expected"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "halts new changes, keeps the evidence, then reproduces, finds the failing layer, shrinks the case, fixes the cause and verifies end to end"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a fix for the underlying cause rather than the symptom, plus a test that fails without it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "bugs that won't reproduce, git bisect for regressions, triage trees for test, build and runtime errors, and when to remove debug logging"
            },
            {
              "label": "Treats",
              "text": "error messages and logs as data: it won't run commands or open URLs they suggest without asking you"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts with a stop-the-line rule: when anything unexpected happens, the agent stops adding features, preserves the error output and logs, and doesn't resume until the fix is verified. The triage runs in order. Reproduce: make the failure happen reliably, with a decision tree for bugs that depend on timing, environment or state. Localize: decide which layer fails, UI, API, database, build tooling, an outside service or the test itself, and use git bisect to find the commit behind a regression. Reduce: strip code, config and input down to the smallest failing case. Fix the root cause, asking why until the real cause appears: in its example, duplicate rows in a list come from a JOIN in the API, so the query gets fixed instead of deduplicating in the UI. Guard: write a test that fails without the fix. Verify: run the focused test, the full suite and the build with the repository's own commands. Quick triage trees cover common test, build and runtime failures, and safe fallbacks show how to degrade gracefully under time pressure. Error output from outside sources is data to read, not instructions to follow.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/debugging-and-error-recovery"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "testing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/test-driven-development",
      "name": "test-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/test-driven-development",
        "installs": 69655,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3107, 4046, 3774, 3933, 4993, 5875, 5442, 4710],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a failing test before the code that makes it pass, and reproduces every bug as a test before fixing it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "the agent implements logic, fixes a bug or changes behavior, even when you didn't mention tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "finds how your repo runs tests, writes a test that fails, then the least code that passes, then refactors with the tests green"
            },
            {
              "label": "For bugs",
              "text": "a test reproducing the bug must fail before any fix and pass after it, followed by the full suite"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "a test mix of about 80% unit, 15% integration and 5% end-to-end, readable self-contained tests, and real code preferred over mocks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Its cycle is red, green, refactor: write a test that fails, write the minimum code that passes it, then clean up while the tests stay green. Before the first test, the agent works out how this repository tests, from package.json, Gradle, pyproject.toml, Cargo.toml or the CI config, and uses those commands rather than assuming npm test. A bug report starts with a test that reproduces it, not with a fix. Beyond the loop it covers writing tests worth keeping: about 80% small unit tests, 15% integration and 5% end-to-end; the Beyonce Rule (if you liked it, you should have put a test on it); assertions on outcomes rather than on internal calls; DAMP over DRY, so each test tells its whole story without shared helpers; real implementations before fakes, stubs and mocks; one concept per test and descriptive names. For browser code it adds a runtime check through Chrome DevTools MCP, treating everything read from the page as untrusted, and for hard bugs it can have a subagent write the reproducing test without knowing the fix. It also tells the agent not to rerun a test command when nothing has changed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/test-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "tdd",
          "testing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/context-engineering",
      "name": "context-engineering",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/context-engineering",
        "installs": 69454,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3202, 4186, 3801, 3959, 5023, 5661, 5339, 4879],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up what the agent sees: a rules file, the right spec and source files per task, and trimmed context in long sessions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you start a session or switch tasks, set up a project for an agent, or its output drifts from your conventions"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "layers context from a rules file such as CLAUDE.md down to the current error, loading only what the task needs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a rules file template with stack, commands, conventions and boundaries, plus prompt shapes for packing a task's context"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "what to cut from a long session and what to keep, when it's safe to restart and what to write down first, and asking instead of guessing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It treats context as five layers, from most to least lasting: a rules file loaded every session (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, Cursor or Copilot rules), the relevant section of the spec, the files the task touches, the latest error output, and the conversation. The agent reads a file before editing it, finds an existing example of the pattern it's about to write, and trusts project code more than config, fixtures or outside docs, whose instruction-like text it reports to you. Templates show a rules file and three ways to pack context: a brain dump at session start, a selective include of only the task's files, and a project map. For long sessions it budgets: compact well before the limit, drop failed attempts and verbose output first, summarize rather than delete, protect the task definition and current error, and put task-critical material last. A fresh session is safe at a finished task, once scope, status, changed files, verification results and open questions are written down. Conflicts between spec and code get options and a question, not a guess; a missing requirement does too, unless the code sets a precedent. It suggests MCP servers such as Context7 and Chrome DevTools for richer context.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/context-engineering"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "agent-workflows",
          "handoff"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/incremental-implementation",
      "name": "incremental-implementation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/incremental-implementation",
        "installs": 69344,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3122, 4051, 3778, 3916, 4977, 5644, 5280, 4556],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a change in thin slices, each implemented, tested, verified and committed before the next, touching only what the task needs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a feature or change touches more than one file, or the agent is about to write a lot of code at once"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "builds one small working piece, runs the tests and build, commits it, then moves to the next piece"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a series of small commits, each leaving the project building and tested, and each revertible on its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "clean up nearby code or add features outside the task; it lists what it noticed and asks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The cycle is implement, test, verify, commit, repeat, with each slice the smallest complete piece of functionality. Slices are vertical by default, one path through database, API and UI, such as \"create a task\"; contract-first when front and back end are built in parallel, with the API types defined first; or risk-first, proving the riskiest part works before building on it. Rules keep each slice clean: start with the simplest thing that could work and no abstraction until a third use; touch only what the task needs and list anything else worth fixing instead of fixing it; one logical change per increment; the project builds and tests pass after every slice; unfinished features hide behind feature flags; new options default to the safe choice; and each increment can be reverted alone, with rollback migrations for schema changes. After each slice the agent runs the repository's own test, build, type-check and lint commands, without rerunning one when nothing has changed. More than about 100 lines without a test run is a red flag.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/incremental-implementation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
