        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a vague idea into a one-page proposal by expanding it into variations, stress-testing the best, and naming what not to build.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a rough concept and want to explore options and test its assumptions before committing to a plan"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "restates the idea as a \"How might we\" question, asks three to five questions, offers five to eight variations, then narrows to two or three directions"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a Markdown one-pager: problem, recommended direction, assumptions to test, MVP scope, a Not Doing list and open questions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Saves",
              "text": "to docs/ideas/, only after you confirm"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "interview-me, which finds out what you want at all, it starts from an idea and widens it before narrowing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A thinking partner for the step before a spec. In the first phase the agent restates your idea as a \"How might we...?\" problem, asks three to five sharpening questions (who is it for, what does success look like, what are the constraints, what's been tried, why now) and won't go on until it knows who it's for and what success means. Then it generates five to eight variations through lenses such as inversion, removing a constraint, a different audience, a version ten times simpler or ten times bigger; inside a codebase it grounds them in the existing code. Once you react, it clusters what resonated into two or three directions, tests each for user value, feasibility and differentiation, and names the hidden assumptions and what could kill each. The last phase writes the one-pager, where the Not Doing list makes the trade-offs explicit. It's told to be honest rather than supportive and to push back on weak ideas. Extra files in the skill's folder hold more ideation frameworks, an evaluation rubric and example sessions.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/idea-refine"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "requirements",
          "planning"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/using-agent-skills",
      "name": "using-agent-skills",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/using-agent-skills",
        "installs": 66394,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3026, 3938, 3658, 3797, 4851, 5496, 5175, 4455],
