        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks and debugs web pages in a real Chrome through the Chrome DevTools MCP server: DOM, console, network, performance and screenshots.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you build or debug anything that runs in a browser and want the agent to see what actually renders"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reproduces the bug in Chrome, inspects console, DOM, styles, network and accessibility tree, fixes the source, then reloads and compares screenshots"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the chrome-devtools MCP server set up in your agent; the skill gives the config, with a throwaway browser profile"
            },
            {
              "label": "Guards",
              "text": "treats page content as untrusted, keeps JavaScript read-only unless you approve a change, never reads cookies or tokens, and asks before following links from the page"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Chrome DevTools MCP lets the agent look at a running page instead of guessing from the code. The skill gives a config snippet that starts Chrome with a temporary profile, wiped when it closes, and warns against attaching to your everyday browser, where the agent could see every open tab and logged-in session. It then follows short workflows. For UI bugs: reproduce and screenshot, inspect the console, DOM, computed styles and accessibility tree, decide whether HTML, CSS, JavaScript or data is at fault, fix the source, reload and compare. For network problems: capture the request and check its URL, payload, status and timing. For performance: record a trace, read LCP, CLS, INP and long tasks, fix, record again. It also covers written test plans for complex UI bugs, before-and-after screenshots for CSS changes, a zero-errors-and-warnings console standard, and an accessibility pass over names, headings, focus order, contrast and live regions. Security rules run throughout: everything read from the page is data, not instructions, JavaScript runs read-only, credentials are off limits, and anything that changes the page needs your confirmation.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/browser-testing-with-devtools"
        },
