    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/shipping-and-launch",
      "name": "shipping-and-launch",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/shipping-and-launch",
        "installs": 65842,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3008, 3916, 3601, 3885, 5084, 5671, 5253, 4598],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Prepares a production launch: a pre-launch checklist, feature flags, a staged rollout with rollback thresholds, monitoring and a written rollback plan.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're about to deploy to production and want to know what must be in place first"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "walks a pre-launch checklist across code quality, security, performance, accessibility, infrastructure and docs, then plans a staged rollout"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rollout",
              "text": "deploy with the flag off, enable for your team, then 5%, 25%, 50% and 100% of users, advancing only while error rate and latency hold"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a rollback plan with triggers, steps, database considerations and expected time to roll back"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "first-hour checks after launch, error reporting, what to monitor, feature flag cleanup and an error-budget release gate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Launches should be reversible, observable and incremental. The pre-launch checklist runs through code quality (tests, build, lint, review, no stray console.log), security (secrets, dependency audit, validation, auth, headers, rate limits, CORS), performance, accessibility, infrastructure (environment variables, migrations, DNS and SSL, logging, a health check) and documentation. Features ship behind flags, each with an owner and an expiry date, removed within two weeks of full rollout and tested both on and off in CI. The rollout moves from staging to production with the flag off, then to the team, a 5% canary and wider steps, with a table of thresholds deciding whether to advance, hold or roll back: for example, roll back when errors double or p95 latency rises more than 50%. It lists what to monitor across the application, infrastructure and client, shows error reporting in a React error boundary and an Express handler, and gives a first-hour check after launch. An error budget, the share of failures your service level objective allows, gates releases: under 20% left means slow rollouts only, none left means a feature freeze. Every deploy needs a written rollback plan before it happens.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/shipping-and-launch"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:cloud",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "deployment",
          "observability"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/source-driven-development",
      "name": "source-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/source-driven-development",
        "installs": 65785,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3024, 3950, 3685, 3860, 4918, 5528, 5219, 4709],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks framework-specific code against the official docs for your exact versions, and cites the source for every pattern it uses.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want framework or library code that follows current documented practice instead of the agent's possibly outdated memory"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads your dependency files for exact versions, fetches the specific official doc page, writes code that follows it, then cites it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "code with full source URLs in comments and replies, and anything it couldn't find documented flagged as unverified"
            },
            {
              "label": "Trusts",
              "text": "official docs and changelogs, then MDN, web.dev and caniuse; never Stack Overflow, blog posts or its own training data"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The process is detect, fetch, implement, cite. The agent first reads package.json, pyproject.toml, go.mod or the like and states the versions it found, asking you when they're missing, since the version decides which pattern is right. It then fetches the one documentation page for the feature at hand, such as a specific React hook's reference, not a homepage or a general search, and notes deprecations and migration advice. Sources rank from official docs, to official blogs and changelogs, to web standards references, to compatibility tables. It writes code the way the docs show, uses the new way where the docs have one, and when the docs disagree with your existing code it shows you both options instead of picking one. Every framework-specific decision gets a citation with a full, deep-linked URL, quoting the passage when the choice isn't obvious. Fetched pages are treated as data: it takes API signatures, examples and deprecation notes from them, ignores anything addressed to the model, and won't hardcode a telemetry or other outside endpoint from an example without telling you. It skips the process for version-independent changes or when you ask for speed.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/source-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "implementation",
          "verification",
          "research"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/ci-cd-and-automation",
      "name": "ci-cd-and-automation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/ci-cd-and-automation",
        "installs": 65706,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2845, 3898, 3586, 3798, 4857, 5480, 5147, 4705],
        "firstSeen": "2026-02-16"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sets up CI that blocks merges until lint, type checks, tests, build and a security audit pass, and plans deploys you can roll back.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you set up or change a build and deploy pipeline, add automated checks, or a CI failure needs fixing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lays out a pipeline where every pull request passes lint, type check, unit tests, build, integration tests, optional end-to-end tests, an audit and a bundle-size check"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "GitHub Actions workflows for CI, a Postgres integration job, Playwright tests, preview deploys and a rollback, plus a Dependabot config"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule",
              "text": "no gate gets skipped: a failing lint rule is fixed, not disabled, and a failing test means fixing the code, not skipping the test"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "feeding CI errors back to the agent, feature flags, staged rollouts, secrets per environment, branch protection and speeding up slow pipelines"
            }
          ],
          "text": "CI is framed as the enforcement mechanism for every other skill, built on two ideas: shift left, catching problems as early in the pipeline as possible, and faster is safer, since small, frequent releases are easier to debug. Every change passes the same gates before review: lint, type check, unit tests, build, integration tests, optional end-to-end tests, a security audit and a bundle-size check, and none can be skipped. The examples are GitHub Actions: a basic quality job on Node, an integration job with a Postgres service and Prisma migrations, a Playwright job that uploads its report on failure, a Vercel preview deploy per pull request, and a manual rollback workflow that passes its input through an environment variable to avoid shell injection. When CI fails, the error goes back to the agent to fix and verify locally before pushing again. It also covers feature flags with a cleanup date, staged rollouts with a monitoring window, keeping production secrets out of CI, Dependabot, a Build Cop who keeps the build green, required reviews and status checks, and, for pipelines over ten minutes, caching, parallel jobs, path filters and sharded tests.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/ci-cd-and-automation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:cloud",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:github",
          "ci",
          "deployment"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/deprecation-and-migration",
      "name": "deprecation-and-migration",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/deprecation-and-migration",
        "installs": 64810,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2917, 3892, 3588, 3781, 4824, 5459, 5119, 4611],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Retires old systems, APIs or features safely: replacement first, consumers migrated one by one, old code removed once nothing uses it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you replace or sunset a system, API, library or feature, or rename or drop a database column in production"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks whether the old system still earns its keep, builds and documents the replacement, migrates each consumer, then removes the old code once usage is zero"
            },
            {
              "label": "For schemas",
              "text": "expand and contract: add the new column, write to both, backfill in batches, switch reads, and drop the old column in a later deploy"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "advisory versus forced deprecation, the strangler, adapter and feature-flag migration patterns, and zombie code nobody owns"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It starts from the view that code is a liability: every line costs upkeep, and with enough users every behavior gets depended on, so removal needs active migration, not just an announcement. Five questions decide whether to deprecate: does it still provide unique value, who depends on it, does a replacement exist, what will migrating cost each consumer, and what does keeping it cost? Deprecation defaults to advisory, with warnings and docs; a hard deadline is for security risks or unsustainable upkeep, and needs migration tooling. The process is to build a replacement proven in production, publish a deprecation notice with a migration guide, migrate consumers one at a time, verifying each, and remove the old code, tests, docs and config only once metrics show zero use. If you own what's being deprecated, migrating its users is your job. Patterns include running old and new side by side while shifting traffic (the strangler pattern), an adapter that keeps the old interface over the new code, and per-user feature flags. Database changes use expand and contract across separate deploys, each with a tested down migration. Zombie code, depended on but unowned, gets an owner or a removal plan.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/deprecation-and-migration"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "migration",
          "databases"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/interview-me",
      "name": "interview-me",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/interview-me",
        "installs": 64800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2930, 4011, 3888, 3975, 5023, 5728, 5365, 4606],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Interviews you one question at a time, each with its best guess attached, until it can predict your answers, then confirms what you want.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an ask is underspecified, such as \"build me a dashboard\", or you say \"interview me\" or \"grill me\""
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "states its read of what you want with a confidence number, then asks one question at a time, each with its own guess for you to correct"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a short statement of intent you confirm with an explicit yes: outcome, user, why now, success, constraint and what's out of scope"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops",
              "text": "when it can predict your reaction to its next three questions; after you confirm, it ends its turn and waits"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "idea-refine and spec-driven-development, which assume you know roughly what you want, it finds that out first"
            }
          ],
          "text": "People ask for what one usually asks for, a dashboard, something faster, and this skill digs for what they actually want before any plan or code exists. The agent writes one sentence on what it thinks you want, with an honest confidence percentage and, below 70%, what's still missing. Then it asks one question per message, always with its guess for the answer, because reacting to a wrong guess is faster than answering from scratch. It listens for answers that sound like best practice rather than a real want (\"scalable\", \"the standard approach\") and asks what you'd want if you didn't have to justify it. When confident, it restates your intent in five to eight lines, including what's out of scope, and only an explicit yes counts: \"whatever you think\" or \"sounds good\" get a follow-up. Once you confirm, it offers to save the statement to docs/intent/, draft a spec or move on to planning, and stops. It isn't for clear or mechanical asks, and it won't run in CI or other loops with no person to answer. In its example, two questions turn a \"dashboard\" into a list of experiments.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/interview-me"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "interview",
          "requirements"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/doubt-driven-development",
      "name": "doubt-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/doubt-driven-development",
        "installs": 64200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3016, 3940, 3706, 3927, 5050, 5637, 5286, 4624],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sends each non-trivial decision to a fresh-context reviewer told to find what's wrong, then weighs every finding before the decision stands.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "stakes are high, such as auth, security or an irreversible migration, or you're working in code you don't fully understand"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "states the claim, hands only the code and its requirements to a subagent told to disprove it, then sorts each finding and fixes the real ones"
            },
            {
              "label": "Offers",
              "text": "a second opinion from another model through the Gemini or Codex CLI every cycle, run only with your go-ahead and in read-only mode"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops",
              "text": "after a round with only trivial findings, after three rounds, or when you say ship it"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "a review of a finished pull request, it checks decisions while they're still cheap to change"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents, started from the main session rather than from inside one"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Long sessions turn assumptions into \"facts\", so this skill makes the agent doubt its own non-trivial work in progress. A decision counts as non-trivial when it adds branching logic, crosses a module boundary, claims something the compiler can't check such as thread safety, depends on context a reader won't see, or can't be undone. Each doubt cycle has five steps. Claim: name the decision and why it matters in two or three lines. Extract: cut out the smallest reviewable unit, the diff or proposal plus the contract it must meet, without the reasoning that produced it. Doubt: give that to a fresh-context reviewer with an adversarial prompt (find issues, don't validate), and never the claim itself, which would bias it toward agreeing. Reconcile: reread the artifact against each finding and class it as a misread contract, a real issue to fix, an accepted trade-off or noise. Stop: after trivial findings, three cycles, or your say-so. In interactive sessions it always offers a cross-model review through Gemini CLI, Codex CLI or a manual paste, checks the tool works, confirms the command with you and pipes the prompt in read-only mode. In CI or loops, it skips this and says so.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/doubt-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "verification",
          "multi-agent"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/observability-and-instrumentation",
      "name": "observability-and-instrumentation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/observability-and-instrumentation",
        "installs": 62100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [3039, 3973, 3752, 3976, 5066, 5641, 5346, 4522],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Instruments a feature with structured logs, metrics, traces and symptom-based alerts, each tied to a question on-call will ask.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you build anything that runs in production, add an endpoint, job or outside integration, or an incident was hard to diagnose"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "writes down what on-call will ask about the feature, adds the log, metric or trace that answers each question, then alerts with runbooks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "JSON logs with a request ID on every line, rate, error and latency metrics, OpenTelemetry traces, and alerts linked to short runbooks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Proves it",
              "text": "forces an error in staging, finds it through the telemetry alone, and fires each new alert once"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "shipping-and-launch, which lists what to watch on launch day, it adds the logs, metrics, traces and alerts that feed it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Instrumentation is written with the feature, like tests. The agent starts from two to four questions an on-call engineer will ask, such as why payments fail, and every signal must answer one: metrics say that something is wrong, traces say where, logs say why. Logs are structured events with stable names, a correlation ID carried across every call and, when several entry points write to one log, a field naming which one started the run; secrets and full personal data never go in. Every endpoint and outside dependency gets RED metrics (rate, errors, duration), resources get USE (utilization, saturation, errors), latency is a histogram read at p50, p95 and p99, and labels come only from small fixed sets, never user IDs or URLs. Tracing uses OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation, with manual spans only around meaningful work. Alerts fire on symptoms users feel, such as error rate or latency, not on CPU; each is actionable, has a justified threshold, one of two severities and a runbook of a few lines in docs/runbooks/. Finally it verifies the telemetry itself: logs are structured, metrics appear, a request can be followed across services, and alerts reach the right channel.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/observability-and-instrumentation"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:cloud",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:opentelemetry",
          "observability"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "addyosmani/agent-skills/skills/constraint-driven-development",
      "name": "constraint-driven-development",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/addyosmani/agent-skills/constraint-driven-development",
        "installs": 40800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [2837, 3875, 3607, 3859, 4861, 5481, 5090, 4612],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-28"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Interviews you for a quality bar, records it in CONSTRAINTS.md with the commands that enforce it, and catches diffs that weaken it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "no quality bar is written down, or an agent keeps skipping tests and silencing checks to get to green"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "detects your stack, asks four questions with defaults, writes CONSTRAINTS.md, installs a standard tool per dimension and places each check by cost"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "CONSTRAINTS.md with a floor, enforced rules that each name their command, measured numbers that mustn't get worse, and dated exceptions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Catches",
              "text": "lowered thresholds, skipped or deleted tests, new @ts-ignore or eslint-disable comments, empty catch blocks, stubs and undiscussed exceptions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Defaults",
              "text": "80% coverage of changed lines, no vulnerabilities rated high or above, LCP of 2.5 seconds or less, no critical or serious accessibility violations, exceptions lasting 90 days"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "its interview only with a person present; in CI or loops it applies just the floor and flags the rest"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Other skills describe good code in prose an agent may forget; this one writes the project's own bar down, with numbers, where any agent and the build can check it. It reads what it can first, stack, test runner, linters, coverage, CI, then asks four questions one at a time, each with a default: which dimensions to enforce beyond the floor (coverage, security, performance, accessibility, architecture), block or warn, target numbers or today's values, and the slowest check you'll tolerate. The floor applies to every project: no new suppression comments, stubs or empty catch blocks, no unexplained skipped tests, no secrets, and no weakening the file itself. Each chosen dimension gets a standard tool, such as Semgrep, gitleaks, osv-scanner, Lighthouse, axe or Stryker, a package script, and a test against a known violation; fast checks run after each edit, slow ones at task end or in CI. Without a target it uses a ratchet: record today's number and refuse to get worse. At review it checks the diff for the five ways agents lower the bar. AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md get a line pointing to it. A gate script and a diff-scoped floor checker ship with the skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/agent-skills/tree/301b02b12bff98e4688e6984af6556802322bbde/skills/constraint-driven-development"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-quality",
          "guardrails",
          "ci"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "software-mansion/argent/packages/skills/skills/argent-react-native-optimization",
