      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4ae2317ddb22b046e26455e4f9c9b3aaaf62212d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Pursues a long-running goal by delegating all work to subagents, keeping memory in a wiki and improving the agents' environment as it goes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a goal that will take many tasks and want one long session to manage it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes the agent a coordinator that hands every task to a fresh background subagent and stays in conversation with you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "a wiki of linked Markdown pages, dated and citing commits, so decisions and lessons outlive the session"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "runs retros on about one in five subagent sessions, turns large work into a spec and tickets, and suggests recurring schedules"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents; retro, to-spec, to-tickets and implement-spec installed beside it, since it reads them by relative path; an issue tracker for large work"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it, then for the rest of the session; it sits in its author's in-progress folder, so expect changes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Matt Pocock's skill for a long-running session. The agent becomes the person responsible for the goal and thinks on two tracks: finishing the task in front of it, and changing the environment so the next task goes better. To protect its context it does no work itself: every task goes to a background subagent with a fresh context, briefed through pointers to notes, commits and wiki pages rather than copied text. Its memory is a wiki, one linked page per decision, problem, subsystem or preference, in a wiki you already keep or a place agreed with you. Pages are dated history: claims about code cite the commit they held at, and a change adds a new claim instead of rewriting the old. It reads the wiki before decisions and suggests a schedule to check it for contradictions. It runs the retro skill on about one in five subagent sessions, worst first, and acts on the findings itself instead of presenting them to you; large work becomes a spec and tickets on your tracker, built with implement-spec. Throughout, it pushes for constrained APIs, lint rules that force correctness, access to logs and test databases, and no workarounds.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/4ae2317ddb22b046e26455e4f9c9b3aaaf62212d/skills/in-progress/chief-of-staff"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:mode",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "memory",
          "agent-workflows",
          "self-improvement"
        ],
        "note": "Runs retro, to-spec, to-tickets and implement-spec by reading their SKILL.md from beside it, so it needs them installed."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "emilkowalski/skills/skills/mobile-native",
      "name": "mobile-native",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/emilkowalski/skills/mobile-native",
        "installs": 113901,
        "weeklyInstalls": [0, 9291, 12906, 13601, 15968, 16291, 18007, 16357],
        "firstSeen": "2026-09-15"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Removes the tells that make a web app feel like a website on a phone, such as sticky hover, tap flashes and input zoom.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a web app or PWA looks fine in desktop Chrome but feels like a website, not an app, on a phone"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "matches what you see to a symptom table and applies the fix, usually a single CSS declaration or meta tag"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the fixes in your code, plus a few lines on what was wrong, what changed and what still needs a real phone to confirm"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike animate-expo",
              "text": "that skill is for React Native apps; this one is for web apps in a mobile browser, and leaves motion design to animate"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Emil Kowalski's checklist for the platform layer of the mobile web: viewport, touch, scroll, safe areas and browser chrome. Each symptom maps to a fix and its reason. Hover styles that stick after a tap go behind a hover-and-fine-pointer media query; the gray tap flash is turned off; 100vh becomes 100dvh for app shells and 100svh for heroes; inputs get 16px text so iOS stops zooming in; taps get touch-action: manipulation and press feedback on :active; overscroll-behavior stops pull-to-refresh; viewport-fit=cover and safe-area insets handle the notch; controls stop selecting their label on long press; carousels claim their swipe axis; and theme-color is set per color scheme. Its hard rules: never disable zoom, detect touch with media queries rather than user agents, write for touch and mouse at once, and never call something fixed from device emulation. It also gives a baseline of meta tags and CSS for a new mobile-facing app and a never-ship table to check before finishing. When it can't test on hardware, it says which fixes it verified from code and which you must check on a phone.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/mobile-native"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "mobile",
          "design"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "emilkowalski/skills/skills/break-ui",
      "name": "break-ui",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/emilkowalski/skills/break-ui",
        "installs": 92872,
        "weeklyInstalls": [0, 0, 0, 5165, 19562, 21800, 18352, 16461],
        "firstSeen": "2026-10-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Stress-tests a component with realistic worst-case data behind a demo/worst-case toggle, then reports every break with its fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a component or screen looks right with demo data and you want to see what real users' data does to it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "builds a realistic worst-case dataset behind a dev-only Demo data / Worst case toggle, then checks it at several widths, 200% zoom, and dark mode or right-to-left if supported"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a table of breaks rated Broken, Ugly or Fragile, each with the value that caused it and a fix, with the toggle left running"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fixes",
              "text": "only when asked: \"fix all\" or \"fix 1, 3\" after the report, or \"fix\" in your first request, which still leaves the design decisions to you"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike emil-design-eng",
              "text": "that skill critiques visual design; this one feeds the UI bad data and records what breaks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An adversarial tester from Emil Kowalski's design skills. It plays the most annoying real user, from a double-barrelled surname and a very long email to an intern called Jo and a 1,284-member workspace. First it lists every value the component renders, including counts, timestamps, badges and avatars, and looks up each one's limit in validation schemas, migrations, API types and form maxLength attributes; a value with no limit is itself a finding. Then it builds one worst-case fixture next to the demo data, spreading failures across the first rows, plus empty, single-item and 1,000-row states. Values must be plausible or schema-backed, never random, and enter where the demo data does, never by editing markup. The toggle is a URL switch or prototype route in a project, or a standalone HTML file. It checks the real container width, 320px and the widest layout, 200% zoom, and dark mode and right-to-left when supported, with screenshots when it can. A table of failure signatures maps breaks such as a squished avatar or \"1 members\" to their usual cause and CSS or Intl fix. The report also lists decisions for you and what held up; after fixes, the fixture stays as a regression test.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/break-ui"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "testing",
          "design"
        ],
        "note": "Leaves a worst-case fixture and a dev-only toggle in your project afterwards, unless you say otherwise."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "emilkowalski/skills/skills/animation-performance",
      "name": "animation-performance",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/emilkowalski/skills/animation-performance",
        "installs": 64873,
        "weeklyInstalls": [0, 0, 0, 0, 1148, 16579, 18512, 16767],
        "firstSeen": "2026-10-11"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds why a web animation drops frames, from the wrong animated property to a busy main thread, and proposes a fix for each.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an animation stutters or drops frames, or you're choosing which property to animate, or CSS versus JavaScript, on performance grounds"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lists every animation with what drives it and what it animates, then works down seven causes ordered by how often each is to blame"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one finding per element with file and line, its cause, before and after code, and whether it was verified from code or needs a device recording"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike animate",
              "text": "which picks easing, duration and whether to animate at all; this one only asks whether each frame finishes in time"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fixes",
              "text": "applied only when you ask; it reports and stops"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Emil Kowalski's frame-budget rules for web motion. At 60fps the browser has 16.7ms per frame, and 8ms on a 120Hz screen, so it designs against the smaller number and treats a fast laptop as proof of nothing. It sorts properties into three costs: composite-only (transform, opacity), paint (box-shadow, border-radius, color) and layout (width, height, margin, top), and swaps down a tier, such as scale() for width or drop-shadow() for box-shadow. It explains which animations can run off the main thread (CSS and the Web Animations API) and which can't (requestAnimationFrame loops, Motion's layout animations), and from which version Motion's x and y shorthands are accelerated. It covers two traps that use transform and still stutter: animating an inherited CSS variable, which recalculates every child, and large blur values. Its diagnosis runs from the animated properties, a busy main thread and React re-rendering every frame, to growing content, CSS variables, blur, and last will-change, added only once a shift or dropped frames are seen. It wants findings confirmed by a DevTools recording on a real mid-range phone, not by feel, marks which it could only check in code, and never writes transition: all.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/emilkowalski/skills/tree/c8a79627b59d72d764d6ab6f2c87c09a48169833/skills/animation-performance"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "animation",
          "performance",
          "debugging"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/git-commit",
      "name": "git-commit",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/github/awesome-copilot/git-commit",
        "installs": 48200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [497, 517, 486, 446, 430, 425, 624, 585],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Commits your changes with Conventional Commits messages read from the diff, one logical change per commit.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want the agent to commit your work, or split it into several commits, with Conventional Commits messages"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads your repo's commit rules, examines the diff, stages one logical change, writes a type(scope): summary message and runs git commit"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "local commits, checked with git log; it never pushes, amends or rewrites history unless you ask"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike caveman-commit",
              "text": "which only writes a message; this one stages the files and makes the commit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Handles",
              "text": "an interrupted merge: it keeps Git's prepared message, stages only safely resolved files and asks you about the rest"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A commit workflow built on the Conventional Commits 1.0.0 spec, with the extra types from commitlint's conventional config: docs, refactor, perf, test, ci, chore and others alongside feat and fix. The repository's own rules come first: a commitlint config, CONTRIBUTING.md and the style of the last ten messages override the skill's defaults. It examines the staged and unstaged diff, then checks for a merge, cherry-pick, revert or rebase in progress and for unmerged files. With none, it unstages anything that belongs to another change and stages this one by path, never with interactive git add -p, and leaves out secrets such as .env files or private keys. During a merge it doesn't unstage or split anything, stages only cleanly resolved text files, asks before adding other edited paths, and stops on a rebase. The message's first line is 72 characters or less, with an imperative summary and no period, an optional body on what changed and why, and footers such as Closes #123; breaking changes get a ! or a BREAKING CHANGE footer. It never changes Git config, doesn't skip hooks with --no-verify unless told, and after a failed hook fixes the problem and commits again rather than amending.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/git-commit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "stack:git",
          "git"
        ]
      }
