              "text": "AWS, GCP or other cloud icons once you download an Excalidraw library and split it with a bundled Python script"
            },
            {
              "label": "Limits",
              "text": "about 20 elements per diagram, no embedded images and no automatic collision detection"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It turns a plain description into a diagram file you can keep editing by hand. The agent first decides the diagram type from your wording, such as \"steps\" for a flowchart or \"database\" for an ER diagram, then pulls out what that type needs: decision points for a flowchart, actors and lanes for a swimlane, cardinality for an ER diagram, lifelines and messages for a sequence diagram. It writes rectangles, ellipses, diamonds, arrows and text with set spacing, a consistent color scheme and the hand-drawn Excalifont on every label, then checks unique IDs, overlaps, readability and valid JSON before delivering. For requests with too many parts it suggests an overview plus detailed sub-diagrams. Cloud architecture diagrams can use real service icons: you download a library from libraries.excalidraw.com, a bundled script splits it into one file per icon, and other scripts place icons and arrows without loading the icon data into the agent's context. Without a library it falls back to labelled shapes.",
