        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits a site for security headers, browser compatibility, deprecated APIs and console errors with Lighthouse, then fixes the code.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a site checked and fixed for the security, compatibility and code-quality problems Lighthouse's Best Practices audit looks for"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs Lighthouse's Best Practices audit, inspects the console and network failures, checks dependencies, headers and config, fixes the code and re-runs the audit"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "fixed code, with security findings kept apart from style preferences; it never presents a high score as proof the app is secure"
            },
            {
              "label": "Works with",
              "text": "Chrome DevTools MCP if available, otherwise the Lighthouse CLI with dependency and header checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "HTTPS and HSTS, CSP, sanitizing HTML, pinned third-party scripts, doctype and charset, deprecated APIs, passive listeners, error handling, source maps, permission prompts"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Part of Addy Osmani's web quality skills. Lighthouse isn't a full security assessment, so it adds dependency, header, configuration and source checks, and verifies headers on the running site, since config files don't prove what's actually sent. A separate security reference, read when security is in scope, covers HSTS, CSP and Trusted Types, Subresource Integrity, cookies and sanitization. The rest is before-and-after code: an HTML5 doctype, charset first in the head, a viewport tag, feature detection over browser sniffing, polyfills bundled at build time rather than loaded from a third-party CDN (it cites the 2024 polyfill.io compromise), replacing document.write and synchronous requests, passive scroll and touch listeners, error boundaries and global error handlers, hidden source maps in production, event delegation and listener cleanup, valid semantic HTML, and asking for permissions such as location only after a user action. It ends with an audit checklist across security, compatibility, code quality and UX, and tools such as npm audit, SecurityHeaders.com, the W3C validator and Mozilla Observatory.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/addyosmani/web-quality-skills/tree/afa8da942115f2961fdbfa80807ea0b232ff6c00/skills/best-practices"
        },
        "tags": [
