      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns data analysis into a story for stakeholders, with narrative structures, presentation templates, annotated charts and headline formulas.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're presenting analytics to executives or investors, writing a data report or business review, or explaining findings to a non-technical audience"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "shapes findings into an arc: a hook, the baseline, the data that builds up, the key insight, recommendations and a call to action"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a report or presentation that leads with the insight and ends with next steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "headlines that state a number and its business impact, transition phrases, and ways to state uncertainty such as ranges and confidence levels"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A guide to presenting data, not to analyzing it. The core is a story structure: set up the context and baseline, name the problem or opportunity, then resolve it with insights and recommendations, carried by three pillars of data, narrative and visuals. Its rules: start with the \"so what\", group in threes, curate instead of dumping data, connect to the audience's goals, avoid jargon, give context before method and end with clear next steps. A separate file holds three story frameworks (problem and solution, trend, comparison), each worked through on an example such as a churn analysis; visualization techniques such as revealing a chart one layer per slide, before-and-after contrasts and annotating a chart in matplotlib; templates for an executive summary slide, a seven-slide deck from the headline to the ask, and a one-page monthly business review; and writing techniques: headlines built from a number, its business impact and context, transition phrases and ways to present uncertainty.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/business-analytics/skills/data-storytelling"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "category:science",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "charts",
          "writing",
          "slides"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/quantitative-trading/skills/backtesting-frameworks",
      "name": "backtesting-frameworks",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/backtesting-frameworks",
        "installs": 17400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [225, 239, 276, 373, 336, 336, 381, 354],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Helps build trading-strategy backtests that avoid look-ahead, survivorship and overfitting bias, using walk-forward testing and realistic trading costs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building a backtester, or checking whether a trading strategy's historical results can be trusted"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "names the biases that inflate backtests (look-ahead, survivorship, overfitting, cherry-picking, ignored trading costs) and how to avoid each"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "backtests that split history into training, validation and test sets and test walk-forward over rolling windows"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "Python for an event-driven and a vectorized backtester, walk-forward optimization, Monte Carlo analysis and performance metrics"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for trading-strategy backtests. Each bias comes with its fix: point-in-time data against look-ahead, delisted securities against survivorship, out-of-sample testing against overfitting, pre-registration against cherry-picking strategies, and realistic cost models for trading costs. History is split three ways: develop on the training set, choose parameters on the validation set without peeking, and touch the test set only for the final estimate; walk-forward analysis repeats that over rolling windows. Its rules: limit parameters, never optimize on the full history, handle adjusted prices with care, use Monte Carlo analysis to understand uncertainty, and account for capacity and market impact. A separate file holds the implementations in Python with pandas and NumPy: an event-driven backtester, a faster vectorized one, walk-forward optimization, Monte Carlo analysis, and a function computing returns, volatility, Sharpe, Sortino and Calmar ratios, maximum drawdown, win rate and profit factor.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/quantitative-trading/skills/backtesting-frameworks"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:science",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:python",
          "finance"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
