      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Adds microinteractions, transitions, loading states and gestures to interfaces, with timing and easing rules and reduced-motion support.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're adding hover and press feedback, loading states, toggles, page transitions or swipe gestures to a UI"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "keeps motion to what communicates: confirming an action, showing where things come from, drawing attention or keeping context"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "components in React with Framer Motion or plain CSS, such as skeleton screens, progress bars, toggles, ripple buttons and swipe-to-dismiss cards"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "honouring reduced-motion settings, animating only transform and opacity, and never blocking input during an animation"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Interaction design principles with code. Motion should give feedback, orientation, focus or continuity, not decoration. A timing table maps durations to uses: 100-150ms for hovers and clicks, 200-300ms for toggles and dropdowns, 300-500ms for modals and page changes, and longer only for choreographed sequences; four easing curves cover entering, exiting, moving between states and a playful overshoot. Examples use React with Framer Motion and Tailwind classes, plus CSS keyframes and transitions. Its rules: animate transform and opacity for 60fps, respect prefers-reduced-motion in CSS and in code, keep one timing scale across the app, prefer springs to linear motion, let users interrupt long animations, work without JavaScript animation, and test on real devices. It names toasts, drag-and-drop and scroll-triggered animations among its uses but shows no code for them. It ends with common problems such as janky animations of width or position, motion fatigue and animation listeners left behind on unmount.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/ui-design/skills/interaction-design"
        },
        "tags": [
