      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies WCAG 2.2 rules to interfaces: semantic HTML, keyboard focus, screen-reader support, color contrast and mobile accessibility.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building or auditing an interface for WCAG 2.2 AA or AAA, screen readers or keyboard-only use"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives the agent rules, such as native elements over ARIA, visible focus, text alternatives and live regions, and the common failures to look for"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "components written to those rules, with React examples of accessible buttons, modal dialogs, forms, skip links and announcements"
            },
            {
              "label": "Test with",
              "text": "automated checks in axe DevTools, WAVE or Lighthouse, and manual ones with VoiceOver, NVDA, JAWS or TalkBack"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike accessibility",
              "text": "from addyosmani/web-quality-skills, which audits the rendered page and fixes what fails, this is a reference with no steps of its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference, not a workflow. Its rules: prefer semantic HTML to ARIA, test with people with disabilities, design for the keyboard first, style focus outlines instead of removing them, give all non-text content a text alternative, keep pages working at 200% zoom, announce dynamic changes with live regions, and honour reduced-motion and contrast preferences. Common failures it names include missing alt text and labels, poor contrast, keyboard traps, auto-playing media, custom controls that rebuild native ones badly, missing skip links and a tab order that doesn't match the screen. A separate file covers WCAG's four principles, ARIA roles, states and live regions, keyboard focus and focus trapping, screen readers, and mobile (44-point touch targets, VoiceOver, TalkBack, Dynamic Type); a checklist of key WCAG 2.2 success criteria; React code for the components; and a contrast-ratio function with the AA and AAA thresholds.",
