    {
      "id": "jimliu/baoyu-skills/skills/baoyu-diagram",
      "name": "baoyu-diagram",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/jimliu/baoyu-skills/baoyu-diagram",
        "installs": 15800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [327, 338, 148, 156, 209, 215, 205, 232],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "ca703c2d7c2c4399a1dc0cf82224f8551e05a96d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Draws architecture, flowchart, sequence and other diagrams as SVG the agent writes itself, in dark and light themes, plus 2x PNGs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a system, process, data flow, state machine or org chart drawn as a clean technical diagram"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "picks a diagram type, plans the layout, writes the SVG with every color as a CSS variable, then runs scripts for the light version and PNGs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "<name>.svg (dark), <name>.light.svg and a 2x PNG of each, in a diagram/ folder"
            },
            {
              "label": "Types",
              "text": "architecture, flowchart, sequence, structural (class, ER, org chart), mind map, timeline, state machine, illustrative and data flow"
            },
            {
              "label": "Themes",
              "text": "dark and light built in; add a JSON file of colors to its themes folder for your own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Bun or npx, and the sharp package installed once for PNGs; without it you get the SVGs only"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike baoyu-infographic",
              "text": "from the same repo, which has an image model make a picture, here the agent writes the SVG markup itself"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It follows a fixed design system: a dark slate background with a grid, monospace type at set sizes, semi-transparent boxes colored by role (frontend, backend, database, cloud, security, queues), and an opaque mask under each box so arrows don't show through. Spacing rules, such as gaps of at least 40px vertically and 30px horizontally, legends outside boundaries and 30px of padding around the content, keep parts from overlapping. Each SVG is one self-contained file that loads no fonts, images or scripts from the network, and Chinese labels get a CJK font and wider boxes. For architecture, flowchart, sequence and structural diagrams it reads a reference for that type before laying out. The PNG script replaces each CSS variable with the theme's color, since its renderer doesn't understand them, and never installs sharp itself. Files go in a diagram/ folder next to the input file, or in diagram/<topic>/ in the project.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jimliu/baoyu-skills/tree/ca703c2d7c2c4399a1dc0cf82224f8551e05a96d/skills/baoyu-diagram"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:science",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:svg",
          "diagrams",
          "architecture"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "expo/skills/plugins/expo/skills/eas-simulator",
      "name": "eas-simulator",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/expo/skills/eas-simulator",
        "installs": 23000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1430, 1468, 1344, 1378, 1288, 1381, 1514, 1554],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-02"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs your Expo app on a cloud iOS simulator or Android emulator from EAS, where the agent taps, screenshots and records it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a simulator you can't run locally, such as iOS on Linux, in CI or in a cloud agent, or want the agent to click through your app"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "starts an EAS Simulator session, installs a build of your app, drives it with agent-device, then stops the session"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "screenshots or screen recordings of your app running, and a browser preview link to watch or drive it yourself"
            },
            {
              "label": "Live edits",
              "text": "need a development build connected to Metro through a tunnel, its default; a local release build or the default EAS simulator build never hot-reloads"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike local simulators",
              "text": "it works from any machine; on a Mac it's only for cloud, shareable or agent-driven sessions, not a plain 'run on the simulator'"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an EAS account with access to this experimental feature, an Expo project, and eas-cli and agent-device, which it runs through npx"
            },
            {
              "label": "Costs",
              "text": "sessions are a paid EAS service: they count toward your plan's limits, and each stops at a set maximum duration"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Expo's skill for EAS Simulator, an experimental, limited-access EAS feature that boots an iOS simulator or Android emulator in the cloud. The agent first checks that your account has access; if not, it says so and falls back to a local simulator or another route. A session is always start, install, drive, stop: eas-cli owns the session and writes its id and token to .env.eas-simulator, which should stay out of git, and agent-device taps, types, reads the accessibility tree, takes screenshots and records video, which it prefers for animations and gestures. The simulator boots blank, so the agent picks how to install your app: by default a development build connected to Metro through a tunnel so edits show live, and a local release build or an EAS build only when you ask for a static screenshot or artifact. Each session gets a short name saying what it's for. The agent stops the sessions it started when the work is done, while one kept for you in the browser preview runs until its maximum duration: 40 minutes on the free plan, 115 on paid ones. On iOS it reads crash reports and the device log when the app crashes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/expo/skills/tree/a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582/plugins/expo/skills/eas-simulator"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:tool",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:account",
          "by:official",
          "stack:expo",
          "stack:agent-device",
          "mobile",
          "testing",
          "verification"
        ],
        "note": "Needs an EAS account with EAS Simulator enabled, a limited-access feature still rolling out. Sessions count toward your plan's limits."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "expo/skills/plugins/expo/skills/expo-web-to-native",
      "name": "expo-web-to-native",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/expo/skills/expo-web-to-native",
        "installs": 22000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1436, 1395, 1296, 1292, 1259, 1377, 1467, 1546],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-07"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Migrates a React web app, from Next.js, Vite or CRA, to a native iOS and Android Expo app, one screen at a time.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to turn an existing React website, such as a Next.js, Vite or Create React App codebase, into an iOS and Android app without a rewrite"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs the whole web UI inside a native shell first, then rebuilds screens natively in order of value, checking each against the running website"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a migration-progress.md worklist, an app you can ship from day one, and screens that move from a webview to native one at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "best as a repeating /goal loop that re-reads the skill each turn; if your agent can't loop, it saves the goal to migration-goal.md for you to launch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike expo-dom",
              "text": "which covers how web code runs inside a native app, it plans and orders the whole migration"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "agent-browser to capture the website and argent to drive the simulator, for the side-by-side checks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Expo's playbook for moving a web React app to native the way a strangler fig replaces a tree: there is no converter, so the app migrates screen by screen and stays shippable throughout. The agent first reads the repo and writes migration-progress.md, separating screens from server code and sorting each screen into port as-is, nativize now, nativize later or hybrid, while flagging browser SDKs and payments, since digital goods must go through the stores' in-app purchase. It scaffolds an Expo app whose routes mirror the web routes, then brings every screen over as a DOM component, web code running in a webview, so the whole app runs on a phone on day one. From there it redesigns the most valuable screens natively, reaching first for @expo/ui's real SwiftUI and Jetpack Compose components, rewires data, auth and storage, and ships through the app stores. Each step hands off to another Expo skill, such as expo-dom, expo-router, expo-data-fetching or eas-app-stores. A screen passes only when the running native app matches the running website in content and behavior, not pixels, with a recording for screens that move.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/expo/skills/tree/a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582/plugins/expo/skills/expo-web-to-native"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:frameworks",
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:browser",
          "by:official",
          "stack:expo",
          "stack:react-native",
          "stack:react",
          "stack:nextjs",
          "migration",
          "mobile",
          "design",
          "verification"
        ],
        "note": "Its checks need agent-browser and Software Mansion's argent CLI; it asks, then installs them if they're missing."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "expo/skills/plugins/expo/skills/expo-design-system",
      "name": "expo-design-system",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/expo/skills/expo-design-system",
        "installs": 19800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [1660, 1673, 1514, 1598, 1602, 1720, 1868, 2007],
        "firstSeen": "2026-08-13"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds or audits a design system in an Expo app: one token theme, reusable components, and fixes for screens that look AI-generated.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an Expo app's screens look inconsistent or generic, or you're setting up theme files and shared components"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "extends the theme or styling library already in the app, or creates one theme of tokens for color, spacing, type, radius, shadow and motion"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one theme entry point, components with variant, size and state props, and a screenshot check of each screen you build"
            },
            {
              "label": "Names",
              "text": "the tells of AI-generated apps, such as The Web Modal and Everything's a Card, with the native alternative for each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike expo-native-ui",
              "text": "which sets what values look native, it decides where values live and how they're reused"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Expo's rules for giving an app one visual source of truth. In an app that already has screens, it adopts before it builds: it looks for a styling library such as NativeWind, Tamagui, Restyle or Unistyles and for a theme file, extends whatever exists in its own idiom, and never adds a second system beside it. Without one, tokens live under src/theme/: platform semantic colors that adapt to dark mode, a small brand set, a 4-point spacing scale, a text ramp that mirrors Apple's text styles, radii, shadow levels and motion durations. Any value used twice becomes a token, and nothing outside the theme hardcodes colors, font sizes or spacing. Shared components define variants, sizes, pressed and loading states, a style override merged last, and accessibility labels; a view moves into src/components/ only once two screens use it, it has a nameable role and its API is smaller than its code. After each screen, the agent screenshots it and checks hierarchy, spacing, repetition and alignment, fixing the shared token rather than the one screen. Its reference files hold a drift audit with grep checks and a scoring rubric, and 20 named tells of AI-generated apps.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/expo/skills/tree/a9e5bd3fa00b4e61f632e43f85da73aee1942582/plugins/expo/skills/expo-design-system"
        },
        "tags": [
