      "skillMd": {
        "version": "797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes software documentation by the Diátaxis framework, as a tutorial, how-to, reference or explanation, after agreeing the audience, goal and outline with you.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a tutorial, a how-to guide, a reference page or an explanation for a piece of software"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "settles the document type, the reader, their goal and the scope with you, then proposes an outline and waits for your approval"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the full document in Markdown, written to the outline you approved"
            },
            {
              "label": "Diátaxis",
              "text": "a documentation framework that splits docs into four kinds: lessons, recipes for one problem, technical descriptions and discussions that explain"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike create-readme",
              "text": "which writes a README straight from the codebase, this one asks first and covers any of the four document types"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Diátaxis sorts documentation by what the reader needs: a tutorial takes a newcomer through practical steps to a first success, a how-to guide solves one specific problem, reference describes the machinery, and explanation discusses a topic so it's understood. The agent works as a technical writer bound to that split. Before writing anything it must pin down the document type, the target audience, what the reader wants to achieve, and what's in and out of scope, asking you whatever is missing. It then proposes a detailed outline, such as a table of contents with a short description of each part, and writes the document only once you approve it. The result is Markdown aimed at clear language, accurate code and details, one user goal per document and consistent terminology. Markdown files you share are read for the project's tone, style and terms but not copied unless you ask, and it consults no outside website unless you give it a link and tell it to.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/documentation-writer"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "docs-writing",
          "writing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/prd",
