      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/github/awesome-copilot/prd",
        "installs": 24400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [243, 203, 221, 240, 172, 201, 175, 175],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Interviews you about a feature's problem, success metrics and constraints, then writes a product requirements document in a fixed five-section structure.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're starting a product or feature, including an AI-powered one, and want its requirements pinned down before building"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks at least two questions first, on the problem, how success is measured and the constraints, then maps the user flow and non-goals"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a PRD with an executive summary, user stories with acceptance criteria, AI requirements if any, technical specs, and risks with a phased roadmap"
            },
            {
              "label": "Insists on",
              "text": "measurable requirements, such as results within 200ms for 10k records, rather than \"fast\" or \"easy\"; a tech stack you didn't name is asked about or marked TBD"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A PRD (product requirements document) is the single source of truth for what a project will and won't build. This skill never writes one without discovery: the agent first asks why this is being built now, how you'll know it worked, and what budget, stack or deadline limits it. It then works out dependencies and hidden complexity, the user flow, and what's explicitly out of scope, and drafts the document in a strict schema. The executive summary states the problem, the proposed solution and three to five measurable KPIs. The user experience section lists personas, user stories in \"As a [user], I want to [action] so that [benefit]\" form, acceptance criteria for each, and non-goals. AI features get a section on the tools and APIs they need and how output quality will be evaluated and tested. Technical specifications cover the architecture, integration points such as APIs, databases and auth, and security and privacy. The last section plans a phased rollout from MVP to v2.0 and names technical risks such as latency, cost or failing dependencies. It presents a draft and asks for feedback on specific sections. A worked example covers an intelligent documentation search.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/prd"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "requirements",
          "planning",
          "interview"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "github/awesome-copilot/skills/refactor",
      "name": "refactor",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/github/awesome-copilot/refactor",
        "installs": 22600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [152, 152, 171, 161, 133, 126, 105, 136],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-23"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Refactors code in small, tested steps without changing what it does, with a catalog of code smells, their fixes and design patterns.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "code is hard to read or change, functions or classes have grown too large, or you ask the agent to clean something up"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "makes sure tests exist, commits and branches, then makes one small change at a time, running the tests and committing after each"
            },
            {
              "label": "Covers",
              "text": "ten code smells, such as long functions, duplication, god classes, magic numbers and nested conditionals, each with a before-and-after fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "restructured code that behaves the same, checked against rules on function size, naming, structure, types and tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike repo-rebuilder",
              "text": "a skill it calls more drastic, this one makes gradual improvements to existing code rather than rebuilding it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Refactoring here means changing how code is organized, never what it does. Its rules: preserve behavior, take small steps, commit at each safe state, have tests (without them you're only editing), and never mix refactoring with feature work. It also says when not to refactor: stable code that won't change again, critical code without tests until tests are added, a tight deadline, or change for its own sake. The process has five stages: prepare with tests, a commit and a feature branch; find the smell and plan the change; refactor one small change at a time, with tests and a commit after each; verify that tests pass and performance hasn't dropped; then update comments and docs. Before-and-after examples, mostly in TypeScript and JavaScript, cover long functions, duplicated code, large classes, long parameter lists, feature envy (a method that uses another object's data more than its own), primitive obsession, magic numbers, nested conditionals, dead code and classes that reach into each other's internals, plus extracting methods, adding types, and the Strategy and Chain of Responsibility patterns. A final checklist asks for functions under 50 lines, no duplication or magic values, one-way dependencies, typed public APIs and passing tests.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/refactor"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:official",
          "refactoring",
          "code-quality"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
