      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Merges the lessons you approve from a domain's memory file into its instruction file, without losing detail, then removes them from the memory file.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a domain's file of remembered lessons has grown and you want the mature ones folded into that domain's standing instructions"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads <domain>-memory.instructions.md and <domain>.instructions.md, proposes which memories go where, waits for your go, then drafts and refines the merge"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a new or updated instruction file, with both files' applyTo patterns combined, and a memory file with the merged lessons removed"
            },
            {
              "label": "Call it as",
              "text": "/memory-merger >domain, optionally with a scope: global (VS Code's user prompts folder, the default) or workspace (.github/instructions/)"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the repo's remember skill installed too: it writes the <domain>-memory.instructions.md files this one merges"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Memory files hold lessons learned, one per heading, next to the instruction files an agent loads for a domain such as clojure or git-workflow; applyTo patterns in their front matter say which files they apply to. This skill consolidates the two. It works out the paths from the domain and scope, globbing the folder for a near match if you mistyped the domain, and reads both files; the instruction file may not exist yet. It lists each memory with its key points and where it would go, then stops until you approve all with \"go\" or name the ones to skip. It drafts the merged instructions without touching any file, judging them against three goals: no detail or example lost, overlapping guidance consolidated, and a clear, scannable structure, and refines until they meet all three. Only then does it write the instruction file, adding front matter if the file is new, and remove the merged sections from the memory file. It tracks its progress in the agent's todo list.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/github/awesome-copilot/tree/797337f33f73e35d08d82f840e21c4b8c4932d88/skills/memory-merger"
        },
