          "text": "A reference for the step after threat modeling. It sorts security requirements into functional (what the system must do), non-functional (how well it must do it) and constraints (limits such as approved crypto libraries), and gives each one a link to the threat or compliance rule behind it, a way to test it, a priority and a risk level. Its templates, written in Python and kept in a separate file, model requirements and sets of them, turn STRIDE-labelled threats into requirement patterns (spoofing becomes authentication and session rules, tampering becomes input validation and integrity checks), set priority from a threat's impact times its likelihood, map requirements to PCI DSS, HIPAA, GDPR and OWASP controls with a traceability matrix and a gap analysis, and write security user stories and epics with acceptance criteria and a definition of done. The rules: trace every requirement to a threat, make it specific enough to test, define when it's done, map to compliance early, and never write \"be secure\" as a requirement.",
