        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes and maintains Architecture Decision Records, with templates, a numbered docs/adr index, a status lifecycle and a review checklist.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're making or recording a significant technical decision, such as a database or framework choice, or setting up a decision process for a team"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "says when an ADR is worth writing, how one moves from proposed to accepted, deprecated or superseded, and how to keep a numbered index in docs/adr"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "ADRs from five templates: standard (MADR), lightweight, a one-sentence Y-statement, deprecation with a migration plan, and RFC style"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "adr-tools commands, and a review checklist for before submission, during review and after acceptance"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An Architecture Decision Record, or ADR, explains why a technical decision was made: its context, the decision and its consequences. Write one for a new framework, a database, an API pattern or a security architecture; skip it for bug fixes, minor upgrades and configuration changes. ADRs live as numbered files in docs/adr, with a README index of title, status and date. An accepted ADR is never edited: a new one supersedes it, and rejected ones stay as a record. Before drafting, the agent reads the matching template: a full record in the MADR format with decision drivers, considered options, pros, cons and risks; a lightweight one; a Y-statement (in the context of, facing, we decided for and against, to achieve, accepting that); a deprecation with a migration plan and lessons learned; or an RFC with detailed design, drawbacks, alternatives and open questions. It also lists adr-tools commands to create, supersede and link records. Keep each ADR to one or two pages and be honest about trade-offs.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/documentation-generation/skills/architecture-decision-records"
        },
        "tags": [
