    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/python-development/skills/python-design-patterns",
      "name": "python-design-patterns",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/python-design-patterns",
        "installs": 23600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [433, 454, 444, 522, 460, 425, 379, 377],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies design principles such as KISS, single responsibility, composition over inheritance and the rule of three when writing or refactoring Python.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're designing a Python service or component, splitting a class that does too much, or deciding whether a new abstraction is worth it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives the agent design principles with Python examples: keep it simple, one reason to change per unit, separate layers, compose rather than inherit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule of three",
              "text": "don't abstract until a third similar case appears, unless the copies already diverge in ways that cause bugs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "code shaped by those rules; it adds no steps or files of its own"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A short reference on structure rather than syntax, with Python examples. Keep it simple: a dictionary beats a factory with registration. Give each unit one reason to change, and separate HTTP handling, business logic and data access into handler, service and repository layers whose imports point one way. Build behavior by composing objects passed in through the constructor, so tests can swap in fakes. Wait for three similar cases before abstracting, since duplication is often cheaper than the wrong abstraction. Keep functions to roughly 20-50 lines with one purpose. Its troubleshooting covers a class that seems to have several responsibilities (list every reason it could change), constructors with seven or more parameters (split the class), composition nested too deep, and a service layer importing from the API layer. Anti-patterns include returning ORM models from an API instead of response schemas, and SQL inside business logic.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/python-development/skills/python-design-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:python",
          "code-quality",
          "refactoring",
          "architecture"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/error-handling-patterns",
      "name": "error-handling-patterns",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/error-handling-patterns",
        "installs": 23500,
        "weeklyInstalls": [297, 317, 349, 395, 368, 308, 302, 330],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies error handling patterns, such as custom error hierarchies, Result types, retries, circuit breakers and graceful degradation, in Python, TypeScript, Rust and Go.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're adding error handling to a feature, designing how an API reports failures, or making a service survive failing dependencies"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "explains when to throw exceptions, return Result types or crash, and which errors are recoverable, with idioms for Python, TypeScript, Rust and Go"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "code shaped by those rules; it adds no steps or files of its own"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "retries with exponential backoff, circuit breakers, collecting several errors at once, fallbacks, and pitfalls like empty catch blocks or logging and re-throwing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference that spans languages. Exceptions are for unexpected conditions, Result types for expected failures such as validation errors, and crashes for bugs nothing can recover from. Network timeouts, missing files, bad input and rate limits are recoverable; running out of memory and null-pointer bugs are not. For each language it shows the native way: a custom exception hierarchy, context managers for cleanup and retry with backoff in Python; custom error classes, a Result type and async handling in TypeScript; Result and Option with the ? operator in Rust; returned and wrapped errors and custom error types in Go. Patterns for any language are a circuit breaker, error aggregation and graceful degradation through fallbacks. The rules: fail fast, keep context such as stack traces and metadata, write messages that say how to fix the problem, catch only where you can handle the error, always clean up resources, and never swallow an error silently.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/error-handling-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:python",
          "stack:typescript",
          "stack:rust",
          "stack:go",
          "code-quality",
          "backend"
        ]
      }
