        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds threats to a system with STRIDE, one category at a time, and writes them up as a prioritized threat model.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're starting a threat model, reviewing a system's architecture or design decisions for security, or preparing for an audit"
            },
            {
              "label": "STRIDE means",
              "text": "six threat categories, each tied to a property: Spoofing (authentication), Tampering (integrity), Repudiation (audit logs), Information disclosure (confidentiality), Denial of service (availability), Elevation of privilege (authorization)"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a threat model document: system overview, data flow diagram, trust boundaries, assets, threats per category with mitigations, a risk matrix and prioritized recommendations"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also has",
              "text": "Python templates that model threats and apply STRIDE to a data flow diagram and to each interaction between components"
            }
          ],
          "text": "STRIDE is a checklist for finding threats: for each part of a system, ask whether an attacker could pretend to be someone else, modify data, deny having done something, read data they shouldn't, disrupt availability or gain higher privileges. This skill gives the agent that matrix, each question paired with the family of controls that answers it, and a template for the write-up. The threat model describes the system, draws its data flow, marks trust boundaries, lists assets by sensitivity, tables threats per category with impact, likelihood and mitigations, scores them on a 4-by-4 impact and likelihood matrix, and ends with immediate, 30-day and 90-day recommendations. Python templates model threats, assets and trust boundaries, analyze a data flow diagram, and apply STRIDE per interaction between components. The rules: cover every category, prioritize realistically, involve security, development and operations, keep the model updated, and follow through with mitigations.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/security-scanning/skills/stride-analysis-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
