        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Debugs systematically: reproduce the bug, gather evidence, form a hypothesis, then test it, with patterns for flaky, performance and production bugs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're chasing a bug, an intermittent failure, a performance problem or a production issue, in any language or stack"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "works in four phases: reproduce and shrink the bug, gather errors, environment and recent changes, form a hypothesis, then test it by bisecting, logging or isolating parts"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a root cause narrowed down by evidence rather than guesses, with notes on what you tried"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "flaky bugs (race conditions, timing), performance (profile before optimizing), safe production debugging, and a checklist of usual suspects such as typos, off-by-ones and stale caches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Tools file",
              "text": "debuggers and profilers for JavaScript, Python and Go, git bisect, comparing working and broken setups, call tracing and memory-leak checks"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A general debugging method for any codebase. It frames debugging as the scientific method: observe, hypothesize, experiment, analyze, repeat. First reproduce the bug and cut it down to a minimal case, then collect the full stack trace, versions, environment variables and recent code, dependency or configuration changes. Hypotheses come from asking what changed, what differs between working and broken, and where it could fail. Testing means binary search (comment out half the code), strategic logging, isolating components with mocks, and diffing working against broken setups. Before choosing tools or bisecting, the agent reads a second file with debugger and profiler examples for JavaScript and TypeScript, Python (pdb, cProfile) and Go (Delve, pprof), git bisect, a working-versus-broken table, a tracing decorator and heap-snapshot leak checks. Separate patterns cover intermittent bugs, performance issues and production bugs, where it says not to change production and to test fixes in staging first. It ends with common mistakes, such as changing several things at once or giving up too soon, and a checklist for when you're stuck.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/developer-essentials/skills/debugging-strategies"
