      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies microservice architecture patterns: service boundaries, sync and event-driven communication, database per service, sagas, circuit breakers and retries.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're splitting a monolith into services, drawing service boundaries, or designing how services talk to each other and survive each other's failures"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives the agent patterns for cutting services, calling them directly or through events, giving each its own data, and staying up when one fails"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "designs along those patterns; it adds no steps or files of its own, and a reference file holds Python examples"
            },
            {
              "label": "Terms",
              "text": "a saga is a chain of local transactions with compensating undo steps; a circuit breaker stops calling a failing service for a while"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference, not a workflow. It names three ways to cut services: by business capability (orders, payments, inventory), by subdomain, where each bounded context from domain-driven design becomes a service, and the strangler fig pattern, which moves features out of a monolith gradually behind a proxy. Services talk synchronously over REST, gRPC or GraphQL, or asynchronously through Kafka, RabbitMQ or SQS. Each service owns its database, and transactions that span services become sagas with eventual consistency. Resilience comes from circuit breakers, retries with exponential backoff and bulkheads, which isolate resources so one failure stays contained. The reference file shows each in Python: services that publish events, a FastAPI gateway that combines several services' data behind circuit breakers and tolerates partial failures, an HTTP client with retries and timeouts, an event bus on Kafka, an order-fulfillment saga that runs compensations when a step fails, and a circuit breaker class with closed, open and half-open states.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/backend-development/skills/microservices-patterns"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "architecture",
          "backend"
        ]
      }
    },
