              "text": "HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault and Google Secret Manager, in a few lines each"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference of copyable snippets rather than a workflow. After a short comparison of four secret stores, it starts a Vault dev server and stores a secret, then reads Vault secrets from GitHub Actions, with HashiCorp's vault-action, and from GitLab CI, with the vault CLI. For AWS it creates a secret with the CLI, reads it in GitHub Actions while masking it in the logs, and passes it to a Terraform database resource. It covers GitHub repository and environment secrets, GitLab's protected, masked and file variables, and the External Secrets Operator, which copies Vault secrets into Kubernetes secrets on a schedule. Rotation comes as an AWS Lambda function plus five manual steps: generate, store, update the apps, verify, revoke the old secret. Secret scanning runs TruffleHog in a pre-commit hook and as a CI job. Ten best practices round it off, among them never committing secrets, separate secrets per environment, least privilege, audit logs, masking and short-lived tokens. It points to github-actions-templates, gitlab-ci-patterns and deployment-pipeline-design for the rest of the pipeline.",
