            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Wireshark or tshark, tcpdump, mitmproxy, Scapy or boofuzz installed, depending on the step"
            },
            {
              "label": "Sends traffic",
              "text": "its fuzzing and replay steps send crafted or modified packets to a live target, and mitmproxy intercepts TLS traffic"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Commands and a seven-step method for working out a protocol from its traffic: capture several sessions, find where messages start and end, map the fixed header and variable payload, identify fields by comparing samples, write a specification, validate it by implementing a parser or generator, then test edge cases with fuzzing. Capture uses Wireshark, tshark (with ring buffers), tcpdump, or mitmproxy and Burp Suite for HTTP and HTTPS. Analysis uses Wireshark display filters and stream following, tshark field extraction and statistics, and Scapy scripts. It lists byte signatures for common protocols, such as HTTP, TLS, SSH, SMB and Redis, and a typical header layout: magic number, version, flags, length, message type, sequence number. Before binary or encryption work, the agent reads a file on length-prefixed and type-length-value structures, a Python parser, hex dumps, entropy checks that hint at encryption, JA3 TLS fingerprints and decrypting TLS with key logs. Before documenting, it reads a specification template and a Lua dissector example. Active testing defines messages for the boofuzz fuzzer and replays or modifies captured packets with Scapy.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/reverse-engineering/skills/protocol-reverse-engineering"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:security",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:wireshark",
          "security",
          "debugging"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "wshobson/agents/plugins/llm-application-dev/skills/llm-evaluation",
      "name": "llm-evaluation",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/wshobson/agents/llm-evaluation",
        "installs": 13200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [263, 277, 280, 359, 310, 259, 256, 248],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds evaluation suites for LLM applications: automatic metrics, LLM-as-judge scoring, human ratings, A/B tests and regression checks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need to know whether a prompt, model or pipeline change made your LLM app better or worse, or want a baseline before shipping"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lays out three kinds of evaluation: computed metrics, human ratings, and LLM-as-judge, where a stronger model scores or compares answers"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "Python for a suite that scores test cases on several metrics; its reference file adds judge prompts, rater guidelines, significance tests and a regression detector"
            },
            {
              "label": "Metrics",
              "text": "BLEU and ROUGE for word overlap, BERTScore for meaning, accuracy and F1 for classification, and MRR, NDCG and precision or recall at K for retrieval"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A reference for testing LLM applications, not one tool's manual. Automated metrics are fast and repeatable: overlap scores for translations and summaries, embedding similarity, classification scores, and ranking scores for retrieval in RAG systems. Human evaluation rates accuracy, coherence, relevance, fluency, safety and helpfulness. LLM-as-judge comes pointwise (score one answer) or pairwise (pick the better of two), with or without a reference answer. The quick start is an EvaluationSuite that runs a model over test cases and averages each metric. Its reference file implements BLEU, ROUGE and BERTScore; custom checks for groundedness and factuality with NLI models, which judge whether a source supports a claim, and for toxicity with Detoxify; Claude judge prompts for single, pairwise and reference-based scoring; annotation guidelines for human raters; Cohen's kappa for agreement between raters; A/B tests with statistical significance and effect size; a detector that flags metrics more than 5% below a baseline; a LangSmith evaluation example; and a benchmark runner.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/llm-application-dev/skills/llm-evaluation"
        },
        "tags": [
