      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds 3–5 year startup financial models: cohort-based revenue, costs, hiring, cash flow, runway and three scenarios for fundraising.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're an early-stage founder projecting revenue, working out burn and runway, or preparing financials for a seed or Series A raise"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "walks through nine steps: business model, cohort revenue, costs, hiring plan, cash flow, key metrics, three scenarios, sanity checks and funding rounds"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a model with monthly detail for two years, quarterly for the third and annual for years four and five, in conservative, base and optimistic versions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Benchmarks",
              "text": "typical ratios for SaaS, marketplace, e-commerce and services businesses, such as 75–85% SaaS gross margin and LTV/CAC above 3"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A method for startup financial projections that names no spreadsheet tool. Revenue is built by cohort: each month's new customers, times the share who stay, times average revenue per user. Costs split into cost of goods sold, sales and marketing, R&D, and general and administrative, and into fixed and variable. The hiring plan uses fully loaded pay, salary times 1.3–1.4 for benefits and taxes. Cash flow gives monthly burn and runway, and the key metrics include CAC, LTV, payback period, burn multiple, magic number and the Rule of 40. Three scenarios, conservative, base and optimistic, vary acquisition, churn, contract value and CAC by set percentages. It has templates for SaaS, marketplace, e-commerce and agency models, dilution and use-of-funds math, with enough runway for the next milestone plus six months, pitfalls such as optimistic revenue and ignoring cash timing, and sanity checks against benchmarks. The detailed steps sit in a reference file, moved there to keep the SKILL.md under Codex's 8 KB limit.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/wshobson/agents/tree/4bde3168b43c0655c4d00ea2726e631719908a6d/plugins/startup-business-analyst/skills/startup-financial-modeling"
        },
