    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/pr-walkthrough",
      "name": "pr-walkthrough",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/pr-walkthrough",
        "installs": 28300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [845, 643, 588, 930, 493, 370, 374, 116],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-14"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a static, interactive D3 site that walks a reviewer through a pull request in four views, each with a guided tour.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you have a pull request's branch checked out and want a map of what it touches before reading the diff"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads the PR, its diff, changed specs, review comments and the surrounding code, then draws a system overview plus data-flow, dependency and user-action graphs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": ".warp/pr-walkthrough/index.html: one self-contained page that opens from a file:// URL, with zoom, search, keyboard shortcuts and links into the PR's diff"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike a review",
              "text": "it adds no findings or verdict; anything it notices is framed as an area to inspect"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the GitHub CLI, gh, and Playwright with Chromium for its bundled validator; the page loads D3 7.9.0 from jsDelivr"
            },
            {
              "label": "Looks like",
              "text": "Warp's brand: dark surfaces, pink accents and Matter fonts, from Warp's hosted guide through the brandalf skill, or built-in fallback values"
            }
          ],
          "text": "An experiment in reading a PR on a canvas instead of a diff. The agent finds the current branch's PR with gh and collects the diff, description, changed specs, and human and agent review comments, then reads the full changed files and their neighbours so explanations rest on the real code, not just the hunks. It builds four views a reviewer toggles between: a system overview of cards that explains the touched subsystem without mentioning the PR, a data-flow graph, a code dependency graph and a user-action graph, each graph with directed, labelled edges, and every view with a guided tour. Size follows the PR: a tiny change gets two or three nodes per view. Screenshots, mocks and Figma exports are saved into the site's assets rather than hotlinked, and each changed file links to its anchor in the PR's Files changed tab. Bundled Python scripts, which its commands call at .agents/skills/pr-walkthrough/scripts/ in your repo, generate the page and check it in a browser; it isn't called ready until that passes, and without a browser it's reported as unverified. On request it publishes the folder to Cloudflare Pages with wrangler.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/pr-walkthrough"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:browser",
          "by:community",
          "stack:github",
          "stack:d3",
          "code-review",
          "diagrams"
        ],
        "note": "Styled in Warp's brand. Publishing to a public Cloudflare Pages URL happens only when you ask, and needs a Cloudflare account."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/council",
      "name": "council",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/council",
        "installs": 28000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [875, 658, 614, 974, 544, 395, 395, 158],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Sends one decision to several subagents on different models and angles, then synthesizes their reports into a single recommendation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want second opinions on a judgment call: an architecture tradeoff, a risky fix, a rollout decision, or whether an alternative is worth pursuing"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "gives each subagent a model and an angle, such as Opus on correctness, GPT on testability and an open-source model as contrarian, and keeps them independent"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a short decision memo: recommendation, why, tradeoffs and risks, and a final call, weighed by evidence rather than votes"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an agent that can launch subagents on different models; it's written for Warp's run_agents tool, one call per model"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A council is for questions where judgment matters more than lookup. The agent states the decision in one sentence, with the options, the code or artifact to inspect, the decision criteria and whether members may edit code. Model diversity comes first: the default roster is the strongest Claude model, the strongest GPT or Codex model and an open-source model such as Kimi or GLM, with any substitution noted; each also gets its own angle, like red-team or implementation cost. On an explicit request it tells you whom it will launch and waits for your approval. Read-only members share the checkout; members who prototype get their own git worktree and branch. Each reports the evidence inspected, current behavior, the alternative, risks, cost, a recommendation and confidence, and a thin report gets a follow-up question to the same member rather than a replacement. The final answer leads with the recommendation, notes consensus and disagreement, and separates what to do now from later hardening.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/council"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "architecture",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/check-impl-against-spec",
      "name": "check-impl-against-spec",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/check-impl-against-spec",
        "installs": 27100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [845, 639, 584, 929, 495, 363, 361, 107],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks a pull request's code against its spec context during an automated review and adds material mismatches to review.json.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an automated review with review-pr finds spec_context.md, the PR's spec context: product behavior, tech details or both"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lists the spec's commitments, compares them with the diff and checked-out code, and flags only material drift: missing behavior, contradictions, significant unplanned scope, skipped validation or migration steps"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "spec findings folded into review.json: broad drift in the summary, inline comments only on changed lines, each at least an important concern"
            },
            {
              "label": "Doesn't",
              "text": "write its own report, post to GitHub, or flag harmless differences in naming, structure or technique"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A supplement to review-pr, not a separate review. It runs only when spec_context.md exists, alongside pr_diff.txt and, when present, the PR description. From the spec it extracts the required behaviors, the files or subsystems it said to change, its constraints, and any required validation, migration or follow-up steps, then checks the diff and the checked-out branch for each. Small implementation choices that keep the spec's intent are accepted: it doesn't demand a one-to-one match or speculate about details the spec doesn't state. Material drift goes into the same review.json that review-pr writes, in the summary or as inline comments tied to changed lines, and nothing is added just to say the code matches.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/check-impl-against-spec"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "code-review",
          "verification",
          "ci"
        ],
        "note": "Works only inside review-pr's review, when spec_context.md exists: it adds to the review.json that skill writes."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/reproduce-bug-report",
      "name": "reproduce-bug-report",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/reproduce-bug-report",
        "installs": 26900,
        "weeklyInstalls": [843, 640, 582, 924, 493, 361, 357, 108],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-20"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Launches Warp's Oz cloud agents with computer use to reproduce a UI bug report and bring back a screen recording and findings.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a GitHub issue, Linear ticket, support report or prompt describes a visible bug: rendering, layout, settings, onboarding or another interaction"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "extracts the reported and expected behavior, steps and environment, then sends one cloud agent, or several with distinct hypotheses, to run the app and try it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "one summary: confirmed, partially confirmed, not reproduced or blocked, what each variant tested, exact steps, environment and versions, and a screen recording with screenshots"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "Oz cloud agents with computer use, launched through Warp's run_agents tool"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "secrets and account details out of recordings and reports; agents don't sign in or post anywhere unless authorized, except the recording to a Slack thread they're given"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The parent agent doesn't try the bug itself unless you ask; it briefs cloud agents that can run the app and record the screen. It first decides whether the skill fits: purely backend, CI or build problems are out of scope, and a report that needs credentials or private state is reported as blocked rather than guessed at. One agent follows the report's steps exactly; others test variants such as a different OS, a fresh versus existing setup, or a stable versus dev build, and when steps are missing one traces UI strings and settings in the code to choose a likely path. Each installs the version closest to the reporter's and records any fallback, saves a recording, numbered screenshots and a manifest to its own directory, and tries at most two targeted variations if the bug doesn't appear. Given a Slack thread, it posts the recording back there, and to no other channel. A repository can add a reproduce-bug-report-local skill for its own setup. The parent waits for every agent, then summarizes what was tested, the evidence, the findings and one next step.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/reproduce-bug-report"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:account",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:official",
          "stack:oz",
          "debugging",
          "testing",
          "computer-use",
          "multi-agent"
        ],
        "note": "Needs Warp's Oz cloud agents with computer use, launched through Warp's run_agents tool."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/respond-to-pr-comments-in-blocklist",
      "name": "respond-to-pr-comments-in-blocklist",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/respond-to-pr-comments-in-blocklist",
        "installs": 25300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [841, 636, 578, 922, 488, 357, 354, 104],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-30"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Walks you through a PR's review comments one by one, makes the fixes you pick, then posts replies and resolves threads after you approve.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want to answer a PR's review comments and post the replies back to GitHub, not just read them or fix code"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "skips bot overviews and threads you already answered, then asks per comment: apply the recommended fix, explain it, acknowledge without changes, or something else"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the fixes made and checked, an optional commit and push, then replies posted with gh and approved threads resolved"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "one by one, fixing as you go, or collect every decision first and apply the fixes in a batch"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the GitHub CLI, gh, and an ask_user_question tool for its multiple-choice prompts, as in Warp's agent"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Written for Warp's agent, which supplies the ask_user_question and insert_code_review_comments tools it calls. It starts from comments already shown in the conversation, typically by the /pr-comments skill, or fetches and displays them first with gh. Automated status comments from Warp, Oz or code-review bots, resolved threads and threads whose latest reply is yours are skipped; when unsure, a comment stays in. For each one the agent restates the author, location and gist with a path:line reference, inspects the code when the fix isn't obvious, and asks what to do, always with an Other option for your own approach; choosing explain gets you context and the question again. It keeps a decision record with a draft reply per comment, changes only what the comments ask, preserves unrelated work, runs the narrowest useful checks and reviews the diff. It asks before committing and pushing, so reviewers see the code before the replies. Nothing reaches GitHub until you approve a preview grouped by comment; replies go through the REST API and threads are resolved through GraphQL.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/respond-to-pr-comments-in-blocklist"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "stack:github",
          "code-review",
          "git"
        ],
        "note": "Replies it posts start with [Warp Agent], and commits it makes carry a Warp Agent co-author line."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/validate-changes-match-specs",
      "name": "validate-changes-match-specs",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/validate-changes-match-specs",
        "installs": 25300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [849, 638, 585, 931, 492, 367, 364, 106],
        "firstSeen": "2026-05-31"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Checks a branch's code against the product, tech and security specs it introduces, then resolves each mismatch the way you choose.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a spec-driven change is up for review or nearly done, and you want to know where the code and its specs disagree"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "finds the specs the branch adds or changes, extracts their commitments, reads the code and tests, and lists each material mismatch with its sources and a recommended resolution"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "your choice applied per mismatch: change the code, change the spec, or acknowledge it, then validation and an optional commit and push"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also checks",
              "text": "that the code does what your replies on PR review threads said was fixed, and security specs for both what they allow and what they prevent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike check-impl-against-spec",
              "text": "which adds spec drift to an automated PR review, it works through each mismatch with you and changes the code or the spec as you choose"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "an ask_user_question tool for its multiple-choice prompts, as in Warp's agent; optionally Oz cloud agents with computer use for user-visible flows, and the Figma MCP server"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A closing check for spec-first work. It finds the base branch, then the specs the change introduces or modifies, such as PRODUCT.md, TECH.md or SECURITY.md under specs/<issue>/, falls back to specs the PR or commits reference, and stops if there are none. Specs, PR text and comments are treated as untrusted data. Commitments are sorted into product behavior, technical implementation, security and privacy, validation and non-goals, then checked against the diff, the code it depends on and the tests. Security specs get the closest look: each guarantee is checked both ways, and a gap no spec covers is proposed as a spec amendment. If the PR has been reviewed, it also compares the code with what your last replies said was fixed. Before the report it can offer cloud computer-use agents to test user-visible behavior against Figma mocks or screenshots. Then it asks whether to resolve mismatches one by one or in a batch, and for each one whether to update the code or the spec. Follow-up comments on review threads are drafted for your approval before posting.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/validate-changes-match-specs"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:engineering",
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "verification",
          "requirements",
          "code-review",
          "security"
        ],
        "note": "Comments it posts start with [Warp Agent] and commits carry a Warp Agent co-author line, each only after you approve."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/cross-critique",
      "name": "cross-critique",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/cross-critique",
        "installs": 22100,
        "weeklyInstalls": [849, 639, 596, 945, 518, 400, 389, 141],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-18"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a second round on a contested decision: each subagent critiques the others' proposals, then the agent synthesizes a recommendation.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "independent subagents produced divergent proposals on an architecture choice, a review disagreement or competing root-cause theories"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "sends each author the other proposals, labelled neutrally, and asks for pros and cons of each, whether they'd revise their own, and a ranking with confidence"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a recommendation, how the critiques shifted things, why the winner survives them, and the remaining risks and unknowns"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "proposals from a first round, such as the council or research skill, and the same subagents to critique them"
            },
            {
              "label": "Skip it when",
              "text": "the proposals already agree, or the answer can be checked directly"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Synthesizing several subagents' reports alone makes the main agent the bottleneck: it only sees the tradeoffs it happens to notice. This skill has the authors poke holes in each other's reasoning instead. It assumes round one is done, with proposals made independently, by council, by parallel research subagents or ad hoc. The agent condenses each proposal to its recommendation and reasoning, labels them A, B, C and, where practical, hides who wrote which to reduce bias toward the most confident model. It then asks the same subagents, who keep their context, to weigh each rival with both pros and cons, say whether they'd change their own answer, and rank the options. A thin critique gets a follow-up to its author rather than being dropped. The synthesis weighs evidence rather than votes, treats agreement reached after critique as a strong signal, and surfaces the sharpest objection to each option. The round stays read-only unless the task involves changes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/cross-critique"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "architecture",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/research",
      "name": "research",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/research",
        "installs": 22000,
        "weeklyInstalls": [849, 644, 586, 924, 498, 365, 362, 113],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-18"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Delegates noisy codebase investigation to subagents and works from their distilled answer, keeping the main agent's context clean.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an answer means reading many files, long logs or a big diff: how X works, where Y is used, why Z fails"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "briefs one local, read-only subagent, or several in parallel for independent sub-questions, on the exact question, where to look and what to return"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a direct answer, the key file paths and symbols as evidence, and any caveats, without the noise in the main context"
            },
            {
              "label": "Skip it when",
              "text": "you'd read two or three known files, run one grep, or need the raw files to edit next"
            },
            {
              "label": "Models",
              "text": "names gpt-6-luna-medium for simple searches and gpt-6-luna-xhigh for involved ones"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Context is the main agent's scarcest resource, and reading twenty files to find the three that matter fills it with noise. This skill hands the digging to a subagent and keeps only the conclusion. The test is whether the noise avoided outweighs a subagent's latency and tokens: good fits are a failing test's root cause, how a class is used across a repo, or a summary of a 4,000-line PR. Subagents always run locally, even under a cloud agent, and use a search model rather than the parent's. One is the default; several run in parallel only when the question splits into independent parts. The brief states the question, where to look, that the work is read-only, and that it should return a distilled answer with file:line evidence rather than a transcript. A bloated report gets a follow-up to the same subagent. Afterwards the agent works from the result and reads files itself only when it needs to edit them.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/research"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "token-saving",
          "agent-workflows",
          "research"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/saga",
      "name": "saga",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/saga",
        "installs": 20800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [839, 636, 582, 922, 491, 362, 356, 114],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-24"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Runs a large feature end to end: plans an airtight spec tree with you, then has worker subagents build and validate each task.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a medium-to-large feature should be built mostly autonomously, or you're resuming a saga from its directory"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks multiple-choice questions until no requirement is open, splits the work into milestones and tasks with validation criteria, then delegates tasks to parallel workers"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the feature built on saga branches and integrated milestone by milestone, plus a spec tree and PROGRESS.md log under ~/.sagas/, outside the repo"
            },
            {
              "label": "You step in",
              "text": "to approve the spec, on blockers the spec can't settle, and for final acceptance"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "subagents, through Warp's run_agents tool, with a git worktree for each local worker, and an ask_user_question tool for its multiple-choice questions"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The bet behind a saga is that if every task's validation criteria are tight enough to be a contract, workers can run in parallel and check themselves. Phase 1 is planning with you: the agent creates a saga directory under ~/.sagas/, works out what kind of program it is, whether computer use is available and which build and test commands run, then asks multiple-choice questions until scope, edge cases and the acceptance bar are settled. It writes saga-level exit criteria, then SAGA.md, a MILESTONE.md per milestone and a file per task, and waits for your approval. Phase 2 delegates: the orchestrator writes no feature code, launching each milestone's independent tasks as one batch, each local worker in its own worktree on a branch named saga/<name>/m<M>t<T>-<slug>. Workers validate in a loop, commit or hand off a patch before removing their worktree, and report compactly; PROGRESS.md records run IDs and status so a fresh agent can resume. Phase 3 checks the exit criteria with the strongest method available and asks you to accept or report issues, which become new tasks.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/saga"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "category:engineering",
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "multi-agent",
          "planning",
          "implementation",
          "verification",
          "agent-workflows"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "warpdotdev/common-skills/.agents/skills/readout",
      "name": "readout",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/warpdotdev/common-skills/readout",
        "installs": 17700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [838, 637, 577, 922, 486, 358, 356, 106],
        "firstSeen": "2026-07-08"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Writes a polished, self-contained HTML document that explains something or captures a conversation's findings, saved under ~/.readouts with an index.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you say \"write this up\" mid-investigation, or want a readable explainer such as how a server processes webhook events"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks two to four scoping questions with concrete options, then hands a short brief to one local child agent that mines the conversation or researches the code"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "~/.readouts/<date>-<topic>.html built from a shared template, with linked and embedded code references, plus a regenerated index.html of every readout"
            },
            {
              "label": "Modes",
              "text": "snapshot, from what the conversation already found; research, starting fresh from the codebase"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "as a side task: the main agent launches the child and carries on, then relays the file path"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A readout is meant to be read weeks later by someone with none of the original context. The main agent's part is small: sharpen the scope, always settling depth and audience but skipping questions when the request is already specific, then write a brief of 10 to 20 lines with pointers rather than pasted findings: the questions to answer, what to cover and exclude, repo paths, and each repo's URL and commit for code links. One child agent, launched locally through Warp's run_agents tool so the file lands on your machine, does the writing. In snapshot mode it searches the parent conversation by run ID and verifies file references; in research mode it traces the real code paths and grounds claims in file:line references, separating verified from inferred. It follows the skill's doc guide and HTML template, embeds snippets and refreshes the index with bundled Python scripts, and opens the file unless the machine is headless. Without subagents, the main agent writes the document itself.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/warpdotdev/common-skills/tree/8792394ff1b655e0af451b8fabb68ef0cfcbd208/.agents/skills/readout"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:subagents",
          "by:community",
          "docs-writing",
          "research"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/health",
      "name": "health",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/health",
        "installs": 16300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [261, 298, 278, 178, 397, 376, 219, 359],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Audits your Claude Code, Codex or Pi setup and the project's AI maintainability, and reports each finding with a copy-pasteable fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "Claude Code, Codex or Pi seems to ignore instructions, or hooks, MCP servers or settings don't work; not for app bugs or PR review"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs its bundled collector scripts read-only and checks instruction files, permissions, hooks, MCP servers, skills, memory and whether important outcomes have an executable check"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a health report: passing checks, then Critical, Structural and Incremental findings, each with its evidence and an exact command or edit"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "to reporting unless you ask for repairs; a plain audit runs no project tests, builds or installers"
            },
            {
              "label": "Deep audit",
              "text": "on request or when you name a problem like config drift or code rot; it warns first, scans every past session of the project, and splits analysis across inspector subagents"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Health checks two things in one report. Agent config: whether the instruction files each runtime actually loads agree with each other, whether Codex trusts the project, whether hook and MCP executables exist, and whether settings deny credential directories and pipe-to-shell installers where the runtime can enforce it. It treats memory and third-party skills as a supply chain too, flagging secrets in memory, skills and plugins not pinned to a release, and MCP servers trusted by wildcard. AI maintainability: whether non-obvious project rules reach the agent when relevant, whether important outcomes have an executable check where they can actually break, and whether docs or verifier output point at stale paths. Its summary pass also scans up to three recent sessions of the project in Claude and Codex, unless you ask only about instructions and config; a deep audit streams all of them and splits the analysis across up to three inspector subagents, falling back to local analysis if one fails. Findings in global config are labelled global and reported once, and secrets only ever appear as [REDACTED]. Like the rest of Waza, its replies start with 🥷.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/health"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "category:debugging",
          "category:security",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:claude",
          "stack:codex",
          "stack:pi",
          "agent-workflows",
          "security",
          "guardrails",
          "code-quality",
          "verification"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/check",
      "name": "check",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/check",
        "installs": 16300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [260, 341, 265, 175, 414, 365, 221, 377],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Reviews a diff or PR for drift, over-design and hard stops, then handles approved commits, pushes, releases and issue follow-up.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a diff or PR is ready for review, or you want to commit, push, release, or triage issues and PRs"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "checks the diff against your stated goal, flags removable parts and must-fix risks at a depth set by size and risk, then runs the project's own checks"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "findings grouped by bug class with file:line, then a sign-off block saying exactly what was committed, pushed, released or blocked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "reviews read-only: it edits, commits, pushes, publishes or comments only with your go-ahead, and asks before stashing or switching branches"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "a plain code review, it carries release and issue follow-through to the end and closes ship runs with a ledger of what is done, not applicable or remaining"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Check first reads git status, treats every uncommitted file as your work, and takes the baseline from your words: local changes, a branch, everything since the last release. It sizes the review as quick, standard or deep by lines and files, and any diff touching CI, release or signing, auth, billing, installers or deletion paths needs a human maintainer's review. Every review asks whether each changed file traces to the stated goal, lists layers that could be removed, and, when a fix covers one instance of a bug, greps for its siblings. A finding needs a file:line, a trigger and a severity a senior reviewer would raise; a clean review is a valid result. Standard and deep reviews add specialist reviewers, and deep ones an adversarial pass. Other modes load their own reference files: triaging issues and PRs, whether a release is worth it, ship and release follow-through, and a whole-project audit scorecard. Handed a plan from think, it skips the review, implements it and runs the project's checks; a \"ship if green\" rule in AGENTS.md lets it ship after a clean review unasked. Any claim that tests pass must come from a command run this session.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/check"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "category:engineering",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:issue-tracker",
          "by:community",
          "stack:git",
          "stack:github",
          "code-review",
          "verification",
          "scope-control",
          "git",
          "issue-tracking"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/write",
      "name": "write",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/write",
        "installs": 15800,
        "weeklyInstalls": [297, 385, 263, 187, 447, 371, 206, 347],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Rewrites Chinese or English prose to remove AI tone while keeping the author's meaning and voice, with modes for release notes, replies and tweets.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a draft in Chinese or English reads as AI-written, or you need release notes, a maintainer reply or a social post"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "cuts AI tells such as repetition, summary tone and em dashes, keeping the meaning, structure and the author's own voice"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the edited text, or a scoped diff for files in a repo; your own finished draft gets light fixes plus a list of every changed sentence"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also runs",
              "text": "a bundled punctuation checker for the output language before returning any text"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "phrase-by-phrase rewording, it mostly cuts, and counts over-editing as a failure as bad as under-editing"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Write treats its rules as a catalog of AI smells, not a checklist. It reads the text first, asks about the audience only when it can't be inferred, and loads a Chinese or English reference, plus bilingual and localization guides when copy spans languages. The default is a line-level rewrite in place: no reordered sections, no deleted links or images without saying why, and no invented anecdotes when writing as you. Banned-phrase lists are examples, so a flagged word that reads naturally stays. Modes cover release notes, public issue or PR replies, installation steps and error text, long drafts that need structural work (proposed first), comparing two versions of a text, document review with a privacy scan, paragraph coherence and social posts. When asked to write rather than edit, it counts the material it actually has before choosing a length, and researches, asks or writes shorter instead of padding. It never outputs em or en dashes. Code comments and commit messages are out of scope.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/write"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:writing",
          "category:marketing",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "anti-slop",
          "writing",
          "docs-writing",
          "copywriting"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/think",
      "name": "think",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/think",
        "installs": 15200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [261, 303, 270, 179, 419, 262, 170, 187],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Turns a rough idea into an approved, decision-complete plan another engineer or agent can implement, writing no code before you approve.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're about to build something new and want the approach settled first, or need to judge whether it's worth building"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads the repo, its rules and official solutions, then recommends one approach beside a minimal option and names its most fragile assumption"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "an approved design summary, or a handoff plan another agent can execute without re-deciding anything"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "to no code, scaffolding or pseudo-code until you approve; \"just do it\" then lets it implement without another round"
            },
            {
              "label": "Unlike",
              "text": "an interview skill such as grill-me, it leads with its own position and says what evidence would change it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Think turns a rough idea into a plan concrete enough to execute. Before proposing, it reads AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md and the one rule that matches, skims past decisions, lifts live config values instead of quoting defaults, and checks for a built-in or official solution; for a hard problem it studies how two or three mature projects solve it. It then gives one recommended approach with effort, risk and what it builds on, always with a minimal option, and prefers forms that ask no setup of users. Plans that touch external services, high load or data migration are attacked for dependency failure, 10x scale and rollback cost. A simplicity gate makes every added layer, retry or config knob justify itself against the brute-force version. Approved plans have no TBDs, and each phase must be shippable on its own. A defined problem gets a lightweight mode: one fix in two or three sentences, with its files and one risk. Value or keep-or-remove questions, and batches of requests, load their own reference files, and bug reports go to hunt.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/think"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "planning",
          "architecture",
          "handoff",
          "scope-control"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/hunt",
      "name": "hunt",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/hunt",
        "installs": 15200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [254, 293, 265, 176, 265, 229, 156, 173],
        "firstSeen": "2026-04-04"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Finds and proves a bug's root cause before any fix, then checks the repo for the same bug pattern and verifies the fix.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "something errors, crashes, regresses or used to work"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "touches no code until it can state the root cause in one sentence with evidence, and runs the probe that would disprove it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "the root cause and fix with file:line, a sweep for the same bug elsewhere, the evidence, and a regression test for bugs that came back"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "\"diagnose\" or \"why\" requests report-only; it fixes only when you ask, and a request to fix never covers committing or pushing"
            },
            {
              "label": "Stops",
              "text": "after three failed hypotheses, with a handoff of what was checked, ruled out and still unknown"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Hunt is Waza's debugging skill. A hypothesis has to name a file, function, line or condition and explain every symptom, including ones the reporter waved off; \"I'll just try this\" counts as having none. Regressions get their own mode: read the diff from the last good version first, or git bisect in a temporary worktree so uncommitted work stays untouched. Another mode treats a known-good screenshot or build as the reference to diff against, and after a fix it greps the repo for the same bug pattern and records each match as same bug, safe or unsure. Before calling a bug fixed it climbs an evidence ladder from source trace and repro to logs, tests and, for UI or visual bugs, a real runtime check. Rules cover logs that each answer a yes-or-no question, measuring performance before and after, and stopping after three rounds of tuning a magic number. A bug that came back isn't done until its regression test has run red on the old code and green on the fix. Native-app freezes, rendering bugs and IME issues load their own reference files.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/tw93/waza/tree/58217546cd94fcf5ddcde4265c00a9377ed3192c/skills/hunt"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:debugging",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "debugging",
          "verification",
          "testing"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "tw93/waza/skills/read",
      "name": "read",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/tw93/waza/read",
        "installs": 14400,
        "weeklyInstalls": [289, 339, 225, 178, 262, 238, 260, 265],
