      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies Anthony Fu's conventions and tool choices to JavaScript and TypeScript projects, from code layout and ESLint to pnpm catalogs and commit checks.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you start or maintain a JavaScript or TypeScript app or library and want it set up the way Anthony Fu sets up his own"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies his code rules and tool picks: ni commands, @antfu/eslint-config with antislop on, Knip, tsdown, simple-git-hooks and named pnpm catalogs"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "new projects that mirror his starter-ts template, and in every project one ci script that lints, type-checks, runs Knip, builds and tests"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule of thumb",
              "text": "run nr lint --fix and nr ci before every commit, and fix the root cause instead of silencing rules"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Anthony Fu's opinionated tooling and conventions for JavaScript and TypeScript projects. When scaffolding, the agent copies the layout of his starter-ts template rather than inventing one. Code rules: one focused purpose per file, types in types.ts and constants in constants.ts, runtime-agnostic code where possible and an @env comment where not, explicit return types, explicit imports with framework auto-imports turned off in new projects, no new path aliases, and comments that explain why rather than how. Tests sit beside the file as foo.test.ts, using describe and it, with snapshots for complex output. Tooling: ni to install and run scripts, fast-npm-meta to look up a package's latest version cheaply, @antfu/eslint-config with antislop on to flag redundant AI-style code, Knip for unused files, exports and dependencies, a pre-commit hook that runs the whole ci gate, skills-npm to link agent skills shipped inside npm packages, npm trusted publishing instead of tokens, and pnpm catalogs named prod, inlined, dev and frontend. References cover the ESLint config, project setup, app and library development, and monorepos.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/antfu/skills/tree/f63e9ec7bc85f67364b6596c2d29da87e83ce445/skills/antfu"
        },
