        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds a Resend email inbox your AI agent can act on, with security checks that keep untrusted senders from controlling it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an AI agent or app should receive email and act on it, such as a support handler or an email-to-task pipeline"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "asks for an address to send tests to and a security level, then sets up the domain, a Resend inbox and a webhook endpoint, and replies inside the thread"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "webhook code for Next.js or Express that verifies Resend's signature, rejects unknown senders and passes the rest to your agent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Security levels",
              "text": "five, from a strict sender allowlist, the recommended one, to a human approving untrusted actions"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "a Resend account with a full-access API key, ideally a new account just for the agent"
            },
            {
              "label": "Fallback",
              "text": "with no custom domain, or no inbox left on your plan, it receives on a resend.app address, with no threads"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Resend's skill for giving an agent its own email address. Its premise: email is untrusted input, so an agent that acts on it can be steered by anyone who writes to it. The agent asks for your email address to send tests to, and whether to use a new Resend account, which it prefers, since the full-access key it needs reaches every project. Then it asks which of five security levels you want: an allowlist of senders, an allowlist of domains, content filtering, sandboxed processing with restricted agent capabilities, or human approval for untrusted actions. You verify a domain on Resend, preferably a subdomain, receiving mail through an MX record or by forwarding. The agent creates the inbox and a POST webhook that checks Resend's signature on the raw body, then fetches the email, since the webhook carries no body. It registers the webhook through the API, reaches localhost through Tailscale Funnel or ngrok, and replies inside the thread. Its common mistakes include comparing the whole From header instead of the address, trusting Reply-To, returning an error for rejected mail so Resend retries, and using a sending-only API key. It also says never to paste the key in chat.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/resend/resend-skills/tree/389de56a2e48903c4b8ac7b8f2b8ee1f2c1bb9df/skills/agent-email-inbox"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:agents",
          "category:security",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:remote",
          "needs:account",
          "by:official",
          "stack:resend",
          "email",
          "security",
          "guardrails",
          "agent-development"
        ],
        "note": "Needs a Resend account with a full-access API key, and a custom domain for the inbox itself."
      }
    },
