      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Drives your Chrome through the OpenCLI command line to read and act on pages, including logged-in sites, capture API calls and manage tabs.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a one-off task needs a real web page, including a site you're signed in to"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lists a page's interactive elements, then clicks, types, fills, uploads, extracts, screenshots or runs JavaScript, one opencli command at a time"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also does",
              "text": "captures the page's network requests, finds likely API calls in its scripts, and manages native Chrome tabs, windows and tab groups"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the opencli CLI connected to Chrome; opencli doctor diagnoses the connection"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not for",
              "text": "reusable site commands, which OpenCLI's opencli-adapter-author skill writes"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The OpenCLI project's skill for browsing with an agent. Page work happens in a named session, so separate tasks don't touch each other's tabs, and a profile flag picks the Chrome profile. The state command returns the URL and the page's interactive elements with refs, and actions target a ref, a CSS selector or flags such as role, name, label, text or test ID. fill replaces a field and checks its value, eval runs JavaScript in the page and can change it, wait holds for a request, selector, text or download, and frames and tabs are covered too. bind attaches the tab you already have open: the agent can navigate it but can't open, switch or close tabs. For data, network lists captured requests with their response shapes, and a static mode scans loaded scripts for candidate API calls without running them, which it treats as unproven. Native Chrome calls such as tabs.query or tabGroups.update need no session, and the tabs and windows they create stay open. Every command returns JSON with ok and data, or an error with a code and a hint; when a write's outcome is unknown, the agent checks the page before retrying.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/jackwener/opencli/tree/36bbbd7f60bd361a96e62e417a28ea09d6c2266b/skills/opencli-browser"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:browser",
