          "effect:remote",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:browser",
          "by:official",
          "stack:opencli",
          "browser-automation",
          "scraping"
        ],
        "note": "Works in your own Chrome, so it can act on the sites you're signed in to."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ibelick/ui-skills/skills/fixing-motion-performance",
      "name": "fixing-motion-performance",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/ibelick/ui-skills/fixing-motion-performance",
        "installs": 28300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [520, 688, 661, 561, 588, 845, 1317, 827],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies rules that keep UI animations from janking, such as defaulting to transform and opacity, or reviews a file against them.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "animations stutter or transitions jank, or you're adding scroll-linked motion, blur or animated layout"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies nine groups of rules in priority order, from never interleaving layout reads and writes to staying inside your animation library"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "animation work within the rules, or for a file, a report quoting each violation with why it matters and a suggested code-level fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Won't",
              "text": "switch or rewrite your animation library unless asked; it works within CSS, WAAPI, Motion, requestAnimationFrame or GSAP"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The skill sorts what an animation changes by the rendering work it causes: composite (transform and opacity), paint (color, borders, gradients, masks, images, filters) and layout (size, position, flow, grid and flex). It keeps motion on the least expensive work that fits, defaulting to transform and opacity. Its rules: never interleave layout reads and writes in one frame, drive animation from scroll events, or run a requestAnimationFrame loop without a stop condition; measure once, then animate with transform, as in FLIP (measure where an element starts and ends, then animate the difference); use Scroll or View Timelines and IntersectionObserver instead of polling scroll position, and pause animations off-screen; animate paint only on small, isolated elements, and never animate inherited CSS variables; use will-change only briefly; keep blur at 8px or less and never animate it continuously; save view transitions for navigation. It prefers a cheaper technique over removing motion. Called alone, it applies the rules to animation work for the rest of the conversation; called with a file, it reviews that file and reports what to change. For any non-default choice it states the constraint that justifies it, such as surface size, duration or interaction.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/ibelick/ui-skills/tree/00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98/skills/fixing-motion-performance"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "animation",
          "performance",
          "code-review"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ibelick/ui-skills/skills/baseline-ui",
      "name": "baseline-ui",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/ibelick/ui-skills/baseline-ui",
        "installs": 25700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [559, 736, 666, 584, 530, 804, 1337, 855],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Holds UI code to an opinionated baseline of stack, animation, typography and design rules against AI slop, or reviews a file against it.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "an agent's UI code looks generic and you want firm rules for it, or a file checked against them"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies MUST, SHOULD and NEVER rules on stack, components, interaction, animation, typography, layout, performance and visual design"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "UI work that follows the rules for the rest of the conversation, or a review quoting each violation with a suggested code-level fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Assumes",
              "text": "Tailwind CSS defaults, motion/react, the cn helper and accessible primitives such as Base UI, React Aria or Radix"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Ibelick's baseline against AI slop, the generic look of model-written interfaces. A sample of its rules: no animation unless asked, only transform and opacity animated, and no more than 200ms for interaction feedback; h-dvh instead of h-screen, and safe-area insets for fixed elements; text-balance for headings, text-pretty for body text and tabular-nums for data; a fixed z-index scale; no gradients unless asked and never purple or multicolor ones, no glow as the main affordance, one accent color per view; an AlertDialog for destructive actions, errors shown next to the action, and paste never blocked; aria-label on icon-only buttons and no hand-built keyboard or focus behavior; no useEffect for what render logic can do. It uses the project's existing components and color tokens first. Called alone, it applies the rules to all UI work in the conversation; called with a file, it lists each violation with the exact line, one sentence on why it matters and a suggested code-level fix.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/ibelick/ui-skills/tree/00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98/skills/baseline-ui"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "stack:tailwind",
          "stack:react",
          "design",
          "anti-slop",
          "animation",
          "accessibility",
          "code-review"
        ],
        "note": "Assumes Tailwind CSS, motion/react for JavaScript animation and the cn helper built on clsx and tailwind-merge."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ibelick/ui-skills/skills/fixing-accessibility",
      "name": "fixing-accessibility",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/ibelick/ui-skills/fixing-accessibility",
        "installs": 23300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [394, 497, 482, 419, 418, 657, 1224, 763],
        "firstSeen": "2026-01-22"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies accessibility rules to UI work, from accessible names and keyboard access to focus traps and form errors, or reviews a file against them.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you add buttons, links, forms, dialogs, menus or keyboard shortcuts, or want a file checked for accessibility"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "applies rules in priority order: accessible names, keyboard access, focus and dialogs, semantics, forms and errors, announcements, contrast, media"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "UI work that follows the rules, or a review quoting each violation with why it matters and a proposed small fix"
            },
            {
              "label": "Rule of thumb",
              "text": "native HTML before ARIA, and targeted fixes rather than rewriting large parts of the UI"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The accessibility rules of ibelick's UI skills, in priority order. Every control needs an accessible name, icon-only buttons an aria-label and decorative icons aria-hidden; nothing clickable is a div without full keyboard support, focus stays visible and tabindex never goes above 0. Dialogs trap focus, set it inside on open, return it to the trigger on close and close on Escape. Native elements come before roles, headings don't skip levels, and lists and tables use their proper elements; complex widgets such as menus, dialogs and comboboxes use established accessible primitives. Form errors are tied to their fields with aria-describedby, invalid fields set aria-invalid and disabled submit buttons explain why. Critical errors use aria-live, toasts never carry critical information alone, hover-only interactions get keyboard equivalents, text and icons get sufficient contrast, disabled states don't rely on color alone, images get correct alt text, and non-essential motion respects reduced-motion settings. It won't migrate UI libraries or refactor unrelated code. Called alone, it applies the rules for the rest of the conversation; called with a file, it reviews that file and proposes small fixes.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/ibelick/ui-skills/tree/00db7d656719c399c091f3ea138c6f42a7fa4d98/skills/fixing-accessibility"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:guidelines",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "accessibility",
