              "text": "it sets three designers' views against each other, such as Jakub and Jhey first for a kids' app, instead of one set of rules"
            }
          ],
          "text": "It works in two modes picked from your request: Create builds or improves a component's motion, Audit reviews what's there. Both start by reading the project, its CLAUDE.md, package.json and existing animations, to work out what kind of product it is and propose which designer leads. Every animation passes a frequency gate first: rare moments can be expressive, interactions used hundreds of times a day get little or no motion, and keyboard-triggered ones are never animated. Durations follow the weighting: under 300ms when Emil leads, 200 to 500ms for Jakub's polish, whatever serves the effect for Jhey. Create mode takes its code from a bundled motion cookbook, checks it against a list of mistakes Claude makes when writing motion, and explains its choices. Audit mode also hunts for motion gaps, UI that appears or swaps with no transition, and flags AI-slop patterns such as pulsing indicators, hover-scale on everything and stagger spam. It writes a self-contained HTML report to motion-audits/ in your project, opens it in your browser and prints a three-line summary; ask for terminal mode to get the full report inline instead.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/kylezantos/design-motion-principles/tree/4a9ca879f24a361f4dca4174fe2da0f67b5ddee3/skills/design-motion-principles"
        },
