      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds believable fake product UI in React, as stills or animated cursor demos for marketing pages, then films them to check the result.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you need a hero image, feature card or walkthrough animation that looks like a real app without being a screenshot"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "plans the story, copies the closest of its gold examples, restyles it as your product and crops it; animations get a scripted 10 to 16 second story"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a React component built from its copy-in kit, a redesigned phone-width version, and a REHEARSAL.md noting what was and wasn't checked"
            },
            {
              "label": "Checks",
              "text": "films the loop in headless Chrome into contact sheets and won't hand over until its gate reports \"rehearsal passed\""
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "a React project, Node.js, and playwright-core with a local Chrome for filming"
            },
            {
              "label": "Not",
              "text": "browser automation: the cursor's clicks are drawn, they don't operate a real app"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Stageshow makes miniature product UI: a believable interface, framed for where it will sit, as one still or a short animation in which one thing happens. It names five ways these fail: too much detail, a bare wireframe, a whole window shrunk into a box, everything at the same weight, and a bland gray window that ignores the brief. The agent writes the plan first: still or loop, where it sits and at what size, the focal object, the action and the payoff. It maps the product's colors and type to the kit's tokens and picks a fidelity, from gray bars only to real content throughout. It copies the closest gold example rather than starting fresh, replaces its look and words with your product's, and crops to the panel where the story happens. Motion is scripted with the kit's Act API in three to five beats, with a poster frame that tells the story on its own. Each round it films at full and phone width, fixes the largest visible fault first and compares against the gold. Studies of Figma, Linear, Slack and other real products are there to borrow interactions, not identities.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/benjitaylor/skills/tree/299140fafd08df55a9c234e610c3f64af3f45531/stageshow"
        },
