      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Pentests a live web app end to end with Strix, exploiting auth, access-control, injection and business-logic flaws and proving each with a PoC.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a black-box (or source-assisted) pentest of a running web app or staging site"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "runs Strix agents against the URL with your test credentials and scope, exploiting and proving each finding"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a report and per-finding PoCs, strongest on multi-tenant IDOR and broken access control"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Strix CLI or cloud; two test accounts and credentials sharply improve results"
            }
          ],
          "text": "The web-app-specific Strix workflow. It first pins down authorization and scope (the target is yours, prefer staging since agents send real payloads and change data, out-of-scope paths, and test credentials), because most real bugs live behind login and cross-account access can only be proven with two accounts. Then it runs a headless scan against the URL, passing credentials and focus areas by instruction, optionally adding the repo as a second target for white-box depth, and can reach a localhost dev server from the sandbox. It reads the report and per-finding PoCs, re-running each exploit to confirm it, and warns that a budget-capped run exiting clean is not full coverage. Setup and all flags live in the penetration-testing skill; hand findings to the fix skill and gate PRs with the CI skill.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/usestrix/strix/tree/06015506625a78b981ae0b34be40239fc1b389be/skills/web-app-penetration-testing"
        },
