        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Mines your denied agent plans for recurring feedback and writes an HTML report with corrective instructions it can feed into future planning sessions.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you've rejected many agent plans and want to see what you keep asking for, so agents stop repeating the same mistakes"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "reads every denied plan in your Plannotator archive with parallel subagents, then sorts your feedback into categories with real counts and quotes"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a versioned HTML report in Plannotator's data folder, ending with numbered planning instructions and the share of denials they would address"
            },
            {
              "label": "Optional hook",
              "text": "saves those instructions to a file Plannotator's plan-mode hook injects into every future planning session"
            },
            {
              "label": "Without Plannotator",
              "text": "uses the reasons you gave when rejecting plans in Claude Code, parsed from its transcripts by a bundled script"
            },
            {
              "label": "Runs",
              "text": "only when you call it"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A map-reduce analysis of your plan reviews. It counts approved and denied plans, their date range and your revision rate, and offers an incremental run when an earlier report exists. Past ten files, Haiku subagents extract each denied plan's topic, feedback, specific asks and recurring phrases, in batches sized to the archive; Sonnet subagents then reduce those into a taxonomy of 8 to 15 denial reasons, the top patterns, your recurring phrases, what you value, what agents keep getting wrong, the plan structure you expect, and how that changed over time. The report has seven editorial sections, from a narrative headline finding to a dark panel holding the corrective prompt with a working copy button, and opens in your browser. Every number and quote must come from your files, and thin data is called preliminary. Afterwards it offers the hook; if one exists, you can replace it, merge the two sets or keep the old one.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/backnotprop/plannotator/tree/a35cc47b50e1b42d3538638cb85f237da83660ce/apps/skills/extra/plannotator-compound"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:planning",
          "category:agents",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:subagents",
          "needs:claude-code",
          "by:official",
          "stack:plannotator",
          "planning",
          "self-improvement"
        ],
        "note": "Reads every denied plan in your archive, launching parallel Haiku and Sonnet subagents on large ones."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/game-feel",
      "name": "game-feel",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/game-feel",
        "installs": 13300,
        "weeklyInstalls": [523, 517, 587, 593, 749, 1098, 2091, 2445],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Adds juice to game actions, such as screen shake, hit-stop, eased tweens, squash and stretch and layered feedback, scaled to each event's importance.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "a hit, jump, pickup or death works but feels weak, weightless or unsatisfying"
            },
            {
              "label": "Juice means",
              "text": "the brief, exaggerated responses that make an action feel good, such as a flash, a shake or a freeze; they always return to rest"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "hooks feedback onto game events, picking from sound, particles, shake, hit-stop, flashes, knockback, tweens and number pops, then playtests it"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "changes to your game code, with small, medium and large feedback presets assigned to events"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code examples",
              "text": "Godot 4.7 and Unity 6.3; the techniques themselves are engine-neutral"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection. Its premise: one satisfying hit is five to eight tiny responses firing within about 100 ms. The workflow checks the mechanic emits events such as on_hit or on_land, starts with two or three feedback channels per event, eases motion instead of moving it linearly, and keeps shake and hit-stop (a split-second freeze) for real impacts. Feedback stays off the simulation: shake moves the camera, never the body, and hit-stop slows time with a real-time timer so the game always resumes. Patterns include shake driven by a decaying trauma value, a hit-stop that survives overlapping hits, and a squash-and-pop tween with overshoot. It warns against over-juicing routine actions and suggests an option to reduce shake and flashing. It polishes a mechanic but doesn't build one: jump physics, camera follow and sound mixing are left to other skills in the repo.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/game-feel"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "animation",
          "design"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/skills/disciplines/game-ui-ux",
      "name": "game-ui-ux",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/game-ui-ux",
        "installs": 13200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [552, 535, 583, 597, 727, 1049, 2020, 2427],
        "firstSeen": "2026-06-27"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Builds game HUDs and menus that scale to any screen and work with a gamepad: anchored layouts, safe areas, focus navigation and event-driven updates.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're building a HUD or a main, pause or settings menu, or fixing game UI that breaks at other resolutions or on a controller"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "lays UI out with anchors and containers, picks a scaling policy, insets for notches and TV overscan, sets gamepad focus and stacks screens"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "UI code, checked by resizing the window, switching aspect ratios and navigating by gamepad alone"
            },
            {
              "label": "Code examples",
              "text": "Godot 4.7, and Unity 6.3 with uGUI and UI Toolkit; the patterns themselves are engine-neutral"
            },
            {
              "label": "Leaves to others",
              "text": "widget styling, button juice and dialogue UI, to engine UI skills and the repo's game-feel and dialogue-systems"
            }
          ],
          "text": "A discipline skill from a game-development collection: it owns UI architecture and leaves concrete widgets to engine skills. Its seven steps: anchors and containers instead of absolute pixel positions; a reference resolution that scales, plus a rule for the extra width of ultrawide screens and phones; safe-area insets for notches, rounded corners and overscan; an initially focused control and defined focus neighbours on every screen, so a gamepad never lands on nothing; screens as a stack you push and pop, so pause and back are trivial; a HUD that updates on events such as health_changed instead of reading game state every frame; and checks across resolutions and input devices, reporting what was observed. Its pitfalls cover tiny fixed fonts, citing the minimum text sizes of Xbox Accessibility Guideline 101, strings that overflow buttons once translated, and Unity menus that ignore the gamepad after switching to the Input System.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/gamedev-skills/awesome-gamedev-agent-skills/tree/19d93f97c9c1ea0f8b306e77aa81e0d3e352d3e9/skills/disciplines/game-ui-ux"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:design",
          "type:workflow",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:none",
          "by:community",
          "design",
          "accessibility"
        ]
      }
    },
