      "id": "hypit-ai/hypit/skills/hypit",
      "name": "hypit",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/hypit-ai/hypit/hypit",
        "installs": 24700,
        "weeklyInstalls": [12, 6119, 3301, 1829, 1881, 2769, 3479, 3176],
        "firstSeen": "2026-09-10"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "2d31e88765e297f0795348b460c77a6067f12261",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Directs and produces videos with Hypit: studies references, generates images, footage and voices, and composes them with captions and motion graphics.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you want a video made: a remake of a reference video, a short drama, a creator-led social clip, or one from a brief"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "studies a reference's frames alongside its speech, writes a brief and a creative treatment, directs image, video and voice models, then composes the video"
            },
            {
              "label": "You get",
              "text": "a finished video, watched and checked by the agent, plus the editable production in Hypit's Studio for timestamped comments"
            },
            {
              "label": "Keeps",
              "text": "its understanding in project files, the brief, treatment, analysis and timeline, so another session can pick the work up"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the Hypit executable, plus HypiHub or your own model API keys for generation, which the skill doesn't include"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Hypit's own skill. The agent acts as director and producer, making creative choices and explaining them as the work develops. Hypit treats a video as one timeline and one canvas whose objects change over time; where speech leads, events are tied to words in the script rather than fixed times, so a new wording moves the picture with its meaning. A-roll is the performance carrying a passage; B-roll, captions, MG (motion graphics) and effects are composed around it, and new visual systems are written as project components in Hypit's own formats (SVML, SVS and SVRun). Before writing an image, video or voice prompt, the agent must read Hypit's direction guide for it, which records how the models respond. It brings you into choices about your goal, services and spending, and explains what is running during long generations. The SKILL.md ends with a map to dozens of reference files, from caption styling and sound mixing to downloading references with yt-dlp.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/hypit-ai/hypit/tree/2d31e88765e297f0795348b460c77a6067f12261/skills/hypit"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:media",
          "type:tool",
          "effect:local",
          "needs:cli",
          "needs:account",
          "by:official",
          "stack:hypit",
          "video",
          "ai-generation",
          "audio"
        ],
        "note": "Generation needs HypiHub or your own model API keys, which cost money; the skill agrees accounts and a budget with you before paid work."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "garrytan/gstack",
      "name": "gstack",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/garrytan/gstack/gstack",
        "installs": 24600,
        "weeklyInstalls": [328, 277, 236, 252, 229, 244, 261, 329],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-12"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "688e3ab9d131bd4f9d7b173d81ed15c299d3387a",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Routes your request to the right gstack skill, such as office-hours, review, qa or ship, or answers directly when none fits.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you've installed Garry Tan's gstack suite and want each request sent to the gstack skill that fits it"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "matches your request against routing rules for dozens of gstack skills, from plan reviews and QA to shipping, security audits and retros"
            },
            {
              "label": "By default",
              "text": "auto-runs only office-hours, review, qa and ship; otherwise it answers directly, unless you name a skill"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bugs",
              "text": "makes one read-only check of the cause first, then fixes a narrow bug or hands it to investigate"
            },
            {
              "label": "Needs",
              "text": "the full gstack install with its setup script; without it, it falls back to safe defaults and tells you to run setup"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also",
              "text": "logs telemetry and session learnings through gstack's scripts, and asks once for privacy consent"
            }
          ],
          "text": "gstack is a large set of role-based skills, and this SKILL.md is only its router. Its rules cover new ideas (office-hours), specs and tickets, plan reviews from CEO, engineering, design and developer-experience angles, bug investigation, QA, code review, shipping and deploys, docs, retros, security audits, PDFs, scraping and iOS testing. A setting called proactive decides how far it goes: off suggests nothing, core, the default, auto-runs four skills, and all invokes whichever skill matches. Browser requests go to browse, which drives the Aside browser with your logged-in sessions first and gstack's own browser otherwise. It routes only to skills that are installed. It starts by running a preamble script, reports its outcome as DONE, DONE_WITH_CONCERNS, BLOCKED or NEEDS_CONTEXT, and sets a voice: direct, no em dashes, no stock AI vocabulary.",
          "permalink": "https://github.com/garrytan/gstack/tree/688e3ab9d131bd4f9d7b173d81ed15c299d3387a"
        },
        "tags": [
          "category:skill-tools",
          "type:router",
          "effect:none",
          "needs:cli",
          "by:community",
          "skill-discovery",
          "agent-workflows",
          "code-review",
          "testing"
        ],
        "note": "Routes to gstack's other skills and runs its scripts, so it needs the full gstack install (git clone and ./setup); it also logs telemetry and learnings locally."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "feature-sliced/skills/feature-sliced-design",
      "name": "feature-sliced-design",
      "skills.sh": {
        "url": "https://skills.sh/feature-sliced/skills/feature-sliced-design",
        "installs": 24200,
        "weeklyInstalls": [219, 243, 237, 414, 2567, 1098, 406, 528],
        "firstSeen": "2026-03-09"
      },
      "skillMd": {
        "version": "5e95aaebb84541742686e56352b7569acf4306f1",
        "updated": "2026-11-06",
        "skew": 0
      },
      "agentReview": {
        "description": "Applies Feature-Sliced Design v2.1 to frontend code: where each file belongs, which layers to use, import boundaries and cross-import fixes.",
        "tldr": {
          "glance": [
            {
              "label": "Use it when",
              "text": "you're structuring or reorganizing a frontend project with Feature-Sliced Design, or deciding where a piece of code should go"
            },
            {
              "label": "It does",
              "text": "places code with a five-step decision tree, enforces the import and public-API rules, and resolves cross-imports between slices"
            },
            {
              "label": "Bias",
              "text": "pages first: code moves down to features or entities only when it's reused now, changes for its own reasons and has a focused job"
            },
            {
              "label": "Layers",
              "text": "app, pages, widgets, features, entities and shared, highest to lowest; most projects can start with only app, pages and shared"
            },
            {
              "label": "Also covers",
              "text": "migrating from FSD v2.0 or a non-FSD codebase, Next.js, Nuxt, Astro and other frameworks, auth, API calls, Redux and TanStack Query"
            },
            {
              "label": "Official",
              "text": "the Feature-Sliced Design project's own skill, based on the v2.1 docs at fsd.how"
            }
          ],
          "text": "Feature-Sliced Design is a methodology for organizing frontend code into layers, which divide into slices by business area, which divide into segments by technical purpose: ui, model, api, lib and config. A module may import only from layers below it, and only through a slice's index.ts. The skill leans hard toward simplicity: duplication across pages is acceptable, the widgets layer is discouraged, plain CRUD is infrastructure rather than an entity, auth tokens live in shared, and files are named for their domain (user.ts) rather than their role (utils.ts). For cross-imports between entities it prefers merging their boundaries over the @x notation, a last resort, and for features it offers four strategies, from merging slices to composing them from the page. It recommends Steiger, the official FSD linter, and reads each of its nine reference files only when a task calls for it.",
